  <span class="hljs-attr">db_data:</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>The Dockerfile now uses the <code>php:7.4-apache-alpine</code> image as the base, and the <code>db</code> service in the docker-compose.yml file uses the <code>mysql:5.7.34-alpine</code> image. These Alpine-based images will significantly reduce the container sizes.</p><p>To build and run the containers, navigate to the root directory of the OWASPWebGoatPHP project and run:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>bash</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-bash">docker-compose up -d
</code></div></div></pre><p>You can access WebGoatPHP at <a href="http://localhost:8080" target="_new">http://localhost:8080</a>.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': '2 / 2fix php docker image to be alpine httpd from here https://hub.docker.com/_/httpd'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>In order to use the Alpine-based httpd image from Docker Hub, you will need to install PHP and required PHP extensions separately. Here's the updated Dockerfile using the Alpine httpd image:</p><p>Dockerfile:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>Dockerfile</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-Dockerfile">FROM alpine:3.14

# Install Apache (httpd) and PHP with necessary extensions
RUN apk add --no-cache apache2 php7 php7-apache2 php7-mysqli php7-gd php7-json php7-xml php7-soap

# Enable Apache rewrite module
RUN sed -i 's#^LoadModule rewrite_module.*#LoadModule rewrite_module modules/mod_rewrite.so#' /etc/apache2/httpd.conf

# Configure Apache
COPY .docker/000-default.conf /etc/apache2/conf.d/default.conf
RUN chown -R apache:apache /var/www/localhost/htdocs/

# Copy the project files
COPY . /var/www/localhost/htdocs/
RUN chown -R apache:apache /var/www/localhost/htdocs/

# Expose Apache port
EXPOSE 80

# Start Apache
CMD ["httpd", "-D", "FOREGROUND"]
</code></div></div></pre><p>.docker/000-default.conf:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>bash</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-bash">ServerAdmin webmaster@localhost
DocumentRoot /var/www/localhost/htdocs

&lt;Directory <span class="hljs-string">"/var/www/localhost/htdocs"</span>&gt;
    Options Indexes FollowSymLinks
    AllowOverride All
    Require all granted
&lt;/Directory&gt;

ErrorLog /var/log/apache2/error.log
LogLevel warn
CustomLog /var/log/apache2/access.log combined
</code></div></div></pre><p>The Dockerfile now uses the <code>alpine:3.14</code> image as the base and installs Apache (httpd) and PHP with necessary extensions. The .docker/000-default.conf file is updated to use the appropriate paths for the Alpine-based setup.</p><p>docker-compose.yml remains the same as before:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>yaml</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-yaml"><span class="hljs-attr">version:</span> <span class="hljs-string">'3.8'</span>

