</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we import the <code>cookie</code> library and use its <code>parse()</code> method to parse the <code>document.cookie</code> string into an object. We can then access the value of a specific cookie by name using bracket notation, like <code>cookies['myCookieName']</code>. Finally, we print the value of the cookie to the console.</p><p>Note that you will need to install the <code>cookie</code> library using a package manager like npm or yarn before you can use it in your code. You can do this by running a command like <code>npm install cookie</code> or <code>yarn add cookie</code> in your project directory.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'please help to add codes for verifying null related exceptions.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Certainly! Here's an updated version of the previous example that includes some null-related exception handling:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-comment">// import the cookie library</span>
