        require(inventory[product] &gt;= quantity, <span class="hljs-string">"Not enough inventory"</span>);
        inventory[product] -= quantity;
    }

    <span class="hljs-function">function <span class="hljs-title">getInventory</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> memory product</span>) <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> view <span class="hljs-title">returns</span> (<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">uint</span></span>)</span> {
        <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> inventory[product];
    }
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>This contract implements a simple inventory management system where products can be added and removed from the inventory using the <code>addProduct</code> and <code>removeProduct</code> functions, respectively. The current inventory level for a given product can be queried using the <code>getInventory</code> function.</p><p>This contract uses a mapping data structure to store the inventory levels for each product. The <code>addProduct</code> function takes two parameters, a string representing the product and a uint representing the quantity to add to the inventory. The <code>removeProduct</code> function takes the same parameters but removes the specified quantity from the inventory. Both functions use the <code>require</code> statement to check for certain conditions before modifying the inventory. In the case of <code>removeProduct</code>, it checks that there is enough inventory to remove.</p><p>The <code>getInventory</code> function takes a string representing the product and returns the current inventory level for that product.</p><p>Note that this is a very simple example and a real-world inventory management system would likely be more complex and include additional functionality. Additionally, this contract would need to be deployed on a blockchain platform, such as Ethereum, and integrated with a user interface and other components to form a complete system.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'does the code for the above deals with fraud-related conditions in inventory or can you modify the code for smart contracts that can add more detailing related to inventory frauds( Preventing counterfeiting, Reducing inventory-related frauds, Ensuring authenticity and Improving transparency)'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>The example code I provided is a simple implementation of an inventory management system on the Ethereum blockchain, and it does not include any specific features for preventing fraud or ensuring authenticity.</p><p>However, smart contracts can be designed to include a wide range of features for preventing fraud and improving transparency in inventory management systems. Here are some possible modifications to the code that could add more functionality related to inventory fraud prevention:</p><ol><li><p>Preventing counterfeiting:
