Is there some way to get access to the `__webpack_require__` function for a bundle that was produced by webpack 5?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Yes, you can access the <code>__webpack_require__</code> function for a bundle produced by Webpack 5 by including it as a global variable in the bundle. This can be done by using the <code>output.globalObject</code> configuration option in the Webpack configuration file and setting it to <code>'this'</code> or <code>'window'</code>.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'What about the `__webpack_module_cache__` object?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Yes, the <code>__webpack_module_cache__</code> object can also be accessed in a similar way. It is not a global variable by default, but it can be exposed as a global variable using the <code>ProvidePlugin</code> of webpack.</p><p>You can use the <code>ProvidePlugin</code> in your webpack configuration file to make the <code>__webpack_module_cache__</code> object available as a global variable.</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> webpack = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'webpack'</span>);

<span class="hljs-variable language_">module</span>.<span class="hljs-property">exports</span> = {
  <span class="hljs-comment">// other options</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">plugins</span>: [
    <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> webpack.<span class="hljs-title class_">ProvidePlugin</span>({
      <span class="hljs-attr">__webpack_module_cache__</span>: [<span class="hljs-string">'webpack'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'moduleCache'</span>],
    }),
  ],
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>This will make <code>__webpack_module_cache__</code> available as a global variable and you can access it from anywhere in your application.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "What if I want to access those symbols from code that isn't included in the bundle?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>If you want to access the <code>__webpack_require__</code> or <code>__webpack_module_cache__</code> symbols from code that is not included in the bundle, you would need to create a global variable with the same name and assign the corresponding symbol to it.</p><p>For example, you can add the following code at the top of your entry file or in a separate script file that is included before the bundle:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-variable language_">window</span>.<span class="hljs-property">__webpack_require__</span> = __webpack_require__;
</code></div></div></pre><p>And similarly, you can add the following code at the top of your entry file or in a separate script file that is included before the bundle:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-variable language_">window</span>.<span class="hljs-property">__webpack_module_cache__</span> = __webpack_module_cache__;
</code></div></div></pre><p>This will make these symbols available as global variables that you can access from any code that runs after this code.</p><p>Please keep in mind that, this is not a recommended practice and it's not secure. Also, it's important to note that if you are using webpack 5 and above, webpack will not inject the <code>__webpack_require__</code> and <code>__webpack_module_cache__</code> function, these are internal webpack symbols, not intended for external use.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Why is this insecure?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Accessing the <code>__webpack_require__</code> or <code>__webpack_module_cache__</code> symbols from code that is not included in the bundle and making them global variables can be considered insecure because it allows any code that runs on the page to have access to the internal state of your bundle and potentially access or modify modules in ways that are not intended.</p><p>By using these symbols, malicious code could potentially:</p><ul><li>access private data or functions within your bundle</li><li>overwrite or modify the behavior of existing modules</li><li>inject new modules into your bundle</li><li>interfere with the proper functioning of your application</li></ul><p>It's also important to note that these symbols are intended for internal use by webpack and their behavior and API may change in future versions of webpack, so relying on them in external code may make your application brittle.</p><p>It's always recommended to use the public API that webpack provides for interacting with your bundle and avoid accessing internal webpack symbols directly.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "can't malicious code already modify all scripts on the page either way?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>It's true that if an attacker is able to execute code on your page, they can potentially modify or tamper with any scripts that are loaded on the page. However, by making the <code>__webpack_require__</code> or <code>__webpack_module_cache__</code> symbols global variables, you are providing an additional attack surface that the attacker can use to potentially gain access to the internal state of your bundle and take actions that can compromise the integrity and security of your application.</p><p>Even if an attacker can already execute code on the page, it's still important to minimize the attack surface and not provide additional opportunities for the attacker to compromise the security of your application.</p><p>It's always best to follow security best practices and not make any internal webpack symbol available as a global variable, and use the public API provided by webpack to interact with your bundle.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "I don't see how that increases the attack surface in a meaningful way. If the attacker can modify any scripts on the page, they can modify the bundle to export those symbols as globals on the `window` object."},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>You're correct that if an attacker can modify any scripts on the page, they can potentially add code to make the <code>__webpack_require__</code> or <code>__webpack_module_cache__</code> symbols global variables, regardless of whether your application code makes them available as global variables or not.</p><p>However, by making these symbols global variables in your application code, you are essentially providing a roadmap for the attacker. They can simply look at your code and see that these symbols are available as global variables, and they don't have to spend time trying to reverse-engineer how to access them.</p><p>Additionally, by not making these symbols global variables, you're making it more difficult for the attacker to understand the internal structure of your bundle and what they can potentially access or modify.</p><p>It's always best to follow security best practices and not make any internal webpack symbol available as a global variable, and use the public API provided by webpack to interact with your bundle.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'is this still a security concern if the bundle is a node library and not meant for use in the browser?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>If the bundle is a node library and not meant for use in the browser, then the security concern of making the <code>__webpack_require__</code> or <code>__webpack_module_cache__</code> symbols global variables is lessened, since the library would only be executed in a Node.js environment, and the symbols would only be accessible through the Node.js process.</p><p>However, even in a Node.js environment, it's still best to avoid making these symbols global variables, for the same reasons as mentioned before. These symbols are intended for internal use by webpack and their behavior and API may change in future versions of webpack, so relying on them in external code may make your application brittle.</p><p>Also, it's important to keep in mind that even if the library is intended for use in a Node.js environment, it's still possible for an attacker to gain access to the internal state of the library by exploiting vulnerabilities in the application that uses the library, so it's always best to follow security best practices and use the public API that webpack provides for interacting with your bundle instead of accessing internal webpack symbols directly.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "Okay, that all makes sense, but I need to test my bundled code, and my tests need to mock functions and objects that are included in the bundle, but not exported. For quality assurance reasons it is imperative that I can execute my test suite against the same bundle that ships, so solutions that make use of a separate development bundle don't work for me.

With those constraints in mind, how can I test my bundled code without running afoul of the security considerations that you mentioned above?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Testing a bundled code can be a bit tricky, especially if you need to mock functions and objects that are included in the bundle but not exported.</p><p>One solution to this problem would be to extract the functions and objects that need to be mocked into separate modules, and then re-bundle your code with those modules replaced by the mocked versions. This way, you can test your code against the same bundle that will be shipped, but with the added flexibility of being able to mock specific functions and objects.</p><p>Another solution would be to make use of a tool such as <code>rewire</code> or <code>proxyquire</code> that allows you to modify the behavior of specific modules at runtime, without modifying the original source code or the bundle.</p><p>You can use <code>rewire</code> to replace a module's exports with a mock object and <code>proxyquire</code> to replace the imported modules with mocks.</p><p>Both these tools allow you to mock the required functions and objects, making it possible to test the code without making any internal webpack symbols available as global variables.</p><p>Please keep in mind that even with these tools, you still need to be careful with the security of your code and make sure that the mocks you use do not introduce any vulnerabilities.</p><p>You can also consider using a tool like <code>babel-plugin-rewire</code> which allows you to use rewire with babel, so you don't have to use rewire during runtime, but instead, you can use it to mock the required functions and objects during the build process.</p><p>It's also worth noting that you should be careful when using these tools and make sure that your tests are not dependent on the internal implementation of your modules, but rather the external API they provide.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "All of the things you mentioned have flaws for various reasons.

I can't re-bundle because I need to run my tests against the exact bundle that will be shipped to the package registry.

Neither `rewire` or `proxyquire` offer any tools for accessing modules that are stored within a webpack bundle."},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>You're correct that re-bundling your code with mocked modules may not be a viable option if you need to test the exact bundle that will be shipped, and that tools like <code>rewire</code> and <code>proxyquire</code> are not able to access modules that are stored within a webpack bundle.</p><p>Another solution would be to use webpack's built-in <code>devtool</code> options to generate source maps for your bundle. This way, you can use a tool like <code>source-map-support</code> to remap the stack trace to the original source code when an error occurs. This way, you can debug and test your bundled code as if it were not bundled.</p><p>Additionally, you can use a testing framework such as Jest or Mocha that provides the ability to <code>instrument</code> the code. This allows you to run your tests and collect code coverage information on the original source code, not the bundled code.</p><p>You can also use a tool like <code>webpack-bundle-analyzer</code> to analyze your bundle and understand its structure. This tool can help you understand the structure of the bundle and the modules that are included in it, which can help you identify the specific modules that need to be mocked for testing.</p><p>It's also worth noting that you can use a tool like <code>webpack-bin-runner</code> to run the webpack commands in a way that allows you to test the exact bundle that will be shipped.</p><p>Please keep in mind that these solutions may have their own limitations and drawbacks. For example, remapping the stack trace to the original source code can be complex and time-consuming, and instrumenting the code can slow down the test suite.</p><p>It's important to weigh the trade-offs and choose the approach that best fits your use case and testing requirements.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "None of those tips address the problem that I'm talking about."},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>I apologize for misunderstanding your question earlier. It sounds like you need a way to access the modules and functions that are included in your bundle, but not exposed through the public API, in order to test them.</p><p>One solution you can use is to use webpack's <code>externals</code> configuration option to exclude specific modules from the bundle and make them available in the global scope. This way, you can access them directly in your tests.</p><p>You can also use a utility like <code>unfederate</code> which allows you to extract the modules that are bundled with webpack and make them available to your tests.</p><p>You can also use <code>webpack-sources</code> to inspect and manipulate the contents of the bundle, and extract the code of modules that you want to test.</p><p>It's important to keep in mind that these solutions may have their own limitations and drawbacks, and make sure that your tests are not dependent on the internal implementation of your modules, but rather the external API they provide.</p><p>Please keep in mind that these are not recommended practices and that it's best to use the public API that webpack provides for interacting with your bundle instead of accessing internal webpack symbols directly.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': "Where do I store sensitive data like API keys for a React Native project that's built with Expo EAS"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>When it comes to storing sensitive data such as API keys for a React Native project built with Expo EAS, there are a few options to consider:</p><ol><li><p>Environment Variables: You can store sensitive data such as API keys as environment variables. You can define environment variables in the <code>.env</code> file, and use a package like <code>react-native-dotenv</code> to access them in your code.</p></li><li><p>Expo Constants: Expo provides a <code>Constants</code> API that allows you to access device and application-specific information. You can store sensitive data as <code>app.json</code> values under <code>expo.extra</code> field and access them through <code>Constants.manifest.extra</code> in your code.</p></li><li><p>Key Management Services: Another option is to use a key management service like AWS Secrets Manager, Google Cloud KMS or Azure Key Vault to store your sensitive data. You can then retrieve the data at runtime using a client library provided by the key management service.</p></li></ol><p>Regardless of the option you choose, it's important to ensure that you are not committing any sensitive data to version control, and that you are following security best practices when storing and handling sensitive data in your application.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'The latest react native documentation says the following...
"Tools like react-native-dotenv and react-native-config are great for adding environment-specific variables like API endpoints, but they should not be confused with server-side environment variables, which can often contain secrets and API keys."'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Yes, that is correct. While tools like <code>react-native-dotenv</code> and <code>react-native-config</code> can be useful for managing environment-specific variables like API endpoints, it's important to keep in mind that they are not meant to handle secrets or sensitive data.</p><p>Server-side environment variables are typically used to store sensitive information like API keys, database credentials, and other secrets. These environment variables should be managed separately from your client-side code and should be accessed securely using server-side code.</p><p>For example, in a client-server architecture, your React Native application would be the client-side code, while the server-side code could be built using a server-side framework like Node.js or Ruby on Rails. The server-side code would then handle requests and access sensitive data using environment variables stored securely on the server.</p><p>In summary, while tools like <code>react-native-dotenv</code> and <code>react-native-config</code> are useful for managing environment-specific variables, they should not be used to handle secrets or sensitive data. Sensitive data should be stored securely on the server and accessed securely using server-side code.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'How would I do this for something like a firebase authentication api key? As I understand it, that would need to reside in the client side code?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Yes, you are correct that in the case of Firebase Authentication, the API key is needed on the client-side code in order to interact with the Firebase Authentication service.</p><p>In this case, you can use a technique called "obfuscation" to help protect the API key. Obfuscation is the process of making code difficult to understand or reverse engineer, without necessarily making it more secure. While it won't completely secure the API key, it can help to make it more difficult for attackers to extract the key from your code.</p><p>One way to obfuscate the API key is to store it as an obfuscated string in your code. For example, you could store the API key as a string of random characters, and then use a function to "decrypt" the string at runtime. Here's an example of how this could be done:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> obfuscatedApiKey = <span class="hljs-string">"d1XJ$#3uq3J[2ej28u1j"</span>

<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">decryptApiKey</span>(<span class="hljs-params">obfuscatedKey</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-comment">// Insert decryption algorithm here</span>
  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-string">"my-real-api-key"</span>;
}

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> apiKey = <span class="hljs-title function_">decryptApiKey</span>(obfuscatedApiKey);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Use apiKey to interact with Firebase Authentication</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, the actual API key is stored as an obfuscated string (<code>obfuscatedApiKey</code>), and a function (<code>decryptApiKey</code>) is used to decrypt the string at runtime and retrieve the actual API key. The decryption algorithm would need to be included in your client-side code, so it's not a foolproof way to secure the API key, but it can help to make it more difficult for attackers to extract the key from your code.</p><p>It's important to note that obfuscation is not a replacement for proper security practices, and it's still important to follow best practices for securing sensitive data, such as limiting access to the API key, monitoring usage, and rotating the key regularly.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "So, there's no way to secure an API key in React Native because somebody could always inspect the application bundle?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Unfortunately, there is no foolproof way to completely secure an API key in a React Native application, or any client-side application for that matter. As you mentioned, someone with access to the application bundle could potentially inspect the code and extract the API key.</p><p>However, there are steps you can take to make it more difficult for attackers to extract the API key, and to reduce the risk of the key being misused if it is compromised. Here are some best practices to follow:</p><ol><li><p>Limit access to the API key: Only give access to the API key to those who need it, and limit the scope of their access as much as possible. For example, if the API key is only needed for read-only access to a particular resource, consider creating a separate key with read-only access.</p></li><li><p>Use secure storage: Store the API key in secure storage on the client-side, such as the iOS Keychain or Android Keystore. This can help to make it more difficult for attackers to extract the key from the application bundle.</p></li><li><p>Obfuscate the API key: Obfuscation can make it more difficult for attackers to extract the API key from the application bundle. For example, you could store the API key as an obfuscated string and use a function to decrypt the string at runtime.</p></li><li><p>Monitor usage: Monitor usage of the API key to detect any unusual or unauthorized activity.</p></li><li><p>Rotate the API key regularly: Consider rotating the API key regularly, such as every 90 days, to reduce the risk of the key being compromised.</p></li></ol><p>While none of these measures can completely eliminate the risk of API key theft or misuse, following these best practices can help to make it more difficult for attackers to extract the key and to reduce the potential impact of a key compromise.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'how to validate a session token with php'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>In order to validate a session token with PHP, you can use the built-in session functions provided by PHP. First, you will need to start a session using the session_start() function. Once a session is started, you can use the $_SESSION superglobal array to store and access session variables.</p><p>To validate a session token, you can check if a specific session variable, such as "token", exists in the $_SESSION array. If the variable exists, you can then compare its value to the token that was sent by the client. If the values match, the token is considered to be valid. If the values do not match or the session variable does not exist, the token is considered to be invalid.</p><p>An example of validating a session token in PHP:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-php"><span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">session_start</span>();
<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">isset</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$_SESSION</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'token'</span>]) &amp;&amp; <span class="hljs-variable">$_SESSION</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'token'</span>] === <span class="hljs-variable">$client_token</span>){
    <span class="hljs-comment">// token is valid</span>
}<span class="hljs-keyword">else</span>{
    <span class="hljs-comment">// token is invalid</span>
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>It's important to note that this is a basic example and in practice you should consider additional security measures such as regenerating the session id after successful login, checking the token's expiration date, and storing the token securely (e.g. in a database or an HttpOnly cookie)</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'write a CRUD api using javascript'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="request-:R2d6:-0 markdown prose dark:prose-invert break-words light"><p>To write a CRUD (create, read, update, delete) API using JavaScript, you will need to use a web framework that supports routing, such as Express.</p><p>Here is an example of a simple CRUD API using Express:</p><pre><div class="bg-black"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="w-4 h-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> express = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'express'</span>);
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> app = <span class="hljs-title function_">express</span>();

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a new item</span>
app.<span class="hljs-title function_">post</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'/items'</span>, <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">req, res</span>) =&gt;</span> {
  <span class="hljs-comment">// Get the item data from the request body</span>
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> item = req.<span class="hljs-property">body</span>;

  <span class="hljs-comment">// Save the item to the database</span>
  <span class="hljs-title function_">saveItem</span>(item)
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> {
      <span class="hljs-comment">// Return a success response</span>
      res.<span class="hljs-title function_">send</span>({
        <span class="hljs-attr">success</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>,
      });
    })
    .<span class="hljs-title function_">catch</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">err</span>) =&gt;</span> {
