[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Hey, please explain certificate pinning process step by step on higher perspective.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Sure! Here is a step-by-step explanation of the certificate pinning process at a high level:</p><ol><li><p>The client (e.g. a web browser or mobile app) initiates a connection to the server (e.g. a website or API).</p></li><li><p>The server responds with its SSL/TLS certificate, which includes a public key and other identifying information.</p></li><li><p>The client checks the certificate against a set of trusted root authorities (e.g. a list of trusted Certificate Authorities stored on the client).</p></li><li><p>If the certificate is trusted, the client proceeds with the SSL/TLS handshake and establishes a secure connection with the server.</p></li><li><p>During this process, the client checks the server's certificate against a pre-defined set of pins, which are stored locally on the client.</p></li><li><p>These pins can be either:</p><p>a) Public key pins: a hash of the server's public key (e.g. SHA-256 hash)</p><p>b) Certificate pins: a hash of the entire certificate (e.g. SHA-256 hash)</p></li><li><p>If the server's certificate matches one of the pre-defined pins, the connection is established. If not, the client rejects the connection and terminates the SSL/TLS handshake.</p></li><li><p>If the client encounters a pin validation failure, it may prompt the user with a warning message or take other action to alert the user of the potential security risk.</p></li></ol><p>In summary, certificate pinning is a mechanism that allows clients to ensure that they are communicating with the intended server and not an imposter. By comparing a server's SSL/TLS certificate to a set of pre-defined pins, clients can mitigate the risk of man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks and other types of certificate-based attacks.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Does it mean every api call using axios for example will be secure ?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Certificate pinning alone does not guarantee that every API call using a library like Axios will be secure, as there are other potential vulnerabilities in the application and network that can be exploited. However, certificate pinning can significantly reduce the risk of man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks, which are a common attack vector in many security breaches.</p><p>When implementing certificate pinning with Axios or any other HTTP client library, it is important to follow best practices and ensure that the client is configured to properly check and validate the server's certificate. Additionally, other security measures should be implemented, such as input validation and output encoding, to further reduce the risk of security vulnerabilities.</p><p>In summary, while certificate pinning is an important security measure, it should be used in conjunction with other security best practices to ensure that API calls using Axios or any other library are as secure as possible.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Okay, how to configure axios to use certificate pinning?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>To configure Axios to use certificate pinning, you can use the <code>https</code> module in Node.js to create a custom HTTPS agent that validates the server's certificate against a set of pre-defined pins. Here is an example of how to do this:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> https = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'https'</span>);
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> axios = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'axios'</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Define the list of pins as an array of SHA-256 hashes</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> pins = [<span class="hljs-string">'sha256/xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'sha256/yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy'</span>];

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a custom HTTPS agent with pin validation</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> agent = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> https.<span class="hljs-title class_">Agent</span>({
  <span class="hljs-attr">pinning</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">checkServerIdentity</span>: <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">host, cert</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> certPins = cert.<span class="hljs-title function_">getPublicKey</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">getFingerprints</span>();
    <span class="hljs-keyword">for</span> (<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> pin <span class="hljs-keyword">of</span> pins) {
      <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (certPins.<span class="hljs-title function_">has</span>(pin)) {
        <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-literal">undefined</span>; <span class="hljs-comment">// Certificate is valid</span>
      }
    }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Error</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Certificate pin validation failed'</span>);
  }
});

<span class="hljs-comment">// Use the custom HTTPS agent with Axios</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> instance = axios.<span class="hljs-title function_">create</span>({
  <span class="hljs-attr">httpsAgent</span>: agent
});

<span class="hljs-comment">// Make a secure API call with Axios</span>
instance.<span class="hljs-title function_">get</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'https://example.com/api/data'</span>)
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">response</span> =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(response.<span class="hljs-property">data</span>);
  })
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">catch</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">error</span> =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">error</span>(error);
  });
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we first define an array of SHA-256 hashes representing the list of pins that we want to validate the server's certificate against. We then create a custom HTTPS agent using the <code>https.Agent</code> constructor, and set the <code>pinning</code> option to <code>true</code> to enable pin validation.</p><p>Next, we define a custom <code>checkServerIdentity</code> function that is called when the server's certificate is received. This function extracts the SHA-256 fingerprints of the certificate's public key and compares them against the pre-defined list of pins. If a match is found, the function returns <code>undefined</code>, indicating that the certificate is valid. If no match is found, the function returns an <code>Error</code> object with a message indicating that the pin validation failed.</p><p>Finally, we create an instance of Axios using the <code>axios.create()</code> method, and set the <code>httpsAgent</code> option to our custom HTTPS agent. We can then make a secure API call using the <code>instance.get()</code> method, which will use our custom agent to validate the server's certificate against our pre-defined pins.</p><p>Note that this is just one example of how to configure Axios to use certificate pinning, and there are other ways to implement this feature depending on your specific use case and environment.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'can you please explain this line in detail:

const certPins = cert.getPublicKey().getFingerprints();'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Sure! In this line, we are using the <code>getPublicKey()</code> method of the server's SSL/TLS certificate to retrieve its public key, and then calling the <code>getFingerprints()</code> method to generate a set of fingerprints for the public key.</p><p>A fingerprint is a short string of characters that uniquely identifies a cryptographic key. In this case, we are using the SHA-256 hashing algorithm to generate the fingerprints, which produces a 256-bit hash value that is then represented as a hexadecimal string. The resulting fingerprints are then stored in a <code>Set</code> object called <code>certPins</code>.</p><p>By comparing the fingerprints of the server's public key to a pre-defined set of pins using <code>certPins.has(pin)</code>, we can validate that the server's SSL/TLS certificate matches one of the pre-defined pins and ensure that we are communicating with the intended server. If the comparison fails, we know that the server's SSL/TLS certificate does not match any of the pre-defined pins and we should reject the connection.</p><p>Overall, by using the <code>getPublicKey()</code> method to retrieve the server's public key and generating a set of fingerprints using the <code>getFingerprints()</code> method, we can perform certificate pinning with Axios and ensure that our API calls are as secure as possible.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'storing these public keys inside the set for a webiste is secure?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>When implementing certificate pinning, it is generally not recommended to store the public keys or certificate fingerprints directly in the source code or client-side storage, as this could potentially expose the pins to attackers and compromise the security of the connection.</p><p>Instead, it is recommended to use a secure key management system, such as a Hardware Security Module (HSM) or a cloud-based Key Management Service (KMS), to securely store and manage the pins. These systems provide strong access controls and encryption to protect the pins from unauthorized access and ensure their confidentiality.</p><p>Additionally, it is important to periodically rotate the pins and update them as necessary to ensure that the certificate pinning mechanism remains effective against new threats and vulnerabilities.</p><p>Overall, while it is important to use certificate pinning to protect against man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks, it is equally important to follow best practices for secure key management to ensure that the pins themselves are not compromised.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Ok please create a table of best practices to look for while implementing certificate pinning.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Sure! Here is a table of best practices to follow when implementing certificate pinning:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Best Practice</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Use a secure key management system</td><td>Use a Hardware Security Module (HSM) or a cloud-based Key Management Service (KMS) to securely store and manage the pins.</td></tr><tr><td>Rotate pins regularly</td><td>Periodically rotate the pins and update them as necessary to ensure that the certificate pinning mechanism remains effective against new threats and vulnerabilities.</td></tr><tr><td>Use a secure connection to retrieve pins</td><td>Use a secure connection (e.g. HTTPS) to retrieve the pins from the key management system to prevent interception or tampering by attackers.</td></tr><tr><td>Validate pins against multiple trusted sources</td><td>Validate the server's SSL/TLS certificate against multiple trusted sources, such as a list of trusted Certificate Authorities, in addition to the pre-defined pins.</td></tr><tr><td>Implement a fallback mechanism</td><td>Implement a fallback mechanism that allows the client to gracefully handle pin validation failures, such as prompting the user with a warning message or taking other appropriate action.</td></tr><tr><td>Use a secure protocol for communication</td><td>Use a secure communication protocol (e.g. HTTPS) for all API calls to ensure that the data is encrypted and secure in transit.</td></tr><tr><td>Use a reputable certificate authority</td><td>Use a reputable certificate authority to obtain and manage SSL/TLS certificates, and ensure that the certificates are properly configured and up-to-date.</td></tr><tr><td>Implement other security measures</td><td>Implement other security measures, such as input validation, output encoding, and access controls, to further reduce the risk of security vulnerabilities.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These best practices can help ensure that certificate pinning is implemented effectively and securely to protect against man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks and other security threats.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'can you write a story in style of Ekhart Tolle about certificate pinning and its benefits?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Certainly! Here is a story in the style of Eckhart Tolle about certificate pinning and its benefits:</p><p>Once upon a time, there was a young programmer who was seeking to create a more secure web application. She had heard of a powerful tool called certificate pinning, but she wasn't sure how it worked or how it could help her.</p><p>So, she went on a journey to discover the truth about certificate pinning. As she traveled deeper into the realm of web security, she encountered many challenges and obstacles, but she persisted on her path.</p><p>Finally, she reached a place of deep understanding and insight. She saw that certificate pinning was like a powerful shield that protected her web application from the attacks of hackers and cybercriminals.</p><p>With certificate pinning, she could ensure that her web application communicated only with the servers that she trusted, and that her users' data was kept safe and secure. She could prevent man-in-the-middle attacks and other security breaches, and she could rest easy knowing that her application was protected by the power of certificate pinning.</p><p>As she looked out upon the vast expanse of the web, she realized that the power of certificate pinning was not just for her, but for all programmers and web developers who sought to create a more secure and trustworthy online world.</p><p>And so, she set out to share the wisdom of certificate pinning with all who would listen, knowing that the power of security and trust was within reach for all who sought it.</p><p>In this way, the young programmer discovered the transformative power of certificate pinning, and she knew that her journey had led her to a place of deep understanding and wisdom.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Great. Can you guide me how what are some requirements for development environment to develop certificate pinning?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Sure! Here are some requirements for a development environment to develop certificate pinning:</p><ol><li><p>A development machine running a supported operating system (e.g. Windows, Linux, macOS) with the necessary software tools installed, such as a code editor, version control system, and package manager.</p></li><li><p>A secure key management system, such as a Hardware Security Module (HSM) or a cloud-based Key Management Service (KMS), to securely store and manage the pins.</p></li><li><p>A secure web server or API endpoint to test the certificate pinning implementation, such as a development or staging environment with a valid SSL/TLS certificate.</p></li><li><p>A testing framework or suite to test the certificate pinning implementation, such as a unit testing framework like Jest or Mocha.</p></li><li><p>A set of pre-defined pins for testing and validation, generated using a secure hash algorithm such as SHA-256.</p></li><li><p>A clear understanding of the security risks and best practices associated with certificate pinning, as well as the ability to configure and validate the pins using the appropriate tools and libraries.</p></li></ol><p>Overall, developing a certificate pinning implementation requires a secure and well-equipped development environment, as well as a solid understanding of the underlying security principles and best practices. By following these requirements, developers can create secure and trustworthy web applications that protect users' data and privacy.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Ok. I am feeling like I am getting good understanding of certificate pinning. Thank you for helping me out!'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>You're welcome! I'm glad that I could help you understand certificate pinning better. If you have any more questions, feel free to ask!</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "I am still confuse about the pins. Is pins are public keys? or digital certificates? Where do I need to store them in React Native app for example. Give me an answer as if I'm Jr. software engineer."},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Great question! The pins used in certificate pinning are actually hashes of either the server's public key or the entire server SSL/TLS certificate, depending on which type of pinning you are using.</p><p>When using public key pinning, the pin is a hash of the server's public key. When using certificate pinning, the pin is a hash of the entire SSL/TLS certificate.</p><p>As a junior software engineer working with a React Native app, it is important to securely store the pins to prevent them from being compromised by attackers. One common approach is to store the pins as a constant variable in a dedicated file in your project, such as a <code>constants.js</code> file.</p><p>For example, you could store your pins as an array of SHA-256 hashes in your <code>constants.js</code> file like this:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-variable constant_">PINS</span> = [
  <span class="hljs-string">'sha256/xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx'</span>,
  <span class="hljs-string">'sha256/yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy'</span>,
  <span class="hljs-string">'sha256/zzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzzz'</span>
];
</code></div></div></pre><p>You can then import the pins from the <code>constants.js</code> file into your React Native app wherever you need to use them for certificate pinning.</p><p>It is important to ensure that the pins are stored securely and cannot be accessed or modified by unauthorized users. For example, you could use encryption or other security measures to protect the pins if they are stored on the client-side.</p><p>Overall, by securely storing the pins in a dedicated file and following other security best practices, you can help ensure that your React Native app is secure and protected against man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks and other security threats.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Ok great. What happens if the pins are end up in wrong hands?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>If the pins used for certificate pinning end up in the wrong hands, it could potentially compromise the security of your application and leave it vulnerable to man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks.</p><p>An attacker who has access to the pins could potentially use them to create a fake server that mimics the legitimate server, and intercept and modify the data being transmitted between the client and the server. This could allow the attacker to steal sensitive data, such as usernames and passwords, credit card numbers, or other private information.</p><p>To prevent this from happening, it is important to follow best practices for secure key management and ensure that the pins are stored in a secure location and are not accessible to unauthorized users. For example, you could use a Hardware Security Module (HSM) or a cloud-based Key Management Service (KMS) to store and manage the pins, and restrict access to the pins using strong access controls and encryption.</p><p>If you suspect that the pins may have been compromised, it is important to rotate the pins and update them as soon as possible to prevent any potential attacks. You should also consider implementing additional security measures, such as multi-factor authentication, to further protect your application and prevent unauthorized access.</p><p>Overall, the security of your application depends on the proper management and protection of the pins used for certificate pinning. By following best practices for key management and taking appropriate security measures, you can help ensure that your application remains secure and protected against potential security threats.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Hardware Security Module (HSM) what is it?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>A Hardware Security Module (HSM) is a physical device that is designed to securely store and manage cryptographic keys, passwords, and other sensitive data. HSMs are typically used in high-security environments, such as financial institutions, government agencies, and large enterprises, where data privacy and security are critical.</p><p>HSMs provide a number of security features that make them ideal for key management and protection. For example, HSMs typically have tamper-resistant hardware that prevents unauthorized access and ensures the integrity of the stored data. They also have built-in encryption and decryption capabilities, and can perform complex cryptographic operations such as key generation and signing.</p><p>In addition to their security features, HSMs are also designed to be highly available and scalable, and can be used in a variety of configurations to meet the needs of different applications and environments. For example, HSMs can be used as standalone devices, as part of a distributed network of devices, or as a cloud-based service.</p><p>Overall, HSMs provide a secure and reliable way to store and manage cryptographic keys and other sensitive data, and are an important tool for ensuring the security and integrity of applications and systems that handle sensitive data.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "I just can't understand how a web app can use certificate pinning. In the case of web app, if I store pins in cloud, do I have to make API calls to bring the pins to check? or what?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>In the case of a web application, certificate pinning is typically implemented on the client-side, using a custom HTTPS agent or library such as <code>axios</code> or <code>fetch</code>. The pins themselves are usually pre-defined and hard-coded into the client-side code, and are not dynamically retrieved from a server or cloud-based service.</p><p>When a user of the web application makes an API call to a server using HTTPS, the client-side code will use the pre-defined pins to validate the server's SSL/TLS certificate and ensure that the connection is secure. If the server's certificate does not match one of the pre-defined pins, the client-side code will reject the connection and raise an error.</p><p>For example, if you were using the <code>axios</code> library for API calls in your web application, you could implement certificate pinning by creating a custom HTTPS agent that validates the server's certificate against a set of pre-defined pins. You would then use this custom agent in your <code>axios</code> instance to make secure API calls.</p><p>Here is an example of how to do this using <code>axios</code>:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> https = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'https'</span>);
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> axios = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'axios'</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Define the list of pins as an array of SHA-256 hashes</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> pins = [<span class="hljs-string">'sha256/xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'sha256/yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy'</span>];

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a custom HTTPS agent with pin validation</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> agent = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> https.<span class="hljs-title class_">Agent</span>({
  <span class="hljs-attr">pinning</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">checkServerIdentity</span>: <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">host, cert</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> certPins = cert.<span class="hljs-title function_">getPublicKey</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">getFingerprints</span>();
    <span class="hljs-keyword">for</span> (<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> pin <span class="hljs-keyword">of</span> pins) {
      <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (certPins.<span class="hljs-title function_">has</span>(pin)) {
        <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-literal">undefined</span>; <span class="hljs-comment">// Certificate is valid</span>
      }
    }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Error</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Certificate pin validation failed'</span>);
  }
});

<span class="hljs-comment">// Use the custom HTTPS agent with Axios</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> instance = axios.<span class="hljs-title function_">create</span>({
  <span class="hljs-attr">httpsAgent</span>: agent
});

<span class="hljs-comment">// Make a secure API call with Axios</span>
instance.<span class="hljs-title function_">get</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'https://example.com/api/data'</span>)
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">response</span> =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(response.<span class="hljs-property">data</span>);
  })
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">catch</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">error</span> =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">error</span>(error);
  });
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, the pins are hard-coded into the client-side code and are not dynamically retrieved from a server or cloud-based service. The custom HTTPS agent created using the <code>https.Agent</code> constructor performs pin validation against the pre-defined pins, and is then used with the <code>axios.create()</code> method to make secure API calls.</p><p>Overall, certificate pinning can be implemented in a web application using a custom HTTPS agent or library, and the pins themselves are typically hard-coded into the client-side code rather than being dynamically retrieved from a server or cloud-based service.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'storing pins in the code is secure?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Storing pins in the code itself is not the most secure way to store them, as it can potentially expose the pins to attackers who may gain access to the code. Therefore, it is important to take appropriate security measures to protect the pins and ensure that they are not accessible to unauthorized users.</p><p>One approach to improving the security of certificate pinning in web applications is to use a secure key management system, such as a Hardware Security Module (HSM) or a cloud-based Key Management Service (KMS), to securely store and manage the pins. This approach provides strong access controls and encryption to protect the pins from unauthorized access and ensure their confidentiality.</p><p>Another approach is to encrypt the pins using a secure encryption algorithm and store the encrypted pins in a secure location, such as a server-side database or file system. The client-side code can then retrieve and decrypt the pins as needed for certificate pinning.</p><p>Overall, while storing pins in the code itself is not the most secure approach, there are steps that can be taken to improve the security of certificate pinning and protect the pins from unauthorized access. It is important to carefully evaluate the security risks and choose an appropriate approach based on the needs of the application and the level of security required.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'how cloud-based Key Management Service works in web app context?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>In a web application context, a cloud-based Key Management Service (KMS) can provide a secure and scalable way to store and manage the pins used for certificate pinning. Here's how it works:</p><ol><li><p>A web application developer creates an account with a cloud-based KMS provider, such as Amazon Web Services (AWS) Key Management Service or Google Cloud KMS.</p></li><li><p>The developer generates a set of pre-defined pins for certificate pinning, typically using a hash algorithm such as SHA-256.</p></li><li><p>The developer uploads the pins to the KMS provider, which securely stores and manages the pins in a highly available and scalable environment.</p></li><li><p>When the web application makes an API call using HTTPS, the client-side code retrieves the pins from the KMS provider using a secure connection, such as HTTPS or Transport Layer Security (TLS).</p></li><li><p>The client-side code then uses the pins to validate the server's SSL/TLS certificate and ensure that the connection is secure. If the server's certificate does not match one of the pre-defined pins, the client-side code will reject the connection and raise an error.</p></li></ol><p>By using a cloud-based KMS to store and manage the pins, web application developers can benefit from the security and scalability of the cloud while also ensuring the confidentiality and integrity of the pins. The KMS provider typically provides strong access controls and encryption to protect the pins from unauthorized access and ensure their confidentiality.</p><p>Overall, a cloud-based KMS can provide a secure and efficient way to store and manage the pins used for certificate pinning in a web application, helping to protect against man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks and other security threats.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'can you write a sample code to implement that using aws service?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Certainly! Here's an example of how to use the AWS Key Management Service (KMS) to securely store and manage the pins used for certificate pinning in a web application:</p><ol><li><p>Create a new KMS key in the AWS Management Console, and give it an appropriate name and description.</p></li><li><p>In your web application code, install the AWS SDK for JavaScript using a package manager like npm:</p></li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs">npm install aws-sdk
</code></div></div></pre><ol start="3"><li>Configure the AWS SDK with your AWS credentials and region, using a method like this:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-variable constant_">AWS</span> = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'aws-sdk'</span>);
<span class="hljs-variable constant_">AWS</span>.<span class="hljs-property">config</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">update</span>({
  <span class="hljs-attr">region</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'us-east-1'</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">credentials</span>: <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-variable constant_">AWS</span>.<span class="hljs-title class_">Credentials</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'accessKeyId'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'secretAccessKey'</span>)
});
</code></div></div></pre><ol start="4"><li>In your web application code, generate a set of pre-defined pins using a hash algorithm like SHA-256:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> crypto = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'crypto'</span>);

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> pins = [
  crypto.<span class="hljs-title function_">createHash</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'sha256'</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">update</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'public_key_1'</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">digest</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'base64'</span>),
  crypto.<span class="hljs-title function_">createHash</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'sha256'</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">update</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'public_key_2'</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">digest</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'base64'</span>)
];
</code></div></div></pre><ol start="5"><li>Encrypt the pins using the KMS key you created earlier, using a method like this:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> kms = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-variable constant_">AWS</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">KMS</span>();

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> encryptedPins = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Promise</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">all</span>(pins.<span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> (pin) =&gt; {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> params = {
    <span class="hljs-title class_">KeyId</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'your-kms-key-id'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-title class_">Plaintext</span>: pin
  };
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> { <span class="hljs-title class_">CiphertextBlob</span> } = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> kms.<span class="hljs-title function_">encrypt</span>(params).<span class="hljs-title function_">promise</span>();
  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">CiphertextBlob</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toString</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'base64'</span>);
}));
</code></div></div></pre><ol start="6"><li><p>Store the encrypted pins in a secure location, such as a server-side database or file system.</p></li><li><p>In your web application code, retrieve the encrypted pins from the secure location and decrypt them using the KMS key, using a method like this:</p></li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> decryptedPins = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Promise</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">all</span>(encryptedPins.<span class="hljs-title function_">map</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> (encryptedPin) =&gt; {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> params = {
    <span class="hljs-title class_">CiphertextBlob</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">Buffer</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">from</span>(encryptedPin, <span class="hljs-string">'base64'</span>)
  };
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> { <span class="hljs-title class_">Plaintext</span> } = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> kms.<span class="hljs-title function_">decrypt</span>(params).<span class="hljs-title function_">promise</span>();
  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Plaintext</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toString</span>();
}));
</code></div></div></pre><ol start="8"><li>Use the decrypted pins to validate the server's SSL/TLS certificate and ensure that the connection is secure, as described earlier in this conversation.</li></ol><p>By using the AWS Key Management Service to securely store and manage the pins, you can help ensure the confidentiality and integrity of your web application's data and protect against man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks and other security threats.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'does aws kms only used for certificate pinning or this is one of their use cases?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>AWS Key Management Service (KMS) is a versatile service that provides a secure and scalable way to manage cryptographic keys and protect sensitive data in a variety of use cases. While certificate pinning is one possible use case for KMS, it is not the only one.</p><p>Here are some other common use cases for AWS KMS:</p><ol><li><p>Data encryption: KMS can be used to generate, store, and manage cryptographic keys used to encrypt and decrypt data in a variety of applications and environments, such as databases, file systems, and messaging systems.</p></li><li><p>Key management: KMS can be used to manage cryptographic keys and enforce access controls and usage policies for these keys, ensuring that they are used only by authorized users and applications.</p></li><li><p>Digital signatures: KMS can be used to generate and manage cryptographic keys used for digital signatures and to verify the integrity of digital documents and transactions.</p></li><li><p>Compliance and auditing: KMS can be used to enforce compliance with regulatory requirements and industry standards for data security and privacy, such as the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) and the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA).</p></li></ol><p>Overall, AWS KMS provides a flexible and powerful way to manage cryptographic keys and protect sensitive data in a variety of applications and environments, making it a valuable tool for developers and organizations seeking to improve their data security and privacy.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'what are cryptographic keys? Can you explain in why are these required at all? In the form of a story of two young friends.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Certainly! Let me tell you a story of two young friends, Alex and Ben, to explain what cryptographic keys are and why they are required.</p><p>Alex and Ben were two best friends who loved sending secret messages to each other. They would often use simple ciphers, such as the Caesar cipher or the Vigenere cipher, to encode their messages and keep them secret from prying eyes.</p><p>One day, Alex and Ben heard about a new encryption algorithm that used a secret key to encode and decode messages. This algorithm, they learned, was called the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES), and it was used by governments, banks, and other organizations to protect sensitive information.</p><p>Excited by the prospect of using AES to send secret messages to each other, Alex and Ben decided to give it a try. They downloaded a simple AES implementation and started playing with it.</p><p>They soon realized, however, that using AES was more complicated than they thought. The algorithm required a secret key, which was used to both encrypt and decrypt messages. Without the key, it was impossible to decode the encrypted messages.</p><p>Alex and Ben realized that they needed a way to securely share the key with each other, without letting anyone else know what the key was. They decided to use a simple method called key exchange, where they would meet in person and exchange the key in a secret location.</p><p>But Alex and Ben soon realized that key exchange was not practical for everyday use. They needed a way to securely share the key over the internet, without anyone else intercepting it.</p><p>That's where cryptographic keys come in. A cryptographic key is a piece of data that is used to encrypt and decrypt messages using an encryption algorithm, such as AES. Cryptographic keys can be shared securely over the internet using secure key exchange protocols, such as the Diffie-Hellman key exchange protocol, which allows two parties to securely generate a shared secret key without ever actually sharing the key over the internet.</p><p>With the help of cryptographic keys, Alex and Ben were able to securely send secret messages to each other using AES encryption. They no longer had to worry about anyone else intercepting their messages, because the messages could only be decoded using the secret key, which only they knew.</p><p>In summary, cryptographic keys are essential for secure communication and data protection. They are used to encrypt and decrypt messages using encryption algorithms, and can be securely shared over the internet using key exchange protocols. By using cryptographic keys, Alex and Ben were able to protect their messages from prying eyes and ensure their confidentiality and integrity.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Wonderful!'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>I'm glad you enjoyed the story! If you have any more questions, feel free to ask.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "Is Android release key are somewhat similar? If yes, please explain how it works in context of the subject we're currently talking about."},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Yes, the Android release key is somewhat similar to cryptographic keys in the context of the subject we're currently talking about.</p><p>In Android app development, the release key is a private key that is used to sign the app's APK file before it is uploaded to the Google Play Store or distributed to users. The release key ensures that the APK file is not tampered with during distribution and that users can verify the app's authenticity and integrity.</p><p>The release key is typically generated and managed by the app developer or organization, and it is kept secret and protected from unauthorized access. The release key is used to sign the app's APK file using a digital signature, which includes information about the app and the release key.</p><p>When a user installs the app from the Google Play Store or another distribution channel, the Android operating system verifies the digital signature using the release key. If the signature is valid, the app is installed and the user can be confident that the app has not been tampered with and is authentic.</p><p>In this sense, the Android release key is similar to a cryptographic key, in that it is used to verify the authenticity and integrity of data (in this case, the app's APK file). The release key is kept secret and protected from unauthorized access, and it is used to sign the app's APK file using a digital signature, which ensures that the app is authentic and has not been tampered with.</p><p>Overall, the Android release key plays an important role in ensuring the security and integrity of Android apps, much like cryptographic keys do in other contexts.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Does certificate pinning is required for buying and selling app I have on apple store and google play?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Certificate pinning is not strictly required for a buying and selling app that is published on the Apple App Store or Google Play, but it is strongly recommended as a best practice for securing network connections and protecting against man-in-the-middle (MITM) attacks.</p><p>When a user of your app makes a purchase or enters sensitive information, such as their credit card details or personal information, the data is typically transmitted over the internet using a secure connection, such as HTTPS. Certificate pinning can be used to verify that the server's SSL/TLS certificate is valid and that the connection is secure, which can help prevent attackers from intercepting and tampering with the data in transit.</p><p>While the Apple App Store and Google Play do not require certificate pinning for app submissions, they do require that apps meet certain security requirements and guidelines. For example, the Apple App Store requires apps to use HTTPS for all network connections and to follow best practices for securing sensitive data, such as using encryption and secure storage. Google Play also has similar security requirements and guidelines.</p><p>By implementing certificate pinning in your buying and selling app, you can help ensure that your app meets these security requirements and guidelines and provides a secure and trustworthy experience for your users.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'If i encrypt a data using a cryptographic key, and stored the encrypted data in db, what happens if I want to rotate my key?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>If you have encrypted data using a cryptographic key and want to rotate the key, you will need to decrypt the existing data using the old key and then re-encrypt it using the new key. This process is known as key rotation.</p><p>Here are the steps you would typically follow to rotate a cryptographic key and update the encrypted data in a database:</p><ol><li><p>Generate a new cryptographic key using a secure key generation algorithm, such as the Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) or RSA.</p></li><li><p>Update your application code to use the new key for encrypting and decrypting data. This might involve changing configuration files or updating code modules that use the key.</p></li><li><p>Decrypt the existing data in the database using the old key. This can be done using a decryption algorithm that corresponds to the encryption algorithm used to encrypt the data.</p></li><li><p>Re-encrypt the data using the new key. This can be done using an encryption algorithm that corresponds to the encryption algorithm used to encrypt the data.</p></li><li><p>Update the database with the new encrypted data. This might involve running SQL scripts or using a database migration tool.</p></li><li><p>Delete the old cryptographic key from your system, to ensure that it is no longer used for encrypting or decrypting data.</p></li></ol><p>It is important to carefully plan and test the key rotation process to ensure that it is done securely and without data loss or corruption. You should also consider using a key management system, such as AWS KMS or Google Cloud KMS, to help manage the key rotation process and ensure that keys are stored securely and accessed only by authorized users and applications.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'How to create bullets in google sheet'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>To create bullets in a Google Sheet, you can use the following steps:</p><ol><li><p>Select the cell(s) where you want to insert bullets.</p></li><li><p>Click on the "Format" menu.</p></li><li><p>Hover over the "Number" option, and then click on "More formats" at the bottom of the list.</p></li><li><p>In the "Custom number formats" box, delete any existing format codes and paste the following code:</p><p><code>@</code></p></li><li><p>Click "Apply" to see the bullets appear in the selected cell(s).</p></li></ol><p>Alternatively, you can also use the keyboard shortcut "Control + Shift + 8" (Windows) or "Command + Shift + 8" (Mac) to insert bullets in a cell.</p><p>Note that the bullet points are not actually bullets, but a character called a "bullet" which is included in many fonts. Therefore, if you change the font, the bullet point may appear differently or may not appear at all.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'In swiftui, how to store token by authenticated user'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>In SwiftUI, you can store a token by an authenticated user in several ways. Here are two common methods:</p><ol><li>UserDefault:
You can use the UserDefaults class to store the token in the user's device. This is a simple way to store small amounts of data, such as a token. Here is an example of how you can store the token using UserDefaults:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-comment">// Store token</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> token = <span class="hljs-string">"abcdefg12345"</span>
<span class="hljs-title class_">UserDefaults</span>.<span class="hljs-property">standard</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">set</span>(token, <span class="hljs-attr">forKey</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"userToken"</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment">// Retrieve token</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> retrievedToken = <span class="hljs-title class_">UserDefaults</span>.<span class="hljs-property">standard</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">string</span>(<span class="hljs-params">forKey: <span class="hljs-string">"userToken"</span></span>) {
    <span class="hljs-title function_">print</span>(retrievedToken)
}
</code></div></div></pre><ol start="2"><li>Keychain:
You can use the Keychain framework to store sensitive data, such as a user's authentication token. This method is more secure than storing data in UserDefaults because it encrypts the data and stores it in a secure location. Here is an example of how you can store the token using the Keychain:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">scss</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-scss"><span class="hljs-comment">// Store token</span>
let token = "abcdefg12345"
let keychain = <span class="hljs-built_in">Keychain</span>(service: "com.yourapp.token")
keychain<span class="hljs-selector-attr">[<span class="hljs-string">"userToken"</span>]</span> = token

<span class="hljs-comment">// Retrieve token</span>
if let retrievedToken = keychain<span class="hljs-selector-attr">[<span class="hljs-string">"userToken"</span>]</span> {
    <span class="hljs-built_in">print</span>(retrievedToken)
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In both methods, you can retrieve the token when the user logs in and use it for future API requests. It's important to ensure that the token is securely stored and only accessible by the authenticated user.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'But for keychain, I thought iphone (apple) users have the option of not using it. What would the app do in case user does not use keychain'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>You are correct that iPhone users have the option to disable the keychain feature in their device. If the keychain feature is disabled, your app will not be able to store the authentication token using the Keychain framework.</p><p>In this case, you can use other secure storage options such as the iOS Key-Value store or Core Data to store the authentication token. These options may not provide the same level of security as the Keychain, but they are still secure enough to store small amounts of sensitive data like authentication tokens.</p><p>Here's an example of how you can store the token using the iOS Key-Value store:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">swift</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-swift"><span class="hljs-comment">// Store token</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> token <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> <span class="hljs-string">"abcdefg12345"</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> key <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> <span class="hljs-string">"userToken"</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> bundleID <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> <span class="hljs-type">Bundle</span>.main.bundleIdentifier {
    <span class="hljs-type">UserDefaults</span>.standard.setValue(token, forKey: <span class="hljs-string">"<span class="hljs-subst">\(bundleID)</span>.<span class="hljs-subst">\(key)</span>"</span>)
}

<span class="hljs-comment">// Retrieve token</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> bundleID <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> <span class="hljs-type">Bundle</span>.main.bundleIdentifier {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> retrievedToken <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> <span class="hljs-type">UserDefaults</span>.standard.string(forKey: <span class="hljs-string">"<span class="hljs-subst">\(bundleID)</span>.<span class="hljs-subst">\(key)</span>"</span>) {
        <span class="hljs-built_in">print</span>(retrievedToken)
    }
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, the token is stored using the UserDefaults class with a key that includes the app's bundle identifier. This helps ensure that the token is only accessible by your app and not by other apps.</p><p>Note that storing sensitive data in any storage option is not completely secure, and there is always a risk of it being compromised. Therefore, it's important to follow best practices for securing user data and regularly review and update your app's security measures.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Weigh the Pros and cons of UserDefault vs iOS Key-Value store vs Core Data'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>Here are the pros and cons of each storage option:</p><p>UserDefault:
Pros:</p><ul><li>Easy to use and implement</li><li>Simple to store and retrieve small amounts of data, such as user settings or preferences</li><li>Can be accessed from anywhere within the app</li></ul><p>Cons:</p><ul><li>Not secure enough to store sensitive data, such as passwords or authentication tokens</li><li>Limited storage capacity, suitable only for storing small amounts of data</li><li>Data is stored in plain text and can be accessed by anyone with access to the device</li></ul><p>iOS Key-Value store:
Pros:</p><ul><li>Easy to use and implement</li><li>Secure enough to store small amounts of sensitive data, such as authentication tokens</li><li>Data is encrypted and stored in a secure location</li><li>Can be accessed from anywhere within the app</li></ul><p>Cons:</p><ul><li>Limited storage capacity, suitable only for storing small amounts of data</li><li>Not suitable for storing large amounts of data</li><li>No built-in support for querying or filtering data</li></ul><p>Core Data:
Pros:</p><ul><li>Suitable for storing large amounts of structured data</li><li>Provides built-in support for querying and filtering data</li><li>Can be used to implement complex data models and relationships</li></ul><p>Cons:</p><ul><li>More complex to use and implement compared to UserDefault or Key-Value store</li><li>Requires more code to store and retrieve data</li><li>Not suitable for storing unstructured data</li><li>Data is not encrypted by default and needs to be encrypted manually to ensure security.</li></ul><p>In summary, each storage option has its own advantages and disadvantages. UserDefault and Key-Value store are suitable for storing small amounts of data, while Core Data is more suitable for storing large amounts of structured data. If you need to store sensitive data, such as authentication tokens, you should use the Keychain or implement additional security measures to ensure data privacy and security.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': "I'm using next-auth with sessions stored in a database right now. How can I migrate over to JWT-based auth with next.js?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Migrating from session-based authentication to JWT-based authentication in a Next.js application using NextAuth.js involves a few steps. Here's</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "I'm using next-auth with sessions stored in a database right now. How can I migrate over to JWT-based auth with next.js?

I'm using Google OAuth."},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Migrating from session-based authentication to JWT-based authentication in a Next.js application using NextAuth.js with Google OAuth involves a few steps. Here's a high-level outline:</p><ol><li>Install dependencies:</li></ol><p>You should already have <code>next-auth</code> installed in your project. If you don't have it, install it with:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>bash</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-bash">npm install next-auth
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>Update your <code>[...nextauth].js</code> configuration file:</li></ol><p>You'll need to update your NextAuth.js configuration file to use JWTs instead of sessions stored in a database. The configuration file is typically located in your <code>pages/api/auth</code> folder and named <code>[...nextauth].js</code>.</p><p>Here's an example of how you might configure it to use JWTs:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">NextAuth</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'next-auth'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Providers</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'next-auth/providers'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> { <span class="hljs-variable constant_">JWT</span> } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'next-auth/jwt'</span>;

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> options = {
  <span class="hljs-attr">providers</span>: [
    <span class="hljs-title class_">Providers</span>.<span class="hljs-title class_">Google</span>({
      <span class="hljs-attr">clientId</span>: process.<span class="hljs-property">env</span>.<span class="hljs-property">GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID</span>,
      <span class="hljs-attr">clientSecret</span>: process.<span class="hljs-property">env</span>.<span class="hljs-property">GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET</span>,
    }),
  ],

  <span class="hljs-comment">// Switch to JWTs</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">session</span>: {
    <span class="hljs-attr">strategy</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'jwt'</span>,
  },

  <span class="hljs-comment">// JWT options</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">jwt</span>: {
    <span class="hljs-attr">secret</span>: process.<span class="hljs-property">env</span>.<span class="hljs-property">JWT_SECRET</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">encryption</span>: <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>, <span class="hljs-comment">// Optional: enable encryption for added security</span>
  },

  <span class="hljs-attr">callbacks</span>: {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">jwt</span>(<span class="hljs-params">token, user, account, profile, isNewUser</span>) {
      <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (user) {
        <span class="hljs-comment">// Set user data when signing in</span>
        token.<span class="hljs-property">id</span> = user.<span class="hljs-property">id</span>;
        token.<span class="hljs-property">email</span> = user.<span class="hljs-property">email</span>;
      }
      <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> token;
    },

    <span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">session</span>(<span class="hljs-params">session, token</span>) {
      <span class="hljs-comment">// Set user data in session</span>
      session.<span class="hljs-property">user</span> = {
        <span class="hljs-attr">id</span>: token.<span class="hljs-property">id</span>,
        <span class="hljs-attr">email</span>: token.<span class="hljs-property">email</span>,
      };
      <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> session;
    },
  },
};

<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">default</span> (req, res) =&gt; <span class="hljs-title class_">NextAuth</span>(req, res, options);
</code></div></div></pre><p>Replace the <code>GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET</code>, and <code>JWT_SECRET</code> values with your actual credentials. Make sure to keep your secrets in a secure environment variable or <code>.env</code> file.</p><ol><li>Update your client-side code:</li></ol><p>Instead of using the <code>useSession</code> hook from <code>next-auth/client</code>, you'll use the <code>useJwt</code> hook:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> { useJwt } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'next-auth/client'</span>;

<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">MyComponent</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> [jwt, loading] = <span class="hljs-title function_">useJwt</span>();

  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (loading) <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>Loading...<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span></span>;

  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (!jwt) <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>You must be signed in to view this content.<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span></span>;

  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>Welcome, {jwt.user.email}!<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span></span>;
}

<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">default</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">MyComponent</span>;
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>Remove any code related to session database storage:</li></ol><p>Since you're migrating to JWT-based authentication, you can remove any code related to storing sessions in a database.</p><p>With these changes, your Next.js application should be using JWT-based authentication with NextAuth.js and Google OAuth.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Will I lose user info this way? Will my existing users need to make new accounts?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Migrating from session-based authentication to JWT-based authentication will not cause your users to lose their account information or require them to create new accounts, as long as you maintain the same user identifier (usually the user's email) and provider (Google OAuth in your case).</p><p>The user information is typically stored in your database, and as long as the database remains intact, the user information will be preserved. The JWT-based authentication only changes the way the application handles the session, not the user's data in the database.</p><p>However, if you're currently storing additional data in the session, you'll need to modify the JWT callback in your <code>[...nextauth].js</code> configuration file to include that data in the JWT:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-attr">callbacks</span>: {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">jwt</span>(<span class="hljs-params">token, user, account, profile, isNewUser</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (user) {
      <span class="hljs-comment">// Set user data when signing in</span>
      token.<span class="hljs-property">id</span> = user.<span class="hljs-property">id</span>;
      token.<span class="hljs-property">email</span> = user.<span class="hljs-property">email</span>;

      <span class="hljs-comment">// Add any additional data you need</span>
      token.<span class="hljs-property">someData</span> = user.<span class="hljs-property">someData</span>;
    }
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> token;
  },

  <span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">session</span>(<span class="hljs-params">session, token</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// Set user data in session</span>
    session.<span class="hljs-property">user</span> = {
      <span class="hljs-attr">id</span>: token.<span class="hljs-property">id</span>,
      <span class="hljs-attr">email</span>: token.<span class="hljs-property">email</span>,

      <span class="hljs-comment">// Add any additional data you need</span>
      <span class="hljs-attr">someData</span>: token.<span class="hljs-property">someData</span>,
    };
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> session;
  },
},
</code></div></div></pre><p>When users sign in again after the migration, they will be authenticated using the same Google OAuth provider, and their account information will be retrieved from your database based on their unique identifier (email address in most cases).</p><p>In summary, migrating to JWT-based authentication will not cause users to lose their account information or require them to create new accounts, as long as you maintain the same user identifier and provider.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Why would anyone use a database for sessions? JWT seems so much better and faster.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Both database-stored sessions and JWTs have their own advantages and disadvantages, which is why some developers prefer one approach over the other. Here's a brief comparison of the two:</p><p><strong>Database-stored sessions:</strong></p><p>Advantages:</p><ol><li>Revoking sessions: With a database, you can easily revoke sessions by deleting the corresponding session record. This provides better control over user sessions and can be useful in cases where a user needs to be logged out immediately (e.g., account compromise).</li><li>Server-side control: All session data is stored server-side, which can offer better security since sensitive information is not exposed to the client.</li><li>Scalability: Database-stored sessions can be more easily managed in a distributed environment, with session data centralized and accessible by multiple server instances.</li></ol><p>Disadvantages:</p><ol><li>Performance: Database access adds latency to each request, as the session data needs to be fetched from the database. This can be mitigated by using caching mechanisms or in-memory databases like Redis.</li><li>Complexity: Managing sessions in a database requires additional setup, including configuring the database, handling migrations, and managing database connections.</li></ol><p><strong>JWTs:</strong></p><p>Advantages:</p><ol><li>Performance: JWTs are stored client-side, so there's no need to access a database to validate the user on each request. This reduces server load and improves performance.</li><li>Stateless: JWTs are self-contained tokens with all the necessary information to validate the user, making the system stateless and easier to scale horizontally.</li><li>Easier setup: JWTs do not require database setup or management, simplifying the authentication process.</li></ol><p>Disadvantages:</p><ol><li>Revoking tokens: It's difficult to revoke JWTs before they expire, as they are stored client-side and can be used for authentication as long as they're valid. One workaround is to use short-lived tokens and maintain a server-side blacklist of tokens that need to be revoked.</li><li>Token size: JWTs can become large if they store a lot of user information, which can increase the size of HTTP requests and responses.</li><li>Security: Since JWTs are stored client-side, there's a risk of exposure if an attacker manages to intercept the token. You should always use HTTPS to mitigate this risk.</li></ol><p>In summary, the choice between database-stored sessions and JWTs depends on the specific requirements of your application and your priorities regarding security, performance, and scalability. Some developers prefer the better control over sessions and security offered by database-stored sessions, while others prefer the performance and simplicity of JWTs.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'I switched to JWT tokens and now my server takes 15s to respond?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>A 15-second server response time after switching to JWT tokens is not expected behavior, as JWT tokens should generally lead to better performance compared to database-stored sessions. There might be other factors causing the delay in your application. Here are some suggestions to help you investigate and resolve the issue:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Check your JWT token size:</strong> Large JWT tokens can increase the size of HTTP requests and responses, causing delays. Ensure that you're only storing the necessary information in the token. Keep in mind that JWTs are sent with each request, so minimizing the token size is important.</p></li><li><p><strong>Profile your application:</strong> Use profiling tools or built-in Next.js metrics to identify bottlenecks in your application. This will help you understand which parts of your application are causing the delay.</p></li><li><p><strong>Inspect your callbacks:</strong> Double-check your <code>[...nextauth].js</code> configuration file, specifically the <code>jwt</code> and <code>session</code> callbacks. Make sure you're not performing any expensive operations or blocking calls within these callbacks.</p></li><li><p><strong>Review network requests:</strong> Use the Network tab in your browser's developer tools to inspect the requests being made by your application. This will help you identify any requests that are taking an unusually long time to complete. If you find such requests, investigate the cause and optimize the corresponding API or resource.</p></li><li><p><strong>Check for external dependencies:</strong> Ensure that any external dependencies, such as third-party APIs, are not causing the delay. Slow external requests can significantly impact the performance of your application.</p></li><li><p><strong>Optimize server-side code:</strong> Review your server-side code for any performance issues, such as inefficient database queries, unoptimized algorithms, or unnecessary blocking operations. Optimize your code to improve the overall performance of your application.</p></li><li><p><strong>Verify server resources:</strong> Make sure your server has sufficient resources (CPU, memory, and network bandwidth) to handle the traffic and workload. If your server is running low on resources, it might lead to slow response times.</p></li><li><p><strong>Check for rate limiting:</strong> Some services, such as the Google OAuth API, have rate limits that might slow down your application if you exceed the allowed number of requests. Verify that you're not exceeding the rate limits imposed by any of the services you're using.</p></li></ol><p>By investigating and addressing these potential issues, you should be able to improve the response time of your server after switching to JWT tokens.</p></div>"}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'hello karim, I need to setup a multifactor authentication for my mikrotik vpn. here is the requirement.

1. Radius
2. OpenLDAP
3. MFA

Please let me know how to start.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>To set up multifactor authentication (MFA) for your Mikrotik VPN using RADIUS and OpenLDAP, you will need to complete the following steps:</p><ol><li><p>Configure RADIUS on your Mikrotik router by setting up a RADIUS server and adding the appropriate RADIUS client settings.</p></li><li><p>Install and configure OpenLDAP on a separate server. You will need to create a directory structure, add users and groups, and configure the appropriate permissions.</p></li><li><p>Integrate RADIUS and OpenLDAP by configuring the RADIUS server to use OpenLDAP as the backend for authentication. This typically involves specifying the OpenLDAP server's IP address and credentials in the RADIUS server's configuration.</p></li><li><p>Configure MFA on the RADIUS server. You can use a third-party MFA solution, or a built-in solution, such as Google Authenticator.</p></li><li><p>Test the VPN connection to ensure that MFA is properly set up and that users are prompted to provide a second form of authentication when connecting.</p></li></ol><p>It's recommended to follow Mikrotik official documentation as well as other resources like tutorials and videos, which can provide more detailed instructions and troubleshooting tips.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Ok. explain no.1 in detail configuration'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>To configure RADIUS on your Mikrotik router, you will need to complete the following steps:</p><ol><li><p>In the Mikrotik router's web interface, go to the "Pools" menu and create a new RADIUS server by specifying the IP address and port of the RADIUS server.</p></li><li><p>Go to the "Users" menu and create new users or import them from a CSV file. Each user will need a unique username and password, which will be used to authenticate to the RADIUS server.</p></li><li><p>Go to the "Access" menu and create a new RADIUS client by specifying the IP address of the Mikrotik router and a shared secret. The shared secret is a password that is used to encrypt the communication between the Mikrotik router and the RADIUS server.</p></li><li><p>Go to the "Ppp" menu, and then to the "Secrets" tab, and create new PPP secrets. PPP secrets are used to authenticate the VPN clients that connect to the Mikrotik router. You will need to specify a username and password for each client, as well as the RADIUS server that the client will use for authentication.</p></li><li><p>Go to the "IP" menu, then to the "Firewall" and create a new firewall rule that allows incoming VPN connections. You will need to specify the protocol (usually "tcp" or "udp"), the port number (usually 1701, 1723, or 500), and the IP addresses of the VPN clients that are allowed to connect.</p></li><li><p>Go to the "IP" menu, then to the "Services" and enable the L2TP server.</p></li><li><p>Verify that the RADIUS server is properly configured by running a test using the "radius" command in the Mikrotik router's terminal.</p></li></ol><p>It's recommended to make sure that the RADIUS server is reachable from the Mikrotik router and also check the logs in the Mikrotik router and the RADIUS server for any errors.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Ok. explain no.2 in detail configuration'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>To configure OpenLDAP on a separate server, you will need to complete the following steps:</p><ol><li><p>Install OpenLDAP on the server by following the appropriate installation instructions for your operating system.</p></li><li><p>Create a directory structure for your OpenLDAP server. This typically involves creating a base DN (distinguished name) for your directory and then creating organizational units (OUs) for users, groups, and other objects.</p></li><li><p>Add users and groups to the directory by creating new entries using the "ldapadd" command. You will need to specify the DN of the entry, as well as its attributes, such as the user's username, password, and email address.</p></li><li><p>Configure permissions for the directory by creating access control lists (ACLs) that specify which users and groups are allowed to read, write, and modify entries in the directory.</p></li><li><p>Test the OpenLDAP server by connecting to it using the "ldapsearch" command and searching for a specific user or group.</p></li><li><p>Configure the OpenLDAP server to use a secure connection, such as TLS (Transport Layer Security) or SSL (Secure Sockets Layer).</p></li></ol><p>It's recommended to make sure that the OpenLDAP server is reachable from the Mikrotik router and also check the logs in the OpenLDAP server for any errors. Additionally, make sure to secure the OpenLDAP server with a strong administrator password and use proper security measures such as firewalls to limit access to the server.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Can you explain this code in freeradius mode-available/default configuration?

