</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, the <code>deposit()</code> and <code>withdraw()</code> functions use the <code>block.timestamp</code> variable instead of <code>now</code>, which guarantees that the contract will use the correct timestamp that can't be manipulated by a miner. This makes the contract more secure against this type of attack.</p><p>It's important to keep in mind that time-based vulnerabilities are not limited to this example and can occur in many other ways and in other forms, so always be aware of the potential bugs and vulnerabilities when developing smart contracts.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Thanks, can you expand more point 6?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark" bis_skin_checked="1"><p>Sure! The "DoS with Block Gas Limit" vulnerability occurs when a contract includes a feature that can be exploited by an attacker to cause a denial of service (DoS) attack by consuming all of the gas that is available in a block.</p><p>Here's an example of how a DoS with Block Gas Limit vulnerability can occur in Solidity:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md" bis_skin_checked="1"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans" bis_skin_checked="1"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto" bis_skin_checked="1"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-scss">contract Victim {
