</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, the <code>withdraw()</code> function uses a <code>lock</code> variable to ensure that the function can only be called once at a time. The <code>lock</code> variable is set to true before the critical section of the function is executed, and is set back to false after the function completes. This prevents the function from being called recursively, making the contract more secure against this type of attack.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Thanks, can you expand more point 8?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark" bis_skin_checked="1"><p>Sure! The "Frontrunning" vulnerability occurs when a contract includes a feature that allows an attacker to execute a transaction before another transaction that would have been executed first, by predicting the transaction that will be executed and executing their own transaction before it. This can be used by an attacker to steal assets or manipulate the contract's state.</p><p>Here's an example of how a frontrunning vulnerability can occur in Solidity:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md" bis_skin_checked="1"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans" bis_skin_checked="1"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto" bis_skin_checked="1"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-scss">contract Exchange {
    <span class="hljs-built_in">mapping</span>(address =&gt; uint) public buyOrders;
