[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Can you explain to me how a password manager works?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Sure! A password manager is a tool that helps you store, generate, and manage your passwords securely. Here's how it typically works:</p><ol><li><p>You create an account with a password manager service and set up a master password that you will use to access your password vault.</p></li><li><p>The password manager will then generate strong and unique passwords for all of your online accounts, which you can save within the password manager. This means that you don't have to remember any of your passwords, except for your master password.</p></li><li><p>When you need to log in to an online account, the password manager will autofill the username and password for you.</p></li><li><p>The password manager also stores other sensitive information, such as your credit card information, addresses, and notes.</p></li><li><p>To ensure that your passwords are secure, password managers use advanced encryption methods to protect your data, such as AES-256 encryption.</p></li><li><p>Many password managers also have additional security features, such as two-factor authentication, biometric authentication, and alerts for data breaches.</p></li></ol><p>In summary, a password manager is a convenient and secure way to manage all of your passwords and other sensitive information. By using a password manager, you can improve your online security and reduce the risk of password-related hacks and data breaches.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'When applying for a job at a company that utilizes Workday for their HR processes, candidates are required to create logins and passwords for every individual company because the software is tenanted. Once logged in, the Candidate Home Page experience is largely the same across companies. Can you help me create a website that stores my logins and passwords tied to each company's unique Workday URL and use the similar construction of the Candidate Home Pages to scrape and display data for multiple companies in one dashboard? 

Here is the most relevant code from the Q2 company's workday site: <section><div><h3>My Applications</h3><span class="css-1vdxpbu"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" class="wd-icon-chevron-up wd-icon"></svg></span></div><div><div><p><span>As we are evaluating your qualifications, we may contact you to provide additional information. In this case, you will receive a notification with instructions. Thank you for your interest in joining our team!</span></p></div></div><div>Active (1)Inactive (1)</div><div><table><thead></thead><tbody><tr><th><div><div>Implementation Engineer</div><div><div><span class="css-1j1yzp9"><span class="css-1ynlkts">Under Consideration</span></span></div></div></div></th><td><span class="css-9gpxd9"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" class="wd-icon-related-actions wd-icon"></svg></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div><table><thead></thead><tbody><tr><th><div><div>Customer Success Manager</div><div><div><span class="css-19xpll6"><span class="css-1ynlkts">Not selected</span></span></div></div></div></th><td><span class="css-9gpxd9"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" class="wd-icon-related-actions wd-icon"></svg></span></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>As an AI language model, I am not able to create a website for you. However, I can provide some guidance on how you can achieve your goal of storing and displaying data from multiple companies in one dashboard.</p><p>Firstly, you will need to create a database to store the login information for each company's unique Workday URL, along with the associated username and password. You could use a relational database like MySQL or a document-based database like MongoDB.</p><p>Next, you will need to develop a web application that can securely access and display the data from each company's Workday site. This can be done using web scraping techniques to extract the relevant data from each page, using Python libraries like Beautiful Soup and Selenium.</p><p>Once you have extracted the data from each company's Workday site, you can store it in your database and display it in a dashboard format using a web framework like Flask or Django. The dashboard can be designed to display the relevant information in a clear and concise way, using charts, tables, and other visualizations.</p><p>It's important to note that web scraping can be a complex and potentially fragile process, as websites may change their layout or structure over time. Therefore, you will need to regularly test and update your web scraping code to ensure that it continues to work reliably.</p><p>In summary, to create a website that stores your logins and passwords for multiple Workday sites and displays data in a dashboard format, you will need to:</p><ol><li>Create a database to store the login information for each company's Workday site</li><li>Develop a web application that can securely access and scrape data from each Workday site</li><li>Store the extracted data in your database</li><li>Display the data in a dashboard format using a web framework like Flask or Django</li></ol></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Expand on how to execute "Create a database to store the login information for each company's Workday site"'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Sure! To create a database to store the login information for each company's Workday site, you will need to follow these steps:</p><ol><li><p>Choose a database management system (DBMS) that suits your needs. There are many different types of DBMS available, such as relational databases like MySQL, PostgreSQL, and SQL Server, and document-based databases like MongoDB.</p></li><li><p>Install the chosen DBMS on your local machine or server, and make sure it's configured correctly. You will need to create a database instance, which will act as a container for your database objects.</p></li><li><p>Once you have a running database instance, you can create a new database within it. The syntax for creating a new database will depend on the DBMS you're using. For example, in MySQL, you can use the CREATE DATABASE statement, like this:</p></li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>sql</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-sql"><span class="hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> DATABASE workday_logins;
</code></div></div></pre><p>This will create a new database named "workday_logins".</p><ol><li>Next, you will need to create a table within the database to store the login information for each company's Workday site. The structure of the table will depend on the specific information you want to store, but it might include columns for the company name, Workday URL, username, and password. Here's an example of creating a table in MySQL:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>sql</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-sql"><span class="hljs-keyword">CREATE</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">TABLE</span> company_logins (
    id <span class="hljs-type">INT</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">NULL</span> AUTO_INCREMENT,
    company_name <span class="hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="hljs-number">255</span>) <span class="hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    workday_url <span class="hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="hljs-number">255</span>) <span class="hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    username <span class="hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="hljs-number">255</span>) <span class="hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    password <span class="hljs-type">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="hljs-number">255</span>) <span class="hljs-keyword">NOT</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">NULL</span>,
    <span class="hljs-keyword">PRIMARY</span> KEY (id)
);
</code></div></div></pre><p>This will create a new table named "company_logins" with columns for the company name, Workday URL, username, and password. The id column is an auto-incrementing primary key, which ensures that each row in the table has a unique identifier.</p><ol><li>Finally, you will need to write code in your web application to connect to the database and perform operations like inserting new login information, updating existing information, and retrieving data to display in the dashboard. The specific syntax for these operations will depend on the DBMS and programming language you're using.</li></ol><p>In summary, to create a database to store the login information for each company's Workday site, you will need to choose a DBMS, create a new database, create a table within the database, and write code to interact with the database in your web application.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Expand on Develop a web application that can securely access and scrape data from each Workday site'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>To develop a web application that can securely access and scrape data from each Workday site, you will need to follow these steps:</p><ol><li><p>Choose a programming language and web framework that suit your needs. Some popular choices for web development include JavaScript with frameworks like React or Angular, Python with Flask or Django, or Java with Spring.</p></li><li><p>Write code to securely authenticate with each Workday site using the login information stored in the database. The authentication process will depend on the specific authentication mechanism used by each Workday site, but typically involves sending a POST request with the username and password, and storing the resulting authentication token for future requests. Make sure to use secure HTTPS connections and follow best practices for handling sensitive user information.</p></li><li><p>Write code to scrape the relevant data from each Workday site. The data you want to scrape will depend on the specific information you're interested in, but might include job postings, application status, and candidate details. You will need to inspect the HTML structure of the Workday site and use web scraping libraries like BeautifulSoup or Scrapy to extract the desired data. Make sure to respect the site's terms of service and use rate limiting or other measures to avoid overloading the server.</p></li><li><p>Store the scraped data in your own database or other storage solution. You might use a relational database like MySQL or PostgreSQL, a NoSQL database like MongoDB, or a file-based solution like SQLite. Make sure to design the database schema and data structures to match the specific data you're collecting, and to handle any duplicates or inconsistencies.</p></li><li><p>Write code to display the scraped data in a dashboard or other user interface. You might use a web framework like React or Flask to build a responsive and user-friendly dashboard that allows users to filter and sort the data, view detailed information about specific candidates or job postings, and receive alerts or notifications when new data is available. Make sure to follow best practices for front-end web development, including accessibility, performance, and security considerations.</p></li></ol><p>In summary, to develop a web application that can securely access and scrape data from each Workday site, you will need to choose a programming language and web framework, write code to authenticate and scrape data from each site, store the data in your own database, and display the data in a dashboard or other user interface.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Show me the code for "Write code to securely authenticate with each Workday site using the login information stored in the database..." Use JavaScript with Python'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To write code to securely authenticate with each Workday site using the login information stored in the database using JavaScript with Python, you could use the <code>node-fetch</code> library for sending HTTP requests and the <code>node-postgres</code> library for interacting with a PostgreSQL database. Here's an example implementation:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> fetch = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'node-fetch'</span>);
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> { <span class="hljs-title class_">Client</span> } = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'pg'</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Connect to the PostgreSQL database</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> client = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Client</span>({
  <span class="hljs-attr">user</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'dbuser'</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">host</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'database.server.com'</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">database</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'mydatabase'</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">password</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'secretpassword'</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">port</span>: <span class="hljs-number">5432</span>,
});

client.<span class="hljs-title function_">connect</span>();

<span class="hljs-comment">// Define a function to authenticate with a Workday site using stored credentials</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">authenticate</span>(<span class="hljs-params">companyName</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-comment">// Get the stored credentials from the database</span>
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> res = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> client.<span class="hljs-title function_">query</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'SELECT * FROM logins WHERE company_name = $1'</span>, [companyName]);
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> { username, password, url } = res.<span class="hljs-property">rows</span>[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>];

  <span class="hljs-comment">// Send a POST request to the Workday login page with the stored credentials</span>
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> response = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">fetch</span>(<span class="hljs-string">`<span class="hljs-subst">${url}</span>/auth/usernamepassword/<span class="hljs-subst">${companyName}</span>`</span>, {
    <span class="hljs-attr">method</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'POST'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">headers</span>: {
      <span class="hljs-string">'Content-Type'</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'application/json'</span>,
    },
    <span class="hljs-attr">body</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">JSON</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">stringify</span>({
      username,
      password,
    }),
  });

  <span class="hljs-comment">// Check if the authentication was successful</span>
  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (response.<span class="hljs-property">ok</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// Extract the authentication token from the response headers</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> authToken = response.<span class="hljs-property">headers</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">get</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'authorization'</span>);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> authToken;
  } <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// Handle authentication errors</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> error = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> response.<span class="hljs-title function_">text</span>();
    <span class="hljs-keyword">throw</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Error</span>(<span class="hljs-string">`Authentication error for company <span class="hljs-subst">${companyName}</span>: <span class="hljs-subst">${error}</span>`</span>);
  }
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this code, the <code>authenticate</code> function takes a <code>companyName</code> argument and uses it to look up the stored credentials for that company in a PostgreSQL database. It then sends a POST request to the Workday login page with the stored username and password, and extracts the authentication token from the response headers. If the authentication fails, it throws an error.</p><p>Note that this code assumes that the Workday login page uses the <code>usernamepassword</code> authentication mechanism, and that the authentication token is returned in the <code>authorization</code> header of the response. You may need to adjust the code to match the specific authentication mechanism and response format used by your Workday site.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Do you know what spectacleJS is?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Yes, I'm familiar with SpectacleJS. It's a JavaScript library for creating presentations in the browser. SpectacleJS allows you to create interactive and visually appealing slideshows using React components. It includes a range of built-in components and supports custom components as well, so you can create presentations that are tailored to your specific needs.</p><p>SpectacleJS is often used for creating technical presentations, but it can also be used for other types of presentations such as product demos, marketing pitches, and more. It's an open-source library that is maintained by Formidable Labs and has a growing community of contributors.</p><p>If you're interested in learning more about SpectacleJS, you can check out the official documentation and examples on its website: <a href="https://formidable.com/open-source/spectacle/" target="_new">https://formidable.com/open-source/spectacle/</a></p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Write a presentation in SpectacleJS. It should load an achievements list from a FastAPI backend endpoint /achievements/ for a given person ID. Each achievement in the list has an id, name, description and metadata; each should be displayed on its own slide.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>As an AI language model, I don't have a graphical user interface to show you the presentation directly, but I can provide you with an example code to get started with using SpectacleJS and retrieving data from a FastAPI endpoint.</p><p>Here's an example presentation code that retrieves an achievement list for a given person ID from a FastAPI backend endpoint and displays each achievement on its own slide:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>jsx</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-jsx"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">React</span>, { useState, useEffect } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'react'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> { <span class="hljs-title class_">Deck</span>, <span class="hljs-title class_">Slide</span>, <span class="hljs-title class_">Heading</span>, <span class="hljs-title class_">Text</span> } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'spectacle'</span>;

<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">Presentation</span>(<span class="hljs-params">{ personId }</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> [achievements, setAchievements] = <span class="hljs-title function_">useState</span>([]);

  <span class="hljs-title function_">useEffect</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-title function_">fetch</span>(<span class="hljs-string">`/achievements/<span class="hljs-subst">${personId}</span>`</span>)
      .<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">response</span> =&gt;</span> response.<span class="hljs-title function_">json</span>())
      .<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-params">data</span> =&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">setAchievements</span>(data));
  }, [personId]);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> (
    <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">Deck</span>&gt;</span>
      {achievements.map(achievement =&gt; (
        <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">Slide</span> <span class="hljs-attr">key</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{achievement.id}</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">Heading</span>&gt;</span>{achievement.name}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">Heading</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">Text</span>&gt;</span>{achievement.description}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">Text</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">Text</span>&gt;</span>{achievement.metadata}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">Text</span>&gt;</span>
        <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">Slide</span>&gt;</span>
      ))}
    <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">Deck</span>&gt;</span></span>
  );
}

<span class="hljs-keyword">export</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">default</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Presentation</span>;
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we define a <code>Presentation</code> component that takes a <code>personId</code> prop as input. We use the <code>useState</code> hook to define a state variable <code>achievements</code> that will hold the list of achievements retrieved from the backend endpoint.</p><p>We use the <code>useEffect</code> hook to fetch the achievements data from the backend endpoint when the <code>personId</code> prop changes. Once we have the achievements data, we map over the list and create a <code>Slide</code> for each achievement, displaying its <code>name</code>, <code>description</code>, and <code>metadata</code>.</p><p>This code assumes that the FastAPI backend endpoint <code>/achievements/&lt;id&gt;</code> returns a list of achievements in the following format:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>json</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-json"><span class="hljs-punctuation">[</span>
  <span class="hljs-punctuation">{</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"id"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-number">1</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"name"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"First Achievement"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"description"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"This is the description of the first achievement."</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"metadata"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"Metadata for the first achievement."</span>
  <span class="hljs-punctuation">}</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
  <span class="hljs-punctuation">{</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"id"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-number">2</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"name"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"Second Achievement"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"description"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"This is the description of the second achievement."</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"metadata"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"Metadata for the second achievement."</span>
  <span class="hljs-punctuation">}</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
  ...
<span class="hljs-punctuation">]</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>Note that this is just an example code to give you an idea of how to get started with SpectacleJS and data retrieval. You may need to customize it to fit your specific use case and data format.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'How would you embed it in an existing page?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To embed the SpectacleJS presentation in an existing page, you can simply add the <code>Presentation</code> component to your page's React component hierarchy.</p><p>Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to embed the <code>Presentation</code> component in an existing page:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>jsx</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-jsx"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">React</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'react'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">ReactDOM</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'react-dom'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Presentation</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'./Presentation'</span>;

<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">App</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> personId = <span class="hljs-number">123</span>; <span class="hljs-comment">// replace with the actual person ID</span>

  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> (
    <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
      <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">h1</span>&gt;</span>My Achievements<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">h1</span>&gt;</span>
      <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">Presentation</span> <span class="hljs-attr">personId</span>=<span class="hljs-string">{personId}</span> /&gt;</span>
    <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span></span>
  );
}

<span class="hljs-title class_">ReactDOM</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">render</span>(<span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">App</span> /&gt;</span></span>, <span class="hljs-variable language_">document</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">getElementById</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'root'</span>));
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we define an <code>App</code> component that renders a <code>h1</code> heading and the <code>Presentation</code> component with the <code>personId</code> prop set to <code>123</code>. You can replace this with the actual person ID that you want to retrieve achievements for.</p><p>We then use <code>ReactDOM.render</code> to render the <code>App</code> component in a container element with the ID <code>root</code>. You can replace this with the ID of the container element in your own page where you want to embed the presentation.</p><p>Once you have added the <code>Presentation</code> component to your page, you should be able to see the list of achievements displayed as slides within the SpectacleJS presentation. You may need to style the presentation and the container element to fit your page's layout and design.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "How would you secure the presentation so that only someone who presents the SHA hash of a person's id can access the presentation?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To secure the presentation and restrict access only to authorized users who present a valid SHA hash of a person's ID, you can implement a server-side authentication system that generates and verifies the SHA hash.</p><p>Here's an example of how you can implement this using FastAPI and Python:</p><ol><li><p>When a user requests to access the presentation for a person's ID, the server generates a random token and stores it in a database along with the person's ID and the current timestamp.</p></li><li><p>The server computes the SHA hash of the token and sends it back to the user as a response.</p></li><li><p>The user must then present the SHA hash as a query parameter when requesting the presentation, e.g. <code>/presentation?hash=xxxxxx</code>.</p></li><li><p>The server receives the request and verifies that the SHA hash matches the SHA hash of the token stored in the database for the given person's ID, and that the token has not expired (e.g. has a timestamp less than a certain number of minutes ago).</p></li><li><p>If the verification is successful, the server returns the presentation, otherwise it returns an error message.</p></li></ol><p>Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to implement this in FastAPI:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>python</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-python"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> hashlib
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> random
<span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> datetime <span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> datetime, timedelta
<span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> fastapi <span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> FastAPI, HTTPException

app = FastAPI()
TOKEN_EXPIRATION_MINUTES = <span class="hljs-number">5</span>
tokens_db = {}

<span class="hljs-meta">@app.get(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-string">"/presentation"</span></span>)</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">def</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">get_presentation</span>(<span class="hljs-params">person_id: <span class="hljs-built_in">int</span>, <span class="hljs-built_in">hash</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">str</span></span>):
    <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> person_id <span class="hljs-keyword">not</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">in</span> tokens_db:
        <span class="hljs-keyword">raise</span> HTTPException(status_code=<span class="hljs-number">404</span>, detail=<span class="hljs-string">"Person ID not found"</span>)
    token, timestamp = tokens_db[person_id]
    hash_check = hashlib.sha256(token.encode(<span class="hljs-string">'utf-8'</span>)).hexdigest()
    <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">hash</span> != hash_check:
        <span class="hljs-keyword">raise</span> HTTPException(status_code=<span class="hljs-number">401</span>, detail=<span class="hljs-string">"Invalid token"</span>)
    <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> datetime.now() - timestamp &gt; timedelta(minutes=TOKEN_EXPIRATION_MINUTES):
        <span class="hljs-keyword">raise</span> HTTPException(status_code=<span class="hljs-number">401</span>, detail=<span class="hljs-string">"Token expired"</span>)
    <span class="hljs-comment"># return the presentation for the given person ID</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> {<span class="hljs-string">"message"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">f"Presentation for person <span class="hljs-subst">{person_id}</span>"</span>}

<span class="hljs-meta">@app.post(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-string">"/token"</span></span>)</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">def</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">generate_token</span>(<span class="hljs-params">person_id: <span class="hljs-built_in">int</span></span>):
    token = <span class="hljs-built_in">str</span>(random.getrandbits(<span class="hljs-number">128</span>))
    timestamp = datetime.now()
    tokens_db[person_id] = (token, timestamp)
    <span class="hljs-built_in">hash</span> = hashlib.sha256(token.encode(<span class="hljs-string">'utf-8'</span>)).hexdigest()
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> {<span class="hljs-string">"hash"</span>: <span class="hljs-built_in">hash</span>}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we define two endpoints: <code>/presentation</code> and <code>/token</code>. The <code>/presentation</code> endpoint expects a <code>person_id</code> and a <code>hash</code> query parameter, while the <code>/token</code> endpoint expects a <code>person_id</code> parameter.</p><p>When the user requests the presentation, the server checks if the person ID is in the database, computes the SHA hash of the stored token, and verifies that it matches the presented hash and has not expired. If the verification is successful, it returns the presentation.</p><p>When the user requests a token, the server generates a random token, stores it in the database along with the person's ID and the current timestamp, computes the SHA hash of the token, and returns the hash to the user. The user must then present this hash when requesting the presentation.</p><p>Note that this is just an example code to give you an idea of how to implement server-side authentication with FastAPI and Python. You may need to customize it to fit your specific use case and requirements.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'How Application Security vulnerabilities such as Owasp Top 10 relates to compliance standards like GDPR, give examples with code in PHP and .NET MVC'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Application Security vulnerabilities, such as those listed in the OWASP Top 10, can directly impact an organization's compliance with data protection regulations like the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR). GDPR is a comprehensive data protection law in the European Union that imposes strict requirements on organizations that collect, process, and store personal data of EU residents. Among these requirements are ensuring data privacy, security, and integrity.</p><p>Here, we'll discuss two OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities and provide examples of how they can relate to GDPR, along with sample code in PHP and .NET MVC.</p><ol><li>Injection (A1:2017)
Injection vulnerabilities occur when an attacker can send untrusted data to an interpreter, leading to the execution of malicious commands or unauthorized data access. An SQL injection, for example, could lead to unauthorized access to personal data or even data deletion, which would violate GDPR's data security and integrity requirements.</li></ol><p>PHP example (vulnerable code):</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>php</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-php"><span class="hljs-variable">$query</span> = <span class="hljs-string">"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '"</span> . <span class="hljs-variable">$_GET</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'email'</span>] . <span class="hljs-string">"' AND password = '"</span> . <span class="hljs-variable">$_GET</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'password'</span>] . <span class="hljs-string">"'"</span>;
<span class="hljs-variable">$result</span> = <span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">mysqli_query</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$conn</span>, <span class="hljs-variable">$query</span>);
</code></div></div></pre><p>To prevent injection attacks, use prepared statements and parameterized queries:</p><p>PHP example (secure code):</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>php</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-php"><span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span> = <span class="hljs-variable">$conn</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">prepare</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = ? AND password = ?"</span>);
<span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">bind_param</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"ss"</span>, <span class="hljs-variable">$_GET</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'email'</span>], <span class="hljs-variable">$_GET</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'password'</span>]);
<span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">execute</span>();
<span class="hljs-variable">$result</span> = <span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">get_result</span>();
</code></div></div></pre><p>.NET MVC example (vulnerable code):</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>csharp</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-csharp"><span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> query = <span class="hljs-string">"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '"</span> + Request[<span class="hljs-string">"email"</span>] + <span class="hljs-string">"' AND Password = '"</span> + Request[<span class="hljs-string">"password"</span>] + <span class="hljs-string">"'"</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> result = db.Database.SqlQuery&lt;User&gt;(query).ToList();
</code></div></div></pre><p>To prevent injection attacks, use Entity Framework's LINQ or parameterized queries:</p><p>.NET MVC example (secure code):</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>csharp</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-csharp"><span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> email = Request[<span class="hljs-string">"email"</span>];
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> password = Request[<span class="hljs-string">"password"</span>];
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> result = db.Users.Where(u =&gt; u.Email == email &amp;&amp; u.Password == password).ToList();
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>Sensitive Data Exposure (A3:2017)
Sensitive Data Exposure occurs when an application does not adequately protect sensitive information, such as personal data, which can lead to unauthorized access or disclosure. This directly violates GDPR's data privacy and security requirements.</li></ol><p>For instance, storing passwords in plain text is a sensitive data exposure vulnerability.</p><p>PHP example (vulnerable code):</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>php</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-php"><span class="hljs-variable">$password</span> = <span class="hljs-variable">$_POST</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'password'</span>];
<span class="hljs-variable">$query</span> = <span class="hljs-string">"INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES (?, ?)"</span>;
<span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span> = <span class="hljs-variable">$conn</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">prepare</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$query</span>);
<span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">bind_param</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"ss"</span>, <span class="hljs-variable">$_POST</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'email'</span>], <span class="hljs-variable">$password</span>);
<span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">execute</span>();
</code></div></div></pre><p>To mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong hashing algorithm like bcrypt:</p><p>PHP example (secure code):</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>php</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-php"><span class="hljs-variable">$password</span> = <span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">password_hash</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$_POST</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'password'</span>], PASSWORD_BCRYPT);
<span class="hljs-variable">$query</span> = <span class="hljs-string">"INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES (?, ?)"</span>;
<span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span> = <span class="hljs-variable">$conn</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">prepare</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$query</span>);
<span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">bind_param</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"ss"</span>, <span class="hljs-variable">$_POST</span>[<span class="hljs-string">'email'</span>], <span class="hljs-variable">$password</span>);
<span class="hljs-variable">$stmt</span>-&gt;<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">execute</span>();
</code></div></div></pre><p>.NET MVC example (vulnerable code):</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>csharp</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-csharp"><span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> user = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> User
{
    Email = Request[<span class="hljs-string">"email"</span>],
    Password = Request[<span class="hljs-string">"password"</span>]
};
db.Users.Add(user);
db.SaveChanges();
</code></div></div></pre><p>To mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong hashing algorithm like BCrypt:</p><p>.NET MVC example (secure code):</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>csharp</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-csharp"><span class="hljs-keyword">using</span> Microsoft.AspNetCore.Cryptography.KeyDerivation;

