</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, you'll need to replace <code>&lt;YOUR_FACEBOOK_APP_ID&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;YOUR_FACEBOOK_APP_SECRET&gt;</code> with your actual Facebook app ID and app secret, respectively.</p><p>Note that passing your app secret in client-side code is generally not recommended for security reasons. Instead, you should handle authentication on your server and use a secure flow like OAuth to authenticate users with Facebook.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'What if I want to use only analytics'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>If you want to use only the Facebook Analytics feature of the <code>react-native-fbsdk-next</code> library, you don't need to pass any credentials in your JS code.</p><p>To use Facebook Analytics, you'll first need to create a Facebook App and obtain an App ID from the Facebook Developer portal. Once you have an App ID, you can initialize the SDK in your app's entry point file like this:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">js</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-js"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> { <span class="hljs-title class_">FBAnalytics</span> } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'react-native-fbsdk-next'</span>;

