[ {'from': 'human', 'value': '1 / 1I have a net core 6 web api that uses Microsoft.Identity (aspnetusers, aspnetroles) etc tables in Postgres, Even though I am not using [Authorize] on my action methods, I am still seeing db connections (seemingly when I pass an .AspNet.Sharedcookie http header) to select from these identity tables. Can you help me understand when any why these tables are being read from?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>It seems that even without using the <code>[Authorize]</code> attribute explicitly, your .NET Core 6 Web API is still configured to use the default authentication and authorization middleware. When you pass the <code>.AspNet.SharedCookie</code> header, the middleware is trying to validate and process the authentication data, which involves querying the Identity tables in your Postgres database.</p><p>To understand when and why these tables are being read from, let's take a closer look at the authentication process in a .NET Core Web API:</p><ol><li>When a client sends a request with an authentication token or cookie (in this case, <code>.AspNet.SharedCookie</code>), the middleware checks if the token or cookie is valid.</li><li>If the token or cookie is valid, the middleware retrieves user and role information from the Identity tables in the database (aspnetusers, aspnetroles, etc.). This is to create a user principal, which represents the authenticated user and their associated claims and roles.</li><li>The user principal is then stored in the <code>HttpContext.User</code> property, which is accessible throughout the request pipeline.</li></ol><p>To prevent this behavior, you can do the following:</p><ol><li><p>Review your <code>Startup.cs</code> file and make sure that you're not adding the authentication middleware unintentionally. Look for lines like <code>app.UseAuthentication();</code> and <code>app.UseAuthorization();</code>. If you don't need authentication and authorization, you can remove or comment out these lines.</p></li><li><p>If you still want to use authentication for some parts of your application but want to avoid querying the Identity tables, you can use the <code>[AllowAnonymous]</code> attribute on the specific action methods or controllers where you don't want the authentication to happen. This will bypass the authentication middleware for those particular methods.</p></li><li><p>Alternatively, you can create a custom authentication middleware that doesn't interact with the Identity tables or use a different authentication scheme altogether.</p></li></ol><p>Keep in mind that disabling authentication or using <code>[AllowAnonymous]</code> might expose your API to unauthorized access, so only use these approaches if you understand the potential security risks.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': '1 / 1I have found the following lines, can you help me to re-write them as custom homegrown middleware?

            services.AddAuthorization(options =&gt;
            {
                options.AddPolicy("Authenticated", policy =&gt; policy.RequireAuthenticatedUser());
            });

            services.AddAuthorization(options =&gt;
            {
                options.AddPolicy("Authenticated", policy =&gt; policy.RequireAuthenticatedUser());
            });'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Sure! Let's first create a custom middleware class to handle the authentication logic. The following example middleware checks for the presence of a custom token in the request header, but you can customize the logic as needed:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>csharp</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-csharp"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title">CustomAuthenticationMiddleware</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">readonly</span> RequestDelegate _next;

    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-title">CustomAuthenticationMiddleware</span>(<span class="hljs-params">RequestDelegate next</span>)</span>
    {
        _next = next;
    }

    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">async</span> Task <span class="hljs-title">InvokeAsync</span>(<span class="hljs-params">HttpContext context</span>)</span>
    {
        <span class="hljs-comment">// Check for the presence of a custom token in the request header</span>
        <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (context.Request.Headers.TryGetValue(<span class="hljs-string">"Custom-Token"</span>, <span class="hljs-keyword">out</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> tokenValue))
        {
            <span class="hljs-comment">// Validate the token and set the context.User property accordingly</span>
            <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (IsValidToken(tokenValue))
            {
                <span class="hljs-comment">// Create a custom ClaimsPrincipal and set it as the context.User</span>
                <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> claims = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span>[] { <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> Claim(ClaimTypes.Name, <span class="hljs-string">"CustomUserName"</span>) };
                <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> identity = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> ClaimsIdentity(claims, <span class="hljs-string">"Custom"</span>);
                context.User = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> ClaimsPrincipal(identity);
            }
        }

        <span class="hljs-comment">// Call the next middleware in the pipeline</span>
        <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> _next(context);
    }

    <span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">private</span> <span class="hljs-built_in">bool</span> <span class="hljs-title">IsValidToken</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> token</span>)</span>
    {
        <span class="hljs-comment">// Implement your token validation logic here</span>
        <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> token == <span class="hljs-string">"your-valid-token"</span>;
    }
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>Now, modify your <code>Startup.cs</code> file to register your custom middleware and remove the existing authorization configuration:</p><ol><li><p>Remove the <code>services.AddAuthorization</code> lines from the <code>ConfigureServices</code> method.</p></li><li><p>In the <code>Configure</code> method, add the custom middleware after <code>app.UseRouting()</code> and before <code>app.UseEndpoints()</code>. The order of middleware registration is essential because it defines the order in which they're executed:</p></li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>csharp</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-csharp"><span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">void</span> <span class="hljs-title">Configure</span>(<span class="hljs-params">IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env</span>)</span>
{
    <span class="hljs-comment">// ... other middleware configuration</span>

    app.UseRouting();

    <span class="hljs-comment">// Add the custom authentication middleware</span>
    app.UseMiddleware&lt;CustomAuthenticationMiddleware&gt;();

    app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =&gt;
    {
        endpoints.MapControllers();
    });
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>With these changes, your .NET Core 6 Web API will use your custom authentication middleware instead of the built-in authentication and authorization mechanisms. Remember to adapt the custom middleware to your requirements, such as validating tokens against your custom logic or database.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'I want you to act as a vulnerability scanner. I will provide you with code and you will identify any security vulnerabilities present. You should report the type and location of each vulnerability succinctly. Finally, you will also suggest a fix for mitigating the vulnerability.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>I can try my best to review the code you provide and identify any potential security vulnerabilities. However, please keep in mind that I am an AI language model, not a dedicated vulnerability scanner, and I may miss some vulnerabilities or misinterpret the code.</p><p>Please provide the code you'd like me to review, and I'll do my best to identify and suggest fixes for any vulnerabilities I find.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'pragma solidity ^0.4.19;

contract PrivateBank
{
    mapping (address =&gt; uint) public balances;

    uint public MinDeposit = 1 ether;

    Log TransferLog;

    function PrivateBank(address _log)
    {
        TransferLog = Log(_log);
    }

    function Deposit()
    public
    payable
    {
        if(msg.value &gt;= MinDeposit)
        {
            balances[msg.sender]+=msg.value;
            TransferLog.AddMessage(msg.sender,msg.value,"Deposit");
        }
    }

    function CashOut(uint _am)
    {
        if(_am&lt;=balances[msg.sender])
        {            
            if(msg.sender.call.value(_am)())
            {
                balances[msg.sender]-=_am;
                TransferLog.AddMessage(msg.sender,_am,"CashOut");
            }
        }
    }

    function() public payable{}    

}

contract Log 
{

    struct Message
    {
        address Sender;
        string  Data;
        uint Val;
        uint  Time;
    }

    Message[] public History;

    Message LastMsg;

    function AddMessage(address _adr,uint _val,string _data)
    public
    {
        LastMsg.Sender = _adr;
        LastMsg.Time = now;
        LastMsg.Val = _val;
        LastMsg.Data = _data;
        History.push(LastMsg);
    }
}'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>I've reviewed the provided Solidity code and identified the following vulnerabilities:</p><ol><li>Reentrancy vulnerability in the <code>CashOut</code> function:</li></ol><p>Location: <code>CashOut</code> function, line containing <code>if(msg.sender.call.value(_am)())</code></p><p>Issue: The contract's state is updated after the external call, which can lead to a reentrancy attack.</p><p>Fix: Use the checks-effects-interactions pattern. Update the contract's state before making the external call.</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>scss</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-scss">function <span class="hljs-built_in">CashOut</span>(uint _am)
{
    <span class="hljs-built_in">if</span>(_am &lt;= balances[msg.sender])
    {            
        balances<span class="hljs-selector-attr">[msg.sender]</span> -= _am;
        (bool success, ) = msg<span class="hljs-selector-class">.sender</span><span class="hljs-selector-class">.call</span>{value: _am}("");
        <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(success, "Transfer failed.");
        TransferLog<span class="hljs-selector-class">.AddMessage</span>(msg.sender, _am, "CashOut");
    }
}
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>Usage of a deprecated Solidity version:</li></ol><p>Location: <code>pragma solidity ^0.4.19;</code></p><p>Issue: The code uses an older version of Solidity which may contain known security vulnerabilities and lacks newer features.</p><p>Fix: Update the Solidity version to the latest stable release and make any necessary adjustments to the code.</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs">pragma solidity ^0.8.0;
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>Restricting access to the <code>AddMessage</code> function:</li></ol><p>Location: <code>function AddMessage(address _adr,uint _val,string _data)</code> in the <code>Log</code> contract.</p><p>Issue: The function is public, which means anyone can add messages to the log.</p><p>Fix: Make the function <code>internal</code> or add a modifier to restrict access to only the <code>PrivateBank</code> contract.</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>csharp</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-csharp"><span class="hljs-function">function <span class="hljs-title">AddMessage</span>(<span class="hljs-params">address _adr, <span class="hljs-built_in">uint</span> _val, <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span> memory _data</span>)
    <span class="hljs-keyword">internal</span></span>
{
    <span class="hljs-comment">// function implementation</span>
}
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>Use <code>immutable</code> for the <code>TransferLog</code>:</li></ol><p>Location: <code>Log TransferLog;</code> in the <code>PrivateBank</code> contract.</p><p>Issue: The <code>TransferLog</code> variable is not marked as <code>immutable</code>, which can lead to potential security risks or gas inefficiencies.</p><p>Fix: Declare the <code>TransferLog</code> variable as <code>immutable</code>.</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs">Log immutable TransferLog;
</code></div></div></pre><p>Please note that these suggestions are not exhaustive, and a thorough security audit by a professional is always recommended before deploying a smart contract.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'help me design a chart that contains all the pixel phone apps i downlodaded and all my chrome extensions so I can choose which ones to keep'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>One way to design a chart for this purpose is to create a table with two columns: one for the apps and one for the extensions. In the first column, list all the Pixel phone apps you have downloaded. In the second column, list all your Chrome extensions. You could also add additional columns to the table to include information such as the date you downloaded the app or extension, the last time you used it, and a rating or notes on its usefulness.</p><p>You can also use a spreadsheet program like Microsoft Excel or Google Sheets to create the chart. This will allow you to sort and filter the data to make it easier to review and decide which apps and extensions to keep.</p><p>Alternatively, you can use a notebook or sticky notes to create a list of all your apps and extensions and then organize them by category like social media, productivity, games etc. and decide which one to keep or delete.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'what criteria can i use to choose which apps to get rid of, which apps to keep, etc'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>When deciding which apps to keep and which to get rid of, you may want to consider the following criteria:</p><ol><li><p>Frequency of use: Keep apps that you use regularly and get rid of ones that you haven't used in a while.</p></li><li><p>Purpose: Keep apps that serve a specific purpose or function that you need, and get rid of ones that do not.</p></li><li><p>Storage space: Get rid of apps that take up a lot of storage space on your device and keep the ones that do not consume much space.</p></li><li><p>Battery usage: Get rid of apps that consume a lot of battery power and keep the ones that do not.</p></li><li><p>Security and privacy: Get rid of apps that may compromise your security or privacy and keep the ones that have good security and privacy practices.</p></li><li><p>Duplicate apps: Get rid of duplicate apps if you have more than one app that serves the same purpose.</p></li><li><p>Personal preference: Keep apps that you enjoy using and get rid of ones that you don't find useful or enjoyable.</p></li></ol><p>It's also a good idea to periodically review your apps and extensions to see which ones you still use and which ones you can remove. You can use the criteria above and also give a rating to each of them using your own criteria.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'is there a quick way to get a list of all of my phone apps and my extensions that i can then copy/paste into a spreadsheet?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Yes, there is a quick way to get a list of all of your phone apps and extensions that you can copy and paste into a spreadsheet.</p><p>For Android phones:</p><ol><li>Go to the Google Play Store app on your phone</li><li>Tap the hamburger icon (three horizontal lines) on the top left corner</li><li>Tap "My apps &amp; games"</li><li>Tap "Installed"</li><li>Select the apps you want to export, tap on the three dots on the top right corner and select "Share"</li><li>You can share the apps list by email, or export it as a file in your phone, copy the exported apps list and paste it into a spreadsheet.</li></ol><p>For Chrome extensions:</p><ol><li>Open the Chrome browser on your computer</li><li>Go to chrome://extensions/</li><li>On the top right corner of the page, you can select the "Export" button, this will download a file with all your installed extensions.</li></ol><p>This will give you a list of all the apps and extensions you have installed on your device. You can then copy and paste this information into a spreadsheet for further organization and analysis.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'how can i find out if my downloaded android apps or my chrome extensions are safe?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>There are a few ways to check if your downloaded Android apps or Chrome extensions are safe:</p><ol><li><p>Check the developer: Look for apps and extensions that are developed by reputable companies or organizations. If an app or extension is developed by a well-known and respected developer, it is more likely to be safe.</p></li><li><p>Check the reviews: Look at the reviews and ratings of the app or extension. If it has many positive reviews and a high rating, it is more likely to be safe. However, be aware that fake reviews exist, so you should also pay attention to the number of reviews and the level of detail in the reviews.</p></li><li><p>Check the permissions: Before installing an app, check the permissions it requires. If an app or extension is asking for permissions that are not related to its intended function, it may be a sign that it is not safe.</p></li><li><p>Check the source: Only download apps and extensions from official app stores like Google Play Store for Android apps and Chrome Web Store for Chrome extensions. These stores have a process for vetting and reviewing apps and extensions, so it's more likely to be safe.</p></li><li><p>Use anti-virus software: Install anti-virus software on your device. This will help you detect and remove any malicious apps or extensions that you may have accidentally installed.</p></li></ol><p>It is important to note that no method is foolproof and it's important to keep your device updated as well as your apps and extensions.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'is there a website or an app that can automatically scan my phone or my chrome and see which ones are unsafe?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Yes, there are websites and apps that can automatically scan your phone or Chrome browser to check for unsafe apps and extensions.</p><p>For Android phones:</p><ol><li><p>Google Play Protect: This is a built-in feature on Android devices that scans apps for malware and other security threats. It is automatically enabled when you download apps from the Google Play Store.</p></li><li><p>Malwarebytes: This is a third-party app that scans your device for malware, adware, and other malicious software. It can also scan apps that you have downloaded from outside of the Google Play Store.</p></li></ol><p>For Chrome browser:</p><ol><li><p>Extension Checker: This is a chrome extension which can scan your chrome browser and check if any of your installed extension is unsafe, malicious or has been compromised.</p></li><li><p>Google Chrome Cleanup Tool: This is a tool developed by Google to scan your Chrome browser and remove any unwanted software, including malware, that might be affecting the performance of your browser.</p></li></ol><p>It's important to note that these tools are not 100% foolproof and new malware and malicious software are constantly being developed. So it's important to keep your device, apps, and extensions updated and also be cautious when downloading new apps or extensions.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'how can i automatically batch delete all the cells with a certain character in google sheets?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="text-orange-500 markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>There are a few ways to automatically batch delete all the cells with a certain character in Google Sheets:</p><ol><li>Using the "Find and Replace" function:</li></ol><ul><li>Select the entire sheet by clicking on the top-left corner of the sheet where the row and column numbers intersect</li><li>Go to "Edit" &gt; "Find and Replace"</li><li>In the "Find" field, enter the character you want to delete</li><li>Leave the "Replace" field empty</li><li>Click on "Replace All"</li><li>This will remove all cells that contain the specified character.</li></ul><ol start="2"><li>Using the "Filter" function:</li></ol><ul><li>Select the entire sheet by clicking on the top-left corner of the sheet where the row and column numbers intersect</li><li>Go to "Data" &gt; "Filter"</li><li>Click on the drop-down arrow for the column that contains the character you want to delete</li><li>Select "Text contains" from the list of options and type in the character you want to delete</li><li>All the rows that contain that character will be highlighted</li><li>Select the entire sheet again</li><li>Press the delete button or right-click and select "Delete rows"</li></ul><ol start="3"><li>Using a script:</li></ol><ul><li>Go to "Tools" &gt; "Script editor"</li><li>In the script editor, you can write a script to delete all cells that contain a certain character. You can use the following script as a starting point:</li></ul><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">css</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-css">function deleteCellsWithCharacter(sheet, character) {
  <span class="hljs-selector-tag">var</span> data = sheet<span class="hljs-selector-class">.getDataRange</span>()<span class="hljs-selector-class">.getValues</span>();
  for (<span class="hljs-selector-tag">var</span> <span class="hljs-selector-tag">i</span> = <span class="hljs-number">0</span>; <span class="hljs-selector-tag">i</span> &lt; data<span class="hljs-selector-class">.length</span>; <span class="hljs-selector-tag">i</span>++) {
    for (<span class="hljs-selector-tag">var</span> j = <span class="hljs-number">0</span>; j &lt; data<span class="hljs-selector-attr">[i]</span><span class="hljs-selector-class">.length</span>; j++) {
      if (data<span class="hljs-selector-attr">[i]</span><span class="hljs-selector-attr">[j]</span><span class="hljs-selector-class">.toString</span>()<span class="hljs-selector-class">.indexOf</span>(character) &gt; -<span class="hljs-number">1</span>) {
        sheet<span class="hljs-selector-class">.getRange</span>(<span class="hljs-selector-tag">i</span> + <span class="hljs-number">1</span>, j + <span class="hljs-number">1</span>)<span class="hljs-selector-class">.clear</span>();
      }
    }
  }
}
</code></div></div></pre><ul><li>Replace "sheet" with the name of the sheet you want to delete the cells from and "character" with the character you want to delete.</li><li>Run the script by clicking on the "play" button.</li></ul><p>Please note that all the above methods will delete the cells permanently and cannot be undone, so make sure to create a backup of your sheet before making any changes.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'can i batch delete empty cells'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Yes, you can batch delete empty cells in Google Sheets. There are a few ways to do this:</p><ol><li>Using the "Filter" function:</li></ol><ul><li>Select the entire sheet by clicking on the top-left corner of the sheet where the row and column numbers intersect</li><li>Go to "Data" &gt; "Filter"</li><li>Click on the drop-down arrow for the column that contains the empty cells</li><li>Select "Filter by condition" and then "is empty"</li><li>All the rows that contain empty cells in that column will be highlighted</li><li>Select the entire sheet again</li><li>Press the delete button or right-click and select "Delete rows"</li></ul><ol start="2"><li>Using a script:</li></ol><ul><li>Go to "Tools" &gt; "Script editor"</li><li>In the script editor, you can write a script to delete all empty cells. You can use the following script as a starting point:</li></ul><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">css</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-css">function deleteEmptyCells(sheet) {
  <span class="hljs-selector-tag">var</span> data = sheet<span class="hljs-selector-class">.getDataRange</span>()<span class="hljs-selector-class">.getValues</span>();
  for (<span class="hljs-selector-tag">var</span> <span class="hljs-selector-tag">i</span> = <span class="hljs-number">0</span>; <span class="hljs-selector-tag">i</span> &lt; data<span class="hljs-selector-class">.length</span>; <span class="hljs-selector-tag">i</span>++) {
    for (<span class="hljs-selector-tag">var</span> j = <span class="hljs-number">0</span>; j &lt; data<span class="hljs-selector-attr">[i]</span><span class="hljs-selector-class">.length</span>; j++) {
      if (data<span class="hljs-selector-attr">[i]</span><span class="hljs-selector-attr">[j]</span> == "") {
        sheet<span class="hljs-selector-class">.getRange</span>(<span class="hljs-selector-tag">i</span> + <span class="hljs-number">1</span>, j + <span class="hljs-number">1</span>)<span class="hljs-selector-class">.clear</span>();
      }
    }
  }
}
</code></div></div></pre><ul><li>Replace "sheet" with the name of the sheet you want to delete the empty cells from.</li><li>Run the script by clicking on the "play" button.</li></ul><p>Please note that all the above methods will delete the empty cells permanently and cannot be undone, so make sure to create a backup of your sheet before making any changes.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'I am working on an application based on SpringBoot and mybatis. I config the database connection information in application.yaml. But the database connection information will passed by env var on the server. 
The content of the env var is a JSON format string, like :
```
{
  "cli": {},
  "postgres": {
    "authentication": {
      "method": "direct",
      "password": "xxx",
      "username": "user"
    },
    "certificate": {
      "certificate_authority": "self_signed",
      "certificate_base64": "...",
      "name": "631b75c3-4296-4ce9-b0f2-426423c5b0e6"
    },
    "composed": [
      "postgres://user:xxx@host.com:32737/ibmclouddb?sslmode=verify-full"
    ],
    "database": "ibmclouddb",
    "hosts": [
      {
        "hostname": "host.com",
        "port": 32737
      }
    ],
    "path": "/ibmclouddb",
    "query_options": {
      "sslmode": "verify-full"
    },
    "scheme": "postgres",
    "type": "uri"
  }
}
```

The env var name is DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION. 

Is there an easy way to set the application.yaml? '},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Yes, you can use the Spring Boot's support for externalized configuration to read environment variables and set the appropriate properties in your <code>application.yaml</code>. In this case, you'll want to read the <code>DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION</code> environment variable and parse the JSON string to extract the necessary properties.</p><p>Here's an example of how you can configure your <code>application.yaml</code>:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>yaml</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-yaml"><span class="hljs-attr">spring:</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">datasource:</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">url:</span> <span class="hljs-string">${MYAPP_DB_URL}</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">username:</span> <span class="hljs-string">${MYAPP_DB_USERNAME}</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">password:</span> <span class="hljs-string">${MYAPP_DB_PASSWORD}</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">driver-class-name:</span> <span class="hljs-string">org.postgresql.Driver</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>Now, you need to set the <code>MYAPP_DB_URL</code>, <code>MYAPP_DB_USERNAME</code>, and <code>MYAPP_DB_PASSWORD</code> environment variables before starting your Spring Boot application. You can achieve this by parsing the <code>DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION</code> JSON string in a script and exporting the necessary variables before starting the application:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>bash</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-bash"><span class="hljs-meta">#!/bin/bash</span>

<span class="hljs-comment"># Read DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION env var and parse JSON</span>
DATABASES_JSON=$(<span class="hljs-built_in">echo</span> <span class="hljs-variable">$DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION</span> | jq <span class="hljs-string">'.'</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment"># Extract properties</span>
DB_URL=$(<span class="hljs-built_in">echo</span> <span class="hljs-variable">$DATABASES_JSON</span> | jq -r <span class="hljs-string">'.postgres.composed[0]'</span>)
DB_USERNAME=$(<span class="hljs-built_in">echo</span> <span class="hljs-variable">$DATABASES_JSON</span> | jq -r <span class="hljs-string">'.postgres.authentication.username'</span>)
DB_PASSWORD=$(<span class="hljs-built_in">echo</span> <span class="hljs-variable">$DATABASES_JSON</span> | jq -r <span class="hljs-string">'.postgres.authentication.password'</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment"># Set environment variables</span>
<span class="hljs-built_in">export</span> MYAPP_DB_URL=<span class="hljs-variable">$DB_URL</span>
<span class="hljs-built_in">export</span> MYAPP_DB_USERNAME=<span class="hljs-variable">$DB_USERNAME</span>
<span class="hljs-built_in">export</span> MYAPP_DB_PASSWORD=<span class="hljs-variable">$DB_PASSWORD</span>

<span class="hljs-comment"># Start Spring Boot application</span>
java -jar my-spring-boot-app.jar
</code></div></div></pre><p>This script assumes you have <code>jq</code> installed on your system, which is a lightweight and flexible command-line JSON processor. If you don't have <code>jq</code>, you can install it or use any other JSON processing tool of your choice.</p><p>Remember to replace <code>my-spring-boot-app.jar</code> with the actual name of your Spring Boot application JAR file.</p><p>Now, when you run the script, it will set the required environment variables, and your Spring Boot application should be able to use the database connection information from the <code>DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION</code> environment variable.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Is there any method to not use the shell scripts?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Yes, you can achieve this without using shell scripts by creating a custom <code>EnvironmentPostProcessor</code> that reads the <code>DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION</code> environment variable and sets the corresponding properties in your Spring Boot application. Here's how you can do it:</p><ol><li>Create a class named <code>DatabaseEnvironmentPostProcessor</code> that implements <code>org.springframework.boot.env.EnvironmentPostProcessor</code>. In the <code>postProcessEnvironment</code> method, read the <code>DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION</code> environment variable, parse the JSON string, and set the properties:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>java</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-java"><span class="hljs-keyword">package</span> com.example.myapp;

<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> org.springframework.boot.env.EnvironmentPostProcessor;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> org.springframework.core.env.ConfigurableEnvironment;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> org.springframework.core.env.MapPropertySource;

<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> java.io.IOException;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> java.util.HashMap;
<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> java.util.Map;

<span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">class</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">DatabaseEnvironmentPostProcessor</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">implements</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">EnvironmentPostProcessor</span> {

    <span class="hljs-meta">@Override</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">public</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">void</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">postProcessEnvironment</span><span class="hljs-params">(ConfigurableEnvironment environment, SpringApplication application)</span> {
        <span class="hljs-type">String</span> <span class="hljs-variable">databasesJson</span> <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> System.getenv(<span class="hljs-string">"DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION"</span>);
        <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (databasesJson != <span class="hljs-literal">null</span>) {
            <span class="hljs-keyword">try</span> {
                <span class="hljs-type">ObjectMapper</span> <span class="hljs-variable">objectMapper</span> <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">ObjectMapper</span>();
                <span class="hljs-type">JsonNode</span> <span class="hljs-variable">databasesNode</span> <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> objectMapper.readTree(databasesJson);
                <span class="hljs-type">JsonNode</span> <span class="hljs-variable">postgresNode</span> <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> databasesNode.get(<span class="hljs-string">"postgres"</span>);

                Map&lt;String, Object&gt; properties = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">HashMap</span>&lt;&gt;();
                properties.put(<span class="hljs-string">"spring.datasource.url"</span>, postgresNode.get(<span class="hljs-string">"composed"</span>).get(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>).asText());
                properties.put(<span class="hljs-string">"spring.datasource.username"</span>, postgresNode.get(<span class="hljs-string">"authentication"</span>).get(<span class="hljs-string">"username"</span>).asText());
                properties.put(<span class="hljs-string">"spring.datasource.password"</span>, postgresNode.get(<span class="hljs-string">"authentication"</span>).get(<span class="hljs-string">"password"</span>).asText());

                <span class="hljs-type">MapPropertySource</span> <span class="hljs-variable">propertySource</span> <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">MapPropertySource</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"databaseConnectionProperties"</span>, properties);
                environment.getPropertySources().addLast(propertySource);

            } <span class="hljs-keyword">catch</span> (IOException e) {
                <span class="hljs-keyword">throw</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">RuntimeException</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"Failed to parse DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION"</span>, e);
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>Register the <code>DatabaseEnvironmentPostProcessor</code> in the <code>spring.factories</code> file under the <code>src/main/resources/META-INF</code> folder. Create the folder and the file if they don't exist:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs">org.springframework.boot.env.EnvironmentPostProcessor=com.example.myapp.DatabaseEnvironmentPostProcessor
</code></div></div></pre><p>Make sure to replace <code>com.example.myapp</code> with your actual package name.</p><ol><li>Remove the placeholders for the URL, username, and password in your <code>application.yaml</code> file:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>yaml</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-yaml"><span class="hljs-attr">spring:</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">datasource:</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">driver-class-name:</span> <span class="hljs-string">org.postgresql.Driver</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>Now, when you run your Spring Boot application, the <code>DatabaseEnvironmentPostProcessor</code> will automatically read the <code>DATABASES_FOR_POSTGRESQL_CONNECTION</code> environment variable and set the required properties.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'I want you to identify security vulnerabilities in the following file:

import {
