out any access control checks. This can lead to IDOR vulnerabilities if an attacker is able to manipulate the <code>request_id</code> parameter and perform actions on requests they shouldn't have access to. To mitigate this, it's recommended to perform access control checks on the server-side and ensure that only authorized users can perform actions on requests they are assigned to.</p></li><li><p>Insecure Communication: The <code>DownloadDocument</code> function uses <code>window.open</code> to open the downloaded document in a new window. However, this method can be vulnerable to clickjacking attacks if the downloaded document contains sensitive information. To prevent this, it's recommended to use a more secure method such as the <code>download</code> attribute on an anchor element or the <code>Content-Disposition</code> header on the server-side response. Additionally, the communication between the client and server should be secured using HTTPS to prevent eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks.</p></li></ol></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Can you provide sources for that information?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Certainly! Here are some sources that provide more information on the security vulnerabilities I identified in the code:</p><ol><li>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS):</li></ol><ul><li>OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet: <a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/xss-prevention" target="_new">https://owasp.org/www-community/xss-prevention</a></li><li>DOMPurify: <a href="https://github.com/cure53/DOMPurify" target="_new">https://github.com/cure53/DOMPurify</a></li></ul><ol><li>Information Disclosure:</li></ol><ul><li>OWASP Access Control Cheat Sheet: <a href="https://owasp.org/www-project-cheat-sheets/cheatsheets/Access_Control_Cheat_Sheet.html" target="_new">https://owasp.org/www-project-cheat-sheets/cheatsheets/Access_Control_Cheat_Sheet.html</a></li></ul><ol><li>Lack of Input Validation:</li></ol><ul><li>OWASP Input Validation Cheat Sheet: <a