}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, the <code>css</code> function is used to create a CSS class with the custom font <code>My Custom Font</code>. This class is then applied to the <code>div</code> element using the <code>className</code> prop.</p><p>You can also use <code>emotion</code>'s <code>jsx</code> syntax to directly apply styles to your element.</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> { jsx } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'emotion'</span>;

