e> or not. This will help ensure that the data passed is safe to use and protects your application from XSS and other types of security vulnerabilities.</p><p>In conclusion, using <code>JSON.stringify()</code> inside of a template literal with structured data is a good practice, but it should be used in conjunction with other security measures, such as input validation, sanitization and filtering, to ensure that the structured data passed is safe to use and protects your application from XSS and other types of security vulnerabilities.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'What could happen if instead of setting the result of the stringifying the structured data inside the template literal inside the script tag. I instead use dangerouslySetInnerHTML with the value of the template literal?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>If you use <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code> with the value of the template literal instead of setting it directly inside the script tag, it could open up your application to security vulnerabilities such as cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.</p><p>By using <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>, you are telling React to insert the value of the template literal directly into the HTML, which means that any special characters or malicious code that are not properly escaped or sanitized could be executed as code.</p><p><code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code> is called that way because it's dangerous to use, it should only be used when you are sure that the data passed is safe, and that's why it's not recommended to use it with user input data.</p><p>It's important to remember that the use of <code>JSON.stringify()</code> and template literals alone is not enough to fully protect against XSS attacks. You should always validate, sanitize and escape user input data, regardless of whether you are using <code>JSON.stringify()</code> or not, to ensure that the data passed is safe to use and protects your application from XSS and other types of security vulnerabilities.</p></div>'}]

