 Reflective DLL injection, the DLL being injected contains a reflective loader. This reflective loader is responsible for loading the DLL into memory and then resolving and calling the DLL's entry point function, typically the DllMain() function.</p><p>The reflective loader performs this by first mapping the DLL into the target process's memory space, then resolving the DLL's imports and relocations, and finally calling the DllMain() function.</p><p>By using reflection, the reflective loader is able to bypass the DEP and ASLR security features. For example, DEP is bypassed because the reflective loader maps the DLL into the target process's memory space and calls the DllMain() function directly, rather than using the traditional DLL injection techniques that are blocked by DEP. Similarly, ASLR is bypassed because the reflective loader does not rely on the traditional DLL injection techniques that are affected by ASLR.</p><p>It's important to note that Reflective DLL injection is not a foolproof method and can be blocked by other security measures such as Anti-virus software, firewall or other endpoint protection software. And also Reflective DLL injection is a complex technique and it is not recommended to use it without a clear understanding of the implications and potential security risks. It is also considered as a malicious activity if done without proper authorization. It is important to always have a valid reason for using DLL injection and to ensure that you have the necessary permissions before proceeding.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Please provide complete adresses of websites mentioned in above replies of yours'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>I apologize, I should have provided the URLs for the websites that I mentioned earlier. Here are the URLs for the websites that I mentioned:</p><ol><li><p>"Windows Internals" book by Mark Russinovich, David Solomon and Alex Ionescu: 