what's the functionality of "external link"?

'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>The term "external link" in the given code is not a valid function or syntax in the C programming language. It appears to be an incomplete statement, and without additional context or code, it's impossible to determine its intended functionality.</p><p>However, assuming that the code was meant to include a valid function call or statement, and that "external link" was a placeholder for the actual function, one possible interpretation could be that the program is using the contents of <code>argv[1]</code> as a format string to print the value of <code>secret</code> to the standard output. This could be achieved using the <code>printf</code> function in C, which allows you to format and print data to the standard output stream.</p><p>For example, if the user runs the program with a command line argument of "%s", the program would print the value of <code>secret</code> to the console:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">kotlin</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-kotlin">$ ./program <span class="hljs-string">"%s"</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">this</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">is</span> a secrete!
</code></div></div></pre><p>However, it's important to note that the code, as it's currently written, is vulnerable to a format string vulnerability. If an attacker were to provide a carefully crafted format string as the command line argument, they could potentially read or write arbitrary memory locations, which could result in a security exploit. Therefore, in practice, it's important to ensure that user input is properly validated and sanitized before using it in a call to <code>printf</code> or any other function that accepts a format string.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'How external variables are correctly used in C program without failing compilation

'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>External variables, also known as global variables, can be used in C programs by declaring them outside of any function in the program. Once declared, these variables can be accessed and modified by any function in the program. Here's an example of how to declare an external variable in C:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">c</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-c"><span class="hljs-comment">// Declare the external variable outside of any function</span>
<span class="hljs-type">int</span> global_var;

<span class="hljs-type">int</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">main</span><span class="hljs-params">()</span> {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// Access and modify the global variable</span>
    global_var = <span class="hljs-number">42</span>;
    <span class="hljs-built_in">printf</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"The value of global_var is %d\n"</span>, global_var);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-number">0</span>;
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>If you need to use an external variable that is defined in another source file, you can declare it as <code>extern</code> in your file to inform the compiler that the variable is defined elsewhere. Here's an example of how to do this:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">c</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-c"><span class="hljs-comment">// Declare the external variable as "extern"</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">extern</span> <span class="hljs-type">int</span> global_var;

<span class="hljs-type">int</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">main</span><span class="hljs-params">()</span> {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// Access and modify the global variable</span>
    global_var = <span class="hljs-number">42</span>;
    <span class="hljs-built_in">printf</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"The value of global_var is %d\n"</span>, global_var);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-number">0</span>;
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>To make sure that external variables are used correctly and don't fail compilation, it's important to follow some best practices:</p><ol><li>Declare external variables at the top of the file, before any function definitions.</li><li>Use descriptive names for global variables to make their purpose clear.</li><li>Limit the use of global variables as much as possible, as they can make it difficult to reason about the behavior of your program.</li><li>If you need to share data between functions, consider using function parameters or return values instead of global variables.</li></ol><p>By following these best practices, you can use external variables in your C programs without running into issues during compilation.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'CVE description: 
Tensorflow is an Open Source Machine Learning Framework. An attacker can craft a TFLite model that would cause an integer overflow in `TfLiteIntArrayCreate`. The `TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes` returns an `int` instead of a `size_t. An attacker can control model inputs such that `computed_size` overflows the size of `int` datatype. The fix will be included in TensorFlow 2.8.0. We will also cherrypick this commit on TensorFlow 2.7.1, TensorFlow 2.6.3, and TensorFlow 2.5.3, as these are also affected and still in supported range.

   :
TfLiteIntArray* TfLiteIntArrayCreate(int size) {
  int alloc_size = TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes(size);
  if (alloc_size &lt;= 0) return NULL;
  TfLiteIntArray* ret = (TfLiteIntArray*)malloc(alloc_size);
  if (!ret) return ret;
  ret-&gt;size = size;
  return ret;
}
int TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes(int size) {
  static TfLiteIntArray dummy;

  int computed_size = sizeof(dummy) + sizeof(dummy.data[0]) * size;
#if defined(_MSC_VER)
  // Context for why this is needed is in http://b/189926408#comment21
  computed_size -= sizeof(dummy.data[0]);
#endif
  return computed_size;
}

      '},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>  TensorFlow TFLite    . TFLite     <code>TfLiteIntArrayCreate</code>  .</p><p><code>TfLiteIntArrayCreate</code>  <code>TfLiteIntArray</code>   .  <code>TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes</code>    <code>int</code>  ,     <code>computed_size</code>  <code>int</code>    ,       .</p><p>  TensorFlow 2.5.3, 2.6.3, 2.7.1  2.8.0  , TensorFlow 2.8.0 .   TensorFlow 2.7.1, 2.6.3  2.5.3 .</p><p>   <code>TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes</code>     <code>size_t</code>     .    .</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>c</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-c"><span class="hljs-type">size_t</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes</span><span class="hljs-params">(<span class="hljs-type">int</span> size)</span> {
  <span class="hljs-type">static</span> TfLiteIntArray dummy;

  <span class="hljs-type">size_t</span> computed_size = <span class="hljs-keyword">sizeof</span>(dummy) + <span class="hljs-keyword">sizeof</span>(dummy.data[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>]) * size;
<span class="hljs-meta">#<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> defined(_MSC_VER)</span>
  <span class="hljs-comment">// Context for why this is needed is in http://b/189926408#comment21</span>
