</code></div></div></pre><p>The updated implementation directly checks whether <code>this</code> and <code>o</code> are the same object and whether they are of the same class, and then performs the necessary equality checks. It also provides a properly implemented <code>hashCode()</code> method, which is necessary for consistent behavior in hash-based data structures like <code>HashMap</code>.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'api authentication in nextjs 13
Post: https://simple-books-api.glitch.me/api-clients/
{
   "clientName": "Postman",
   "clientEmail": "sajid@example.com"
}

{
    "accessToken": "918df582b1998acbd2cb32c66b2965ef19a8dc596f24592aba04bb598d8f3637"
}'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To authenticate API requests in Next.js 13, you can make use of the built-in <code>fetch</code> function provided by the browser to make HTTP requests to your API.</p><p>First, you need to store the <code>accessToken</code> that you received from your API server. You can store it in a cookie or in local storage depending on your use case.</p><p>Then, when making an API request, you can include the <code>accessToken</code> in the <code>Authorization</code> header of your HTTP request. Here's an example:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>js</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-js"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> accessToken = <span class="hljs-comment">// retrieve the access token from storage</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> headers = {
  <span class="hljs-string">'Content-Type'</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'application/json'</span>,
  <span class="hljs-string">'Authorization'</span>: <span class="hljs-string">`Bearer <span class="hljs-subst">${accessToken}</span>`</span>
}

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> response = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">fetch</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'https://simple-books-api.glitch.me/api-clients/'</span>, {
  <span class="hljs-attr">method</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'POST'</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">headers</span>: headers,
  <span class="hljs-attr">body</span>: <span class="hljs-title class_">JSON</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">stringify</span>({
    <span class="hljs-attr">clientName</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'Postman'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-attr">clientEmail</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'sajid@example.com'</span>
  })
})

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> data = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> response.<span class="hljs-title function_">json</span>()

<span class="hljs-comment">// process the response data here</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we're including the <code>accessToken</code> in the <code>Authorization</code> header using the <code>Bearer</code> authentication scheme. This is a common convention for token-based authentication.</p><p>Note that you should always ensure that the <code>accessToken</code> is sent securely over HTTPS to prevent eavesdropping and man-in-the-middle attacks.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'I changed the name of my next-auth cookies along with some other config and now by login is broken. 

  cookies: {
    sessionToken: {
      name: "your-custom-session-token-name",
      options: {
        httpOnly: true,
        sameSite: "none",
        path: "/",
        secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production",
      },
    },
    csrfToken: {
      name: "your-custom-csrf-token-name",
      options: {
        httpOnly: false,
        sameSite: "none",
        path: "/",
        secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production",
      },
    },
  },'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Changing the name of your cookies can certainly break your login functionality. Here are a few things you can try:</p><ol><li><p>Check your server-side code to ensure that it's also using the new cookie names. For example, if you're using a session store like MongoDB or Redis, you'll need to update the cookie names in your server-side code as well.</p></li><li><p>Check your client-side code to ensure that it's also using the new cookie names. Make sure that any requests you make to the server include the new cookie names, and that any cookies you set on the client-side use the new names.</p></li><li><p>Check your browser's cookie storage to ensure that the old cookies have been removed and the new cookies have been set. You can do this by inspecting the cookies in your browser's developer console.</p></li><li><p>Make sure that your server is returning the new cookies with the correct name, domain, path, and other attributes. You can use a tool like Postman or cURL to inspect the cookies returned by your server.</p></li></ol><p>If none of these steps help, you may want to revert the changes you made and start over with a fresh configuration. When changing cookie names, it's important to make sure that all components of your application are updated to use the new names.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'My session and CSRF token data are saved in a script referenced by my popup.html file. But my request is being sent from a content script. How can my content script get data from popup.js, referenced in popup.html'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To share data between a content script and a popup script, you can use the <code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage</code> method. Here's an example of how you can use this method:</p><ol><li>In your <code>popup.js</code> script, you can use the <code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage</code> method to send a message to the background script with the session and CSRF token data:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>php</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-php">chrome.runtime.<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">sendMessage</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">sessionToken</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'your-session-token'</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">csrfToken</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'your-csrf-token'</span> });
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>In your <code>background.js</code> script, you can listen for this message using the <code>chrome.runtime.onMessage</code> method:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript">chrome.<span class="hljs-property">runtime</span>.<span class="hljs-property">onMessage</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">addListener</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">request, sender, sendResponse</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (request.<span class="hljs-property">sessionToken</span> &amp;&amp; request.<span class="hljs-property">csrfToken</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// Save the session and CSRF token data in a variable or storage.</span>
  }
});
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>In your content script, you can use the <code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage</code> method to request the session and CSRF token data from the background script:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript">chrome.<span class="hljs-property">runtime</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">sendMessage</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">action</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'getSessionAndCSRFTokenData'</span> }, <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">response</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-comment">// Use the session and CSRF token data returned by the background script.</span>
});
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>In your <code>background.js</code> script, you can handle this message and return the session and CSRF token data:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript">chrome.<span class="hljs-property">runtime</span>.<span class="hljs-property">onMessage</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">addListener</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">request, sender, sendResponse</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (request.<span class="hljs-property">action</span> === <span class="hljs-string">'getSessionAndCSRFTokenData'</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> sessionToken = <span class="hljs-comment">// Get the session token data from storage or a variable.</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> csrfToken = <span class="hljs-comment">// Get the CSRF token data from storage or a variable.</span>
    <span class="hljs-title function_">sendResponse</span>({ sessionToken, csrfToken });
  }
});
</code></div></div></pre><p>This way, your content script can request the session and CSRF token data from the background script, which can get the data from the popup script and return it to the content script.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'I recently learned about the nullish coalescing operator in JS. Is there an equivalent to this in other languages? '},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Yes, there are equivalent operators to the nullish coalescing operator in other programming languages. Here are a few examples:</p><ol><li><p>C#: The null-coalescing operator <code>??</code> is used to return the value of its left-hand operand if it is not null, otherwise it returns the value of its right-hand operand.</p></li><li><p>Swift: The nil-coalescing operator <code>??</code> is used to return the value of its left-hand operand if it is not nil, otherwise it returns the value of its right-hand operand.</p></li><li><p>Kotlin: The null-coalescing operator <code>?:</code> is used to return the value of its left-hand operand if it is not null, otherwise it returns the value of its right-hand operand.</p></li><li><p>Rust: The <code>unwrap_or</code> method on an <code>Option</code> object is similar to the nullish coalescing operator. It returns the value of the <code>Option</code> if it is <code>Some</code>, otherwise it returns the value passed to the method.</p></li></ol><p>These are just a few examples, but many programming languages have similar operators or methods for achieving the same functionality as the nullish coalescing operator in JavaScript.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Is there an equivalent in GO?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Yes, in Go programming language, there is an equivalent operator to the nullish coalescing operator in JavaScript called the <code>nil coalescing operator</code>.</p><p>The <code>nil coalescing operator</code> is represented by two vertical bars <code>||</code> and it is used to return the value of its left-hand operand if it is not <code>nil</code>, otherwise it returns the value of its right-hand operand. Here is an example:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">go</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-go"><span class="hljs-comment">// Using the nil coalescing operator</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> name *<span class="hljs-type">string</span> = <span class="hljs-literal">nil</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> defaultName <span class="hljs-type">string</span> = <span class="hljs-string">"John Doe"</span>

result := name || defaultName
fmt.Println(result) <span class="hljs-comment">// Output: John Doe</span>

name = <span class="hljs-built_in">new</span>(<span class="hljs-type">string</span>)
*name = <span class="hljs-string">"Jane Doe"</span>

result = name || defaultName
fmt.Println(result) <span class="hljs-comment">// Output: Jane Doe</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>In the example above, the <code>||</code> operator checks whether the <code>name</code> variable is <code>nil</code>. Since it is <code>nil</code> in the first case, the operator returns the value of the <code>defaultName</code> variable. In the second case, the <code>name</code> variable is assigned a value, so the operator returns the value of the <code>name</code> variable.</p><p>Note that the <code>||</code> operator only returns the value of the right-hand operand if the left-hand operand is <code>nil</code>. If the left-hand operand has a zero value (e.g. <code>0</code>, <code>false</code>, <code>""</code>, or <code>[]</code>), the operator will return the zero value instead of the right-hand operand.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Can you write an example of this that will be a React component? It will be taking in user data from a fetch request, and an assigning user.email (if it exists) to displayName, which will be rendered as the first row of a column about user data. You can decide what other data to put in the column. '},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Sure! Here's an example of a React component that takes in user data from a fetch request, assigns the <code>user.email</code> to <code>displayName</code> if it exists, and renders it as the first row of a column about user data:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">jsx</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-jsx"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">React</span>, { useState, useEffect } <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">"react"</span>;

<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">UserData</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> [user, setUser] = <span class="hljs-title function_">useState</span>({});
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> [displayName, setDisplayName] = <span class="hljs-title function_">useState</span>(<span class="hljs-string">""</span>);

  <span class="hljs-title function_">useEffect</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-title function_">fetch</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"https://api.example.com/user-data"</span>)
      .<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">response</span>) =&gt;</span> response.<span class="hljs-title function_">json</span>())
      .<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">data</span>) =&gt;</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">setUser</span>(data));
  }, []);

  <span class="hljs-title function_">useEffect</span>(<span class="hljs-function">() =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (user.<span class="hljs-property">email</span>) {
      <span class="hljs-title function_">setDisplayName</span>(user.<span class="hljs-property">email</span>);
    } <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
      <span class="hljs-title function_">setDisplayName</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"No email provided"</span>);
    }
  }, [user.<span class="hljs-property">email</span>]);

  <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> (
    <span class="xml"><span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">div</span>&gt;</span>
      <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">h2</span>&gt;</span>User Data<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">h2</span>&gt;</span>
      <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">table</span>&gt;</span>
        <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">tbody</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">tr</span>&gt;</span>
            <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>Display Name:<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>
            <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>{displayName}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">tr</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">tr</span>&gt;</span>
            <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>User ID:<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>
            <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>{user.id}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">tr</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">tr</span>&gt;</span>
            <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>First Name:<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>
            <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>{user.firstName || "N/A"}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">tr</span>&gt;</span>
          <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">tr</span>&gt;</span>
            <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>Last Name:<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>
            <span class="hljs-tag">&lt;<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>{user.lastName || "N/A"}<span class="hljs-tag">&lt;/<span class="hljs-name">td</span>&gt;</span>
