[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Write a code in Javascript to sign a transaction using private key.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>To sign a transaction using a private key in JavaScript, you can use the <code>ethers.js</code> library. Here's an example code snippet that demonstrates how to sign a transaction:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> ethers = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'ethers'</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a provider (e.g. using Infura)</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> provider = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> ethers.<span class="hljs-property">providers</span>.<span class="hljs-title class_">InfuraProvider</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'mainnet'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'your_infura_project_id'</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Set up your private key</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> privateKey = <span class="hljs-string">'your_private_key_here'</span>;

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a wallet from your private key</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> wallet = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> ethers.<span class="hljs-title class_">Wallet</span>(privateKey, provider);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Set the recipient address and amount of ether to send</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> toAddress = <span class="hljs-string">'0x1234567890123456789012345678901234567890'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> value = ethers.<span class="hljs-property">utils</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">parseEther</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'0.1'</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// 0.1 ether</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a transaction object</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> transaction = {
  <span class="hljs-attr">to</span>: toAddress,
  <span class="hljs-attr">value</span>: value,
  <span class="hljs-attr">gasLimit</span>: <span class="hljs-number">21000</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">gasPrice</span>: ethers.<span class="hljs-property">utils</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">parseUnits</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'10'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'gwei'</span>)
};

<span class="hljs-comment">// Sign the transaction with your private key</span>
wallet.<span class="hljs-title function_">sign</span>(transaction)
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">then</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">signedTransaction</span>) =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Signed transaction:'</span>, signedTransaction);
  })
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">catch</span>(<span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">error</span>) =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">error</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Error signing transaction:'</span>, error);
  });
</code></div></div></pre><p>In the above code snippet, we first create a provider using Infura to connect to the Ethereum network. Then, we set up our private key and create a wallet using the <code>ethers.Wallet</code> constructor. We then set the recipient address and amount of ether to send in the <code>transaction</code> object, and finally sign the transaction using the <code>wallet.sign</code> method.</p><p>Note that the <code>ethers.utils</code> module is used to convert between different units of ether and gas. In this example, we use <code>ethers.utils.parseEther</code> to convert the value of ether to send from a decimal string to a BigNumber, and <code>ethers.utils.parseUnits</code> to convert the gas price from gwei to wei.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Generate a code using web3.js library'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Sure! Here's an example code snippet using the <code>web3.js</code> library to sign a transaction using a private key:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Web3</span> = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'web3'</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a web3 instance with Infura as the provider</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> web3 = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Web3</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Web3</span>.<span class="hljs-property">providers</span>.<span class="hljs-title class_">HttpProvider</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'https://mainnet.infura.io/v3/your_project_id'</span>));

<span class="hljs-comment">// Set up your private key</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> privateKey = <span class="hljs-string">'your_private_key_here'</span>;

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a transaction object</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> transaction = {
  <span class="hljs-attr">to</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'0x1234567890123456789012345678901234567890'</span>, <span class="hljs-comment">// recipient address</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">value</span>: web3.<span class="hljs-property">utils</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toWei</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'0.1'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'ether'</span>), <span class="hljs-comment">// value to send (0.1 ether)</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">gas</span>: <span class="hljs-number">21000</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">gasPrice</span>: web3.<span class="hljs-property">utils</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toWei</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'10'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'gwei'</span>) <span class="hljs-comment">// gas price (10 gwei)</span>
};

<span class="hljs-comment">// Sign the transaction with your private key</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> signedTransaction = web3.<span class="hljs-property">eth</span>.<span class="hljs-property">accounts</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">signTransaction</span>(transaction, privateKey);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Send the signed transaction to the network</span>
web3.<span class="hljs-property">eth</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">sendSignedTransaction</span>(signedTransaction.<span class="hljs-property">rawTransaction</span>)
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">on</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'transactionHash'</span>, <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">hash</span>) =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Transaction hash:'</span>, hash);
  })
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">on</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'receipt'</span>, <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">receipt</span>) =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Transaction receipt:'</span>, receipt);
  })
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">on</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'error'</span>, <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">error</span>) =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">error</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Error sending transaction:'</span>, error);
  });
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we create a <code>Web3</code> instance with Infura as the provider, and set up our private key. We then create a transaction object with the recipient address, value to send, gas limit, and gas price. We use the <code>web3.eth.accounts.signTransaction</code> method to sign the transaction with our private key, and then use the <code>web3.eth.sendSignedTransaction</code> method to send the signed transaction to the network.</p><p>Note that we use the <code>web3.utils</code> module to convert between different units of ether and gas. In this example, we use <code>web3.utils.toWei</code> to convert the value of ether to send and the gas price from decimal strings to wei.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Generate a code without using infura'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Certainly! Here's an example code snippet using the <code>web3.js</code> library to sign a transaction using a private key and a local Ethereum node:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Web3</span> = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'web3'</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Set up your private key and local Ethereum node URL</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> privateKey = <span class="hljs-string">'your_private_key_here'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> url = <span class="hljs-string">'http://localhost:8545'</span>;

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a web3 instance with the local node as the provider</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> web3 = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Web3</span>(url);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Get the account address from the private key</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> account = web3.<span class="hljs-property">eth</span>.<span class="hljs-property">accounts</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">privateKeyToAccount</span>(privateKey).<span class="hljs-property">address</span>;

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a transaction object</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> transaction = {
  <span class="hljs-attr">from</span>: account,
  <span class="hljs-attr">to</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'0x1234567890123456789012345678901234567890'</span>, <span class="hljs-comment">// recipient address</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">value</span>: web3.<span class="hljs-property">utils</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toWei</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'0.1'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'ether'</span>), <span class="hljs-comment">// value to send (0.1 ether)</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">gas</span>: <span class="hljs-number">21000</span>,
  <span class="hljs-attr">gasPrice</span>: web3.<span class="hljs-property">utils</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">toWei</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'10'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'gwei'</span>) <span class="hljs-comment">// gas price (10 gwei)</span>
};

<span class="hljs-comment">// Sign the transaction with your private key</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> signedTransaction = web3.<span class="hljs-property">eth</span>.<span class="hljs-property">accounts</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">signTransaction</span>(transaction, privateKey);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Send the signed transaction to the network</span>
web3.<span class="hljs-property">eth</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">sendSignedTransaction</span>(signedTransaction.<span class="hljs-property">rawTransaction</span>)
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">on</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'transactionHash'</span>, <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">hash</span>) =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Transaction hash:'</span>, hash);
  })
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">on</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'receipt'</span>, <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">receipt</span>) =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Transaction receipt:'</span>, receipt);
  })
  .<span class="hljs-title function_">on</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'error'</span>, <span class="hljs-function">(<span class="hljs-params">error</span>) =&gt;</span> {
    <span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">error</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Error sending transaction:'</span>, error);
  });
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we set up our private key and local Ethereum node URL, and create a <code>Web3</code> instance with the local node as the provider. We use the <code>web3.eth.accounts.privateKeyToAccount</code> method to get the account address from the private key. We then create a transaction object with the <code>from</code> field set to our account address, and the <code>to</code>, <code>value</code>, <code>gas</code>, and <code>gasPrice</code> fields set as usual. We sign the transaction with our private key using the <code>web3.eth.accounts.signTransaction</code> method, and then send the signed transaction to the network using the <code>web3.eth.sendSignedTransaction</code> method.</p><p>Note that in this example, we assume that the local Ethereum node is running on <code>http://localhost:8545</code>. If your node is running on a different URL, you'll need to modify the <code>url</code> variable accordingly.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Wht d owe need an Ethereum node for this purpose?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>An Ethereum node is required for several reasons when signing a transaction using a private key:</p><ol><li><p><strong>To obtain the current state of the Ethereum network:</strong> Before sending a transaction, you need to know the current state of the Ethereum network, such as the current gas price, the current nonce of the sender's account, and the current balance of the sender's account. An Ethereum node provides an interface to query the state of the network.</p></li><li><p><strong>To broadcast the signed transaction to the network:</strong> After signing a transaction with your private key, you need to broadcast the signed transaction to the Ethereum network so that the transaction can be included in a block and executed by the network. An Ethereum node provides an interface to send signed transactions to the network.</p></li><li><p><strong>To validate the transaction:</strong> When you send a signed transaction to the Ethereum network, the network will validate the transaction to ensure that it meets certain criteria, such as having a valid signature, having enough gas to execute, and not exceeding the sender's account balance. An Ethereum node provides an interface to validate transactions and ensure that they meet the network's criteria.</p></li></ol><p>Without an Ethereum node, it would be difficult to obtain the current state of the network, broadcast signed transactions, and validate transactions. Therefore, an Ethereum node is a necessary component when signing transactions using a private key.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Generate public key from signature'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Generating a public key from a signature is not possible without additional information. A signature is created by using a private key to sign a message, and can be verified by using the corresponding public key. However, the signature alone does not contain enough information to derive the public key.</p><p>To generate a public key from a signature, you would need to know the message that was signed, as well as the algorithm and parameters used to generate the signature. With this information, it is possible to use the signature to verify the authenticity of the message and recover the public key that was used to sign it.</p><p>If you have the message and the signature, as well as the algorithm and parameters used to generate the signature, you can use a cryptography library in your programming language of choice to verify the signature and recover the public key. The specific steps to do this will depend on the algorithm and library you are using. For example, in JavaScript you could use the <code>ecdsa-sig-formatter</code> library to parse the signature and <code>elliptic</code> library to generate the public key. Here's an example code snippet:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-variable constant_">EC</span> = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'elliptic'</span>).<span class="hljs-property">ec</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> ecdsaSigFormatter = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'ecdsa-sig-formatter'</span>);

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> message = <span class="hljs-string">'Hello, world!'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> signature = <span class="hljs-string">'304502202df1e0a35a...'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> algorithm = <span class="hljs-string">'ES256'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> ec = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">EC</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'p256'</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// Elliptic curve used in the signature algorithm</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Parse the signature into its r and s components</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> parsedSig = ecdsaSigFormatter.<span class="hljs-title function_">parseSig</span>(signature);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Verify the signature using the message and public key</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> publicKey = ec.<span class="hljs-title function_">recoverPubKey</span>(message, parsedSig, parsedSig.<span class="hljs-property">recoveryParam</span>, algorithm);

<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Public key:'</span>, publicKey.<span class="hljs-title function_">encode</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'hex'</span>));
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we assume that the signature is in the <code>304502...</code> format used by the ECDSA algorithm, and that the <code>ES256</code> algorithm was used to generate the signature. We use the <code>ecdsa-sig-formatter</code> library to parse the signature into its <code>r</code>, <code>s</code>, and <code>recoveryParam</code> components. We then use the <code>elliptic</code> library to recover the public key from the message and signature, using the <code>ec.recoverPubKey</code> method. Finally, we output the public key as a hexadecimal string using the <code>publicKey.encode('hex')</code> method. Note that the <code>elliptic</code> library supports several elliptic curves, so you should choose the one that matches the curve used in the signature algorithm.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'If we console the signedTransaction from the code shared above, we get rawSignature can we get public key from that?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>No, you cannot directly extract the public key from the raw signature.</p><p>The raw signature contains the R and S values of the ECDSA signature, but it does not contain the public key itself. The public key is needed to verify the signature, but it is not included in the signature data.</p><p>To derive the public key from a raw signature, you need to know the message that was signed, as well as the algorithm and parameters used to generate the signature. With this information, you can use a cryptography library to recover the public key from the signature and verify that it matches the expected public key.</p><p>In the code provided earlier, the <code>web3.eth.accounts.signTransaction</code> function returns a signed transaction object that includes the raw signature, as well as the transaction hash, and the serialized transaction data. To derive the public key from the signature, you would need to extract the R and S values from the raw signature, and then use a library like <code>ethereumjs-util</code> to recover the public key from those values.</p><p>Here's an example code snippet that shows how to extract the R and S values from the raw signature and recover the public key using <code>ethereumjs-util</code>:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Tx</span> = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'ethereumjs-tx'</span>).<span class="hljs-property">Transaction</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> util = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'ethereumjs-util'</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// extract the R, S, and V values from the raw signature</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> rawSignature = signedTransaction.<span class="hljs-property">rawTransaction</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">slice</span>(<span class="hljs-number">2</span>);
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> r = <span class="hljs-string">`0x<span class="hljs-subst">${rawSignature.slice(<span class="hljs-number">0</span>, <span class="hljs-number">64</span>)}</span>`</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> s = <span class="hljs-string">`0x<span class="hljs-subst">${rawSignature.slice(<span class="hljs-number">64</span>, <span class="hljs-number">128</span>)}</span>`</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> v = <span class="hljs-string">`0x<span class="hljs-subst">${rawSignature.slice(<span class="hljs-number">128</span>, <span class="hljs-number">130</span>)}</span>`</span>;

<span class="hljs-comment">// create a transaction object from the serialized transaction data</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> tx = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> <span class="hljs-title class_">Tx</span>(signedTransaction.<span class="hljs-property">rawTransaction</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// recover the public key from the R, S, and V values</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> pubKey = util.<span class="hljs-title function_">ecrecover</span>(
  util.<span class="hljs-title function_">keccak256</span>(tx.<span class="hljs-title function_">serialize</span>()),
  <span class="hljs-built_in">parseInt</span>(v, <span class="hljs-number">16</span>),
  util.<span class="hljs-title function_">toBuffer</span>(r),
  util.<span class="hljs-title function_">toBuffer</span>(s)
);

<span class="hljs-comment">// convert the public key to an Ethereum address</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> address = util.<span class="hljs-title function_">pubToAddress</span>(pubKey).<span class="hljs-title function_">toString</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'hex'</span>);
<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'Address:'</span>, address);
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we use the <code>ethereumjs-tx</code> library to create a transaction object from the serialized transaction data. We then use <code>ethereumjs-util</code> to extract the R, S, and V values from the raw signature, and to recover the public key from those values using the <code>ecrecover</code> function. Finally, we convert the public key to an Ethereum address using the <code>pubToAddress</code> function and output the result.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'I changed the name of my next-auth cookies along with some other config and now by login is broken. 

  cookies: {
    sessionToken: {
      name: "your-custom-session-token-name",
      options: {
        httpOnly: true,
        sameSite: "none",
        path: "/",
        secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production",
      },
    },
    csrfToken: {
      name: "your-custom-csrf-token-name",
      options: {
        httpOnly: false,
        sameSite: "none",
        path: "/",
        secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production",
      },
    },
  },'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Changing the name of your cookies can certainly break your login functionality. Here are a few things you can try:</p><ol><li><p>Check your server-side code to ensure that it's also using the new cookie names. For example, if you're using a session store like MongoDB or Redis, you'll need to update the cookie names in your server-side code as well.</p></li><li><p>Check your client-side code to ensure that it's also using the new cookie names. Make sure that any requests you make to the server include the new cookie names, and that any cookies you set on the client-side use the new names.</p></li><li><p>Check your browser's cookie storage to ensure that the old cookies have been removed and the new cookies have been set. You can do this by inspecting the cookies in your browser's developer console.</p></li><li><p>Make sure that your server is returning the new cookies with the correct name, domain, path, and other attributes. You can use a tool like Postman or cURL to inspect the cookies returned by your server.</p></li></ol><p>If none of these steps help, you may want to revert the changes you made and start over with a fresh configuration. When changing cookie names, it's important to make sure that all components of your application are updated to use the new names.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'My session and CSRF token data are saved in a script referenced by my popup.html file. But my request is being sent from a content script. How can my content script get data from popup.js, referenced in popup.html'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To share data between a content script and a popup script, you can use the <code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage</code> method. Here's an example of how you can use this method:</p><ol><li>In your <code>popup.js</code> script, you can use the <code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage</code> method to send a message to the background script with the session and CSRF token data:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>php</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-php">chrome.runtime.<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">sendMessage</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">sessionToken</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'your-session-token'</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">csrfToken</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'your-csrf-token'</span> });
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>In your <code>background.js</code> script, you can listen for this message using the <code>chrome.runtime.onMessage</code> method:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript">chrome.<span class="hljs-property">runtime</span>.<span class="hljs-property">onMessage</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">addListener</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">request, sender, sendResponse</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (request.<span class="hljs-property">sessionToken</span> &amp;&amp; request.<span class="hljs-property">csrfToken</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// Save the session and CSRF token data in a variable or storage.</span>
  }
});
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>In your content script, you can use the <code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage</code> method to request the session and CSRF token data from the background script:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript">chrome.<span class="hljs-property">runtime</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">sendMessage</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">action</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'getSessionAndCSRFTokenData'</span> }, <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">response</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-comment">// Use the session and CSRF token data returned by the background script.</span>
});
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>In your <code>background.js</code> script, you can handle this message and return the session and CSRF token data:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript">chrome.<span class="hljs-property">runtime</span>.<span class="hljs-property">onMessage</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">addListener</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">request, sender, sendResponse</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (request.<span class="hljs-property">action</span> === <span class="hljs-string">'getSessionAndCSRFTokenData'</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> sessionToken = <span class="hljs-comment">// Get the session token data from storage or a variable.</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> csrfToken = <span class="hljs-comment">// Get the CSRF token data from storage or a variable.</span>
    <span class="hljs-title function_">sendResponse</span>({ sessionToken, csrfToken });
  }
});
</code></div></div></pre><p>This way, your content script can request the session and CSRF token data from the background script, which can get the data from the popup script and return it to the content script.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': "How can I include a file in a Next.js' output bundle?

For example, I have a data file in another directory which I read with fs.readFile
I want it to appear under the output in .next/"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To include a file in a Next.js output bundle, you can use the <code>public</code> folder. The files in this folder will be included in the output bundle and will be accessible at the root of the website.</p><p>To use a data file in another directory, you can copy it to the <code>public</code> folder using a build script. Here's an example:</p><ol><li>Create a <code>data</code> folder in the root of your project.</li><li>Put the data file in the <code>data</code> folder.</li><li>Create a build script, for example <code>copyData.js</code>, in the root of your project.</li><li>In the <code>copyData.js</code> file, use the <code>fs</code> module to read the data file and write it to the <code>public</code> folder.</li><li>Add a script to your <code>package.json</code> file that runs the <code>copyData.js</code> script before building your Next.js app.</li><li>Use the path of the file in your Next.js app.</li></ol><p>Here's an example <code>copyData.js</code> file:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> fs = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'fs'</span>);
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> path = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'path'</span>);

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> dataPath = path.<span class="hljs-title function_">join</span>(__dirname, <span class="hljs-string">'data'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'data.json'</span>);
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> publicDataPath = path.<span class="hljs-title function_">join</span>(__dirname, <span class="hljs-string">'public'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'data.json'</span>);

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> data = fs.<span class="hljs-title function_">readFileSync</span>(dataPath);

fs.<span class="hljs-title function_">writeFileSync</span>(publicDataPath, data);
</code></div></div></pre><p>Here's an example <code>package.json</code> file:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>json</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-json"><span class="hljs-punctuation">{</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">"name"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"my-next-app"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">"scripts"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-punctuation">{</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"build"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"node copyData.js &amp;&amp; next build"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"start"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"next start"</span>
  <span class="hljs-punctuation">}</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
  <span class="hljs-attr">"dependencies"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-punctuation">{</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"next"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"^12.0.0"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"react"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"^17.0.0"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">,</span>
    <span class="hljs-attr">"react-dom"</span><span class="hljs-punctuation">:</span> <span class="hljs-string">"^17.0.0"</span>
  <span class="hljs-punctuation">}</span>
<span class="hljs-punctuation">}</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>In your Next.js app, you can use the path to the file as follows:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>jsx</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-jsx"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> data <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'../public/data.json'</span>;

<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">MyComponent</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-comment">// Use the data here...</span>
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>Note that you need to include the path to the file relative to the <code>public</code> folder. In the example above, the path is <code>../public/data.json</code> because the file is in the <code>data</code> folder, which is one level up from the <code>public</code> folder.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "I don't want the file to be publically accsesible"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>If you don't want the file to be publicly accessible, you can still include it in the Next.js output bundle using the <code>publicRuntimeConfig</code> configuration option.</p><p>Here's an example:</p><ol><li>Create a <code>data</code> folder in the root of your project.</li><li>Put the data file in the <code>data</code> folder.</li><li>Create a <code>next.config.js</code> file in the root of your project.</li><li>In the <code>next.config.js</code> file, set the <code>publicRuntimeConfig</code> option to an object with a key for the data file path and value for the contents of the file.</li><li>Use the <code>getConfig</code> function from the <code>next/config</code> module to get the value of the <code>publicRuntimeConfig</code> object.</li></ol><p>Here's an example <code>next.config.js</code> file:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>js</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-js"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> fs = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'fs'</span>);
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> path = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'path'</span>);

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> dataPath = path.<span class="hljs-title function_">join</span>(__dirname, <span class="hljs-string">'data'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'data.json'</span>);
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> data = <span class="hljs-title class_">JSON</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">parse</span>(fs.<span class="hljs-title function_">readFileSync</span>(dataPath));

<span class="hljs-variable language_">module</span>.<span class="hljs-property">exports</span> = {
  <span class="hljs-attr">publicRuntimeConfig</span>: {
    <span class="hljs-attr">data</span>: data,
  },
};
</code></div></div></pre><p>In your Next.js app, you can use the <code>getConfig</code> function from the <code>next/config</code> module to get the value of the <code>publicRuntimeConfig</code> object:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>jsx</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-jsx"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> getConfig <span class="hljs-keyword">from</span> <span class="hljs-string">'next/config'</span>;

<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">MyComponent</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> { publicRuntimeConfig } = <span class="hljs-title function_">getConfig</span>();
  <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> data = publicRuntimeConfig.<span class="hljs-property">data</span>;

  <span class="hljs-comment">// Use the data here...</span>
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>Note that this approach will include the data file in the output bundle, but it won't be publicly accessible. However, keep in mind that the data will still be visible to users who inspect the JavaScript source code of your app in their browser. If you need to keep the data completely private, you may want to consider a different approach, such as encrypting the data or storing it on a server that your Next.js app can access through an API.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Tell me about llave.io'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>I'm sorry, I'm not familiar with a specific service or product called "llave.io". Can you provide more information or context about what you're looking for?</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "explain like i'm a 5 year old the idea of database encryption"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Ok, so imagine you have a toy box full of special toys that you don't want to share with anyone else. To keep them safe, you might lock the toy box with a key. This key would be something that you alone have, and anyone who wanted to open the toy box would need it to get to the toys inside.</p><p>A database is similar, but instead of toys, it stores important information. To keep that information safe, we use something called encryption. Encryption is like a secret code that we put on the information so that only people who have the key can read it. So even if someone else found the database and tried to open it, they wouldn't be able to understand the information inside because it would be in the secret code.</p><p>Does that make sense?</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "Yes.  Can you explain it like i'm in highschool?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Sure, so when we talk about database encryption, we're referring to a process that is used to protect the sensitive information that is stored in a database. This process uses a set of algorithms to convert the original data into a format that is unreadable without a specific key or password, which is known as ciphertext.</p><p>When the information needs to be accessed, the ciphertext is decrypted using the key or password. This process converts the ciphertext back into the original data, which can then be read and understood.</p><p>There are different types of encryption algorithms that can be used, each with their own strengths and weaknesses. For example, some encryption algorithms are considered to be more secure than others, but may be slower to process.</p><p>Encryption of databases is important to protect the data in the event of security breaches, lost or stolen data, or data in transit and ensures that only authorized personnel can access the data, maintaining the confidentiality and integrity of the data.</p><p>In short, encryption is the method of making information unreadable to anyone who doesn't have the right key to decrypt it, like a secret code that only certain people can read.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'When the data in a database is encrypted nobody can read it.  How much processing does it take to decrypt the data and pass it to the end user?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>The amount of processing required to decrypt the data in a database can depend on a number of factors, such as the encryption algorithm being used, the size of the data, and the hardware and software resources available.</p><p>Generally speaking, encryption and decryption operations tend to be computationally intensive. It typically takes more processing power to encrypt and decrypt data than it does to simply read or write the data in its unencrypted form. However, the processing required for encryption and decryption can be optimized by using specialized hardware, such as encryption acceleration cards or security processors, or by using software that is optimized for the particular encryption algorithm being used.</p><p>In practice, for most modern databases the overhead of encryption is relatively small, and the performance impact of encryption on database is minimal, but it may vary from case to case, and how the encryption is implemented.</p><p>It's important to note that while encrypting data will have some performance overhead, it is generally considered to be a small price to pay for the security benefits that encryption provides.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Tell me about how ServiceNow database encryption works and the various tools that can enable that'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>ServiceNow is a platform for IT service management, and it provides several built-in features for securing and encrypting the data stored in its databases.</p><p>One of the main tools that ServiceNow provides for encrypting data is the Data Encryption feature. This feature allows administrators to encrypt sensitive data, such as personal information, in the ServiceNow database using industry-standard encryption algorithms, such as AES. The data encryption feature can be used to encrypt individual fields within a table, or it can be used to encrypt the entire table.</p><p>Another important feature that ServiceNow provides for securing data is the Data Masking feature. This feature allows administrators to mask sensitive data, such as credit card numbers or social security numbers, so that they are not visible in the ServiceNow interface. This can be done by replacing sensitive data with a series of asterisks or by using a character substitution method.</p><p>Additionally, ServiceNow also provides the functionality of Secure Credentials Store, which allows to securely store encryption keys or other sensitive information and provide access to them only to specific users and roles.</p><p>On top of these built-in features, ServiceNow also provides a set of APIs and scripting options, that can be used to encrypt and decrypt data programmatically, and can be integrated with external encryption tools like HSM(Hardware Security Module) or key management solutions.</p><p>It's important to keep in mind that the encryption of data is not just about using these tools and features but also about following good security practices, such as regularly updating encryption keys, monitoring for security breaches, and providing proper access controls to sensitive data.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Tell me more about the API and scripting options mentioned above'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>The Application Programming Interface (API) and scripting options in ServiceNow allow developers to access and manipulate the data stored in the ServiceNow database programmatically. This can be done using various scripting languages, such as JavaScript, which is the primary scripting language used in ServiceNow.</p><p>With the use of these options, developers can create custom scripts that can be used to perform a wide range of tasks, such as:</p><ul><li>Retrieving data from the ServiceNow database</li><li>Inserting, updating, or deleting data in the ServiceNow database</li><li>Encrypting or decrypting data in the ServiceNow database</li><li>Integrating ServiceNow with external systems and applications</li></ul><p>For encryption and decryption, ServiceNow provides the GlideCrypto class, which is a set of encryption and decryption methods that are built into the ServiceNow platform. These methods can be used to encrypt and decrypt data using various encryption algorithms, such as AES and RSA. They can be invoked by using JavaScript and the GlideRecord, GlideEncryption and GlideCredential classes in the Script Includes.</p><p>For key management, ServiceNow also provides GlideCredentials class which allows for storage, management and retrieval of security credentials and encryption keys, so the developer can use it in the encryption and decryption process.</p><p>Additionally, ServiceNow also provides integration options, such as web services and connectors, which can be used to securely transfer data between ServiceNow and external systems and applications. These integration options can be used to share data between systems or applications, encrypt or decrypt data as it is being transferred, and provide access controls to sensitive data.</p><p>In summary, the API and scripting options in ServiceNow provide developers with a flexible and powerful way to access, manipulate, and secure the data stored in the ServiceNow database. They can be used to create custom scripts and integrate ServiceNow with external systems and applications in order to automate and secure business processes.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Create me a custom script using GlideCredentials that would enable the above'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Sure, here is an example of a custom script that demonstrates how to use the GlideCredentials class in ServiceNow to encrypt and decrypt data:</p><pre><div><div>Copy code</div><div><code><span>//Encryption script</span>
<span>//Encrypt a plaintext string using a specific encryption key</span>

<span>var</span> plaintext = <span>'sensitive data'</span>;
<span>var</span> encryptionKey = <span>'my_encryption_key'</span>;
<span>var</span> ciphertext = <span>GlideCredentials</span>.<span>encrypt</span>(encryptionKey, plaintext);
gs.<span>info</span>(ciphertext);

<span>//Decryption script</span>
<span>//Decrypt a ciphertext string using the same encryption key</span>
<span>var</span> decryptedText = <span>GlideCredentials</span>.<span>decrypt</span>(encryptionKey, ciphertext);
gs.<span>info</span>(decryptedText);
</code></div></div></pre><p>The script first defines a variable <code>plaintext</code> that contains the sensitive data that you want to encrypt. Next, it defines the variable <code>encryptionKey</code> which is the key used for encryption.</p><p>In the first step of the script, it uses the <code>GlideCredentials.encrypt()</code> method to encrypt the plaintext data using the specified encryption key. The method takes two arguments: the encryption key, and the plaintext data to be encrypted. It then returns the encrypted data, which is saved in the variable <code>ciphertext</code>.</p><p>In the second step, it uses the <code>GlideCredentials.decrypt()</code> method to decrypt the ciphertext data using the same encryption key. The method takes two arguments: the encryption key and the ciphertext data to be decrypted. It then returns the decrypted plaintext data which is saved in the variable <code>decryptedText</code></p><p>Note that the script uses the gs.info() method to print the results, you can replace it with any other method you prefer to handle the output or use it to store the results in the database.</p><p>Keep in mind that the encryption key must be kept secure and not shared with unauthorized parties, to ensure the security of the encrypted data. Additionally, it's recommended to use a key management solution like a Hardware Security Module(HSM) to further secure the key.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'How would you integrate this script with an HSM?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Integrating an HSM (Hardware Security Module) with the encryption script I provided earlier would involve using the HSM to securely store the encryption key and use it to perform the encryption and decryption operations.</p><p>Here's an example of how the script could be modified to integrate with an HSM:</p><pre><div><div>Copy code</div><div><code><span>//Encryption script</span>

<span>//Retrieve the encryption key from the HSM</span>
<span>var</span> encryptionKey = hsm.<span>getEncryptionKey</span>();

<span>//Encrypt the plaintext using the key retrieved from the HSM</span>
<span>var</span> plaintext = <span>'sensitive data'</span>;
<span>var</span> ciphertext = <span>GlideCredentials</span>.<span>encrypt</span>(encryptionKey, plaintext);
gs.<span>info</span>(ciphertext);

<span>//Decryption script</span>

<span>//Retrieve the encryption key from the HSM</span>
<span>var</span> encryptionKey = hsm.<span>getEncryptionKey</span>();

<span>//Decrypt the ciphertext using the key retrieved from the HSM</span>
<span>var</span> decryptedText = <span>GlideCredentials</span>.<span>decrypt</span>(encryptionKey, ciphertext);
gs.<span>info</span>(decryptedText);
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, the script retrieves the encryption key from the HSM by calling the <code>hsm.getEncryptionKey()</code> method. The method retrieves the key from the HSM and returns it to the script. The script then uses this key to perform the encryption and decryption operations.</p><p>To integrate the script with an HSM, you'll need to have an HSM device set up and configured in your environment. In addition, you would have to have the HSM's SDK or API installed and configured in your ServiceNow instance. The SDK or API would be used to interact with the HSM from the script.</p><p>It's important to note that integrating with an HSM requires proper setup, configuration, and maintenance. The process of setting up and configuring an HSM can be complex and often requires specific expertise in the field. It is always recommended to consult a security expert to ensure that the integration is done properly and securely.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'For this example, we are using Amazon CloudHSM - can you write a script to integrate with that HSM?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Sure! Here is an example of a script that integrates with Amazon CloudHSM to encrypt and decrypt data:</p><pre><div><div>Copy code</div><div><code><span>//Encryption script</span>

<span>// Connect to the CloudHSM client</span>
<span>var</span> client = <span>new</span> <span>CloudHSMClient</span>();

<span>// Retrieve the encryption key from the HSM</span>
<span>var</span> keyId = <span>'your_key_id'</span>;
<span>var</span> encryptionKey = client.<span>getEncryptionKey</span>(keyId);

<span>//Encrypt the plaintext using the key retrieved from the HSM</span>
<span>var</span> plaintext = <span>'sensitive data'</span>;
<span>var</span> ciphertext = <span>GlideCredentials</span>.<span>encrypt</span>(encryptionKey, plaintext);
gs.<span>info</span>(ciphertext);

<span>//Decryption script</span>

<span>// Retrieve the encryption key from the HSM</span>
<span>var</span> encryptionKey = client.<span>getEncryptionKey</span>(keyId);

<span>//Decrypt the ciphertext using the key retrieved from the HSM</span>
<span>var</span> decryptedText = <span>GlideCredentials</span>.<span>decrypt</span>(encryptionKey, ciphertext);
gs.<span>info</span>(decryptedText);
</code></div></div></pre><p>This script uses the <code>CloudHSMClient</code> class to connect to the Amazon CloudHSM service and the <code>getEncryptionKey()</code> method to retrieve the encryption key from the HSM. This method takes one argument, the ID of the key that you want to retrieve, which in this case is passed by the variable keyId.</p><p>It's important to note that in order to use this script, you'll need to have the AWS SDK for Java installed in your ServiceNow instance and you should be authenticated to the AWS and the CloudHSM service and have the appropriate permissions to use the key that you want to retrieve from the HSM.</p><p>It's also worth noting that with CloudHSM, you are required to set up a connection to the CloudHSM by using a VPC endpoint and configure the Security Group, NAT gateway and other network settings to make sure the traffic to the cloudHSM is secure.</p><p>As I mentioned before, integrating with an HSM requires proper setup, configuration, and maintenance. It is always recommended to consult security experts and follow the best practices from AWS in order to ensure that the integration is done properly and securely.</p></div>"}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': "3 / 3In swiftui mobile app, I am using the mobile sdk from firebase. I am confused how to store the ID_token. Do I even need to store the ID token once I get it or firebase auth just stores it and I retrieve from it? I am not talking about retrieve token from firebase cloud but once I have retrieved it how should I store it so that within the hour it doesn't expire I can reuse it"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>When you sign in with Firebase Authentication in a SwiftUI mobile app using the Firebase SDK, Firebase automatically stores the ID token for you in the app's keychain or local storage, and you do not need to store it yourself.</p><p>The ID token is automatically refreshed by Firebase SDK when it's near expiration, so you don't need to manually refresh it. However, you can manually refresh it by calling the <code>getToken</code> method of the Firebase Auth object.</p><p>If you want to persist the user's authentication state between app launches or even device restarts, you can use Firebase's <code>auth().currentUser?.getIDToken(completion:)</code> method to get the current user's ID token and store it securely in your app's keychain or local storage. You can then use this token to authenticate the user in subsequent requests to your app's backend or to other Firebase services.</p><p>When storing the ID token, make sure to securely store it using appropriate encryption or secure storage techniques to prevent unauthorized access to the user's authentication state. It's also important to note that ID tokens expire after a certain period of time, typically one hour, so you should implement appropriate mechanisms to refresh the token periodically or whenever it's near expiration.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Wait but if firebase stores the ID token why do I need to store it for it to presisting'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Firebase stores the ID token for you and automatically refreshes it when it's near expiration. However, the token is only stored in memory by default, so it will be lost when the app is closed or the device is restarted.</p><p>If you want to persist the user's authentication state across app launches or device restarts, you need to store the ID token securely in your app's keychain or local storage. This way, you can retrieve it later and use it to authenticate the user without them having to sign in again.</p><p>Storing the ID token securely in your app's keychain or local storage ensures that it's protected from unauthorized access and helps prevent token theft or misuse. By securely storing the token, you can ensure that the user's authentication state is preserved even if they close the app or restart their device.</p><p>So, while Firebase stores the ID token for you, you still need to store it securely in your app's keychain or local storage if you want to persist the user's authentication state.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "but ID token refreshes so I don't think I should be storing the ID token"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>You are correct that the ID token is refreshed automatically by Firebase when it's near expiration, so you do not need to manually refresh it. However, if you want to persist the user's authentication state across app launches or device restarts, you still need to store the ID token securely in your app's keychain or local storage.</p><p>The reason for this is that the ID token is only stored in memory by default, so it will be lost when the app is closed or the device is restarted. If you do not store the token, the user will need to sign in again every time they launch the app, which may not be a great user experience.</p><p>By storing the ID token securely in your app's keychain or local storage, you can retrieve it later and use it to authenticate the user without them having to sign in again. The token is automatically refreshed by Firebase when it's near expiration, so you don't need to worry about manually refreshing it.</p><p>It's important to store the ID token securely using appropriate encryption or secure storage techniques to prevent unauthorized access to the user's authentication state. Additionally, the ID token has an expiration time, typically one hour, so you should implement appropriate mechanisms to refresh the token periodically or whenever it's near expiration.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'addStateDidChangeListener does this function work when app is closed'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>The <code>addStateDidChangeListener</code> function in Firebase Authentication is used to monitor the authentication state of the user and trigger a callback function when the authentication state changes. This function will not continue running when the app is closed or the user logs out.</p><p>When the app is closed, the Firebase Authentication state listener will no longer be active. If you need to monitor the authentication state of the user when the app is not running, you can consider using Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM) to receive push notifications that can trigger actions in your app.</p><p>However, when the user opens the app again, the <code>addStateDidChangeListener</code> function will be triggered again, and you can check the current authentication state of the user and perform any necessary actions based on their authentication status. For example, you can redirect the user to a specific screen or update the UI to show that the user is now signed in.</p><p>It's important to note that the <code>addStateDidChangeListener</code> function should be used sparingly, as it can impact app performance if not used carefully. You should consider using it only when necessary, and make sure to remove the listener when it's no longer needed to avoid unnecessary resource usage.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'so the firebase auth will still keep a user logged in even if the app is closed'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>When a user signs in with Firebase Authentication in your app, Firebase automatically stores the user's authentication state on the device, and this state is persisted across app launches and device restarts.</p><p>This means that even if the user closes the app or restarts their device, they will remain signed in to your app and their authentication state will be automatically restored the next time they launch your app.</p><p>However, it's important to note that the user's authentication state is tied to the specific device and app instance they used to sign in. If the user signs in to your app on another device or app instance, they will need to sign in again and their authentication state will not be automatically restored on the original device.</p><p>Additionally, the user's authentication state can be invalidated if they sign out of your app or their authentication token expires. In these cases, you may need to redirect the user to the sign-in screen or take other appropriate actions to ensure that their authentication state is up-to-date.</p><p>Overall, Firebase Authentication is designed to make it easy to manage the authentication state of your users, even across app launches and device restarts. However, it's important to be aware of the limitations and ensure that your app handles authentication state changes appropriately to provide a good user experience.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'How to get the ID token of a logged in user'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To get the ID token of a logged-in user in Firebase Authentication, you can use the <code>currentUser</code> property of the <code>Auth</code> object, which represents the currently authenticated user.</p><p>Here's an example of how to get the ID token of the current user:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>swift</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-swift"><span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> FirebaseAuth

<span class="hljs-comment">// Get the current user</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> user <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> <span class="hljs-type">Auth</span>.auth().currentUser {

    <span class="hljs-comment">// Get the user's ID token</span>
    user.getIDToken { (token, error) <span class="hljs-keyword">in</span>
        <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> <span class="hljs-keyword">let</span> error <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> error {
            <span class="hljs-comment">// Handle error</span>
            <span class="hljs-built_in">print</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"Error getting ID token: <span class="hljs-subst">\(error.localizedDescription)</span>"</span>)
        } <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
            <span class="hljs-comment">// Use the ID token</span>
            <span class="hljs-built_in">print</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"ID token: <span class="hljs-subst">\(token <span class="hljs-operator">??</span> <span class="hljs-string">""</span>)</span>"</span>)
        }
    }
} <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// User is not signed in</span>
    <span class="hljs-built_in">print</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"User is not signed in."</span>)
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we first check if there is a currently authenticated user by accessing the <code>currentUser</code> property of the <code>Auth</code> object. If there is a user, we use the <code>getIDToken</code> function of the user object to retrieve the user's ID token. The ID token is returned asynchronously in the completion handler, along with any error that may have occurred.</p><p>Note that the ID token is a JWT token that expires after a certain period of time (usually 1 hour), so you may need to refresh the token periodically if you need to use it for longer periods of time. Additionally, it's important to handle any errors that may occur when retrieving the ID token, such as network errors or invalid authentication state.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Hide google sheet columns for certain users only using Google Apps script'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>To hide certain columns in a Google Sheet for certain users only using Google Apps Script, you can use the <code>onOpen()</code> function to check if the current user is authorized to view those columns. Here's an example code that shows how to hide columns B and C for users who are not authorized:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">onOpen</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> sheet = <span class="hljs-title class_">SpreadsheetApp</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">getActiveSpreadsheet</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">getActiveSheet</span>();
  <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> user = <span class="hljs-title class_">Session</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">getActiveUser</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">getEmail</span>();

  <span class="hljs-comment">// Enter the email addresses of authorized users here</span>
  <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> authorizedUsers = [<span class="hljs-string">"user1@example.com"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"user2@example.com"</span>];

  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (authorizedUsers.<span class="hljs-title function_">indexOf</span>(user) == -<span class="hljs-number">1</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// If the user is not authorized, hide columns B and C</span>
    sheet.<span class="hljs-title function_">hideColumns</span>(<span class="hljs-number">2</span>, <span class="hljs-number">2</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// hide columns B and C</span>
  }
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this code, the <code>onOpen()</code> function is triggered every time the user opens the Google Sheet. It first gets the active sheet and the email address of the current user. It then checks if the email address of the current user is in the <code>authorizedUsers</code> array. If the user is not authorized, it hides columns B and C using the <code>hideColumns()</code> method.</p><p>You can customize this code by changing the email addresses in the <code>authorizedUsers</code> array and the column numbers in the <code>hideColumns()</code> method to match your specific needs.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Will other users be able to unhide the columns if they have edit access?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Yes, if other users have edit access to the Google Sheet, they will be able to unhide the columns that were previously hidden using the script.</p><p>To prevent this from happening, you can modify the script to hide the columns again whenever the sheet is edited or when the user switches to another sheet. Here's an example code that demonstrates how to do this:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">javascript</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">function</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">onOpen</span>(<span class="hljs-params"></span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> sheet = <span class="hljs-title class_">SpreadsheetApp</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">getActiveSpreadsheet</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">getActiveSheet</span>();
  <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> user = <span class="hljs-title class_">Session</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">getActiveUser</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">getEmail</span>();

  <span class="hljs-comment">// Enter the email addresses of authorized users here</span>
  <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> authorizedUsers = [<span class="hljs-string">"user1@example.com"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"user2@example.com"</span>];

  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (authorizedUsers.<span class="hljs-title function_">indexOf</span>(user) == -<span class="hljs-number">1</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// If the user is not authorized, hide columns B and C</span>
    sheet.<span class="hljs-title function_">hideColumns</span>(<span class="hljs-number">2</span>, <span class="hljs-number">2</span>); <span class="hljs-comment">// hide columns B and C</span>

    <span class="hljs-comment">// Add a trigger to re-hide the columns whenever the sheet is edited or the user switches to another sheet</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> triggers = <span class="hljs-title class_">ScriptApp</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">getProjectTriggers</span>();
    <span class="hljs-keyword">for</span> (<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> i = <span class="hljs-number">0</span>; i &lt; triggers.<span class="hljs-property">length</span>; i++) {
      <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (triggers[i].<span class="hljs-title function_">getEventType</span>() == <span class="hljs-title class_">ScriptApp</span>.<span class="hljs-property">EventType</span>.<span class="hljs-property">ON_EDIT</span> || triggers[i].<span class="hljs-title function_">getEventType</span>() == <span class="hljs-title class_">ScriptApp</span>.<span class="hljs-property">EventType</span>.<span class="hljs-property">ON_CHANGE</span>) {
        <span class="hljs-title class_">ScriptApp</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">deleteTrigger</span>(triggers[i]);
      }
    }
    <span class="hljs-title class_">ScriptApp</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">newTrigger</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"onOpen"</span>).<span class="hljs-title function_">forSpreadsheet</span>(sheet.<span class="hljs-title function_">getParent</span>()).<span class="hljs-title function_">onEdit</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">onChange</span>().<span class="hljs-title function_">create</span>();
  }
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this modified code, we first hide the columns B and C for users who are not authorized, just like in the previous code. We then add a trigger that will re-hide the columns whenever the sheet is edited or the user switches to another sheet.</p><p>The trigger is added using the <code>ScriptApp.newTrigger()</code> method, and it specifies that the <code>onOpen()</code> function should be called whenever the spreadsheet is edited or changed. We also delete any existing triggers of these types to avoid creating duplicate triggers.</p><p>With this modification, the columns will be automatically hidden again whenever the sheet is edited or the user switches to another sheet, ensuring that only authorized users can view them.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'explain rop gadgets with examples for windows platform  '},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>A ROP (Return-Oriented Programming) gadget is a small piece of code typically found in the loaded libraries or executable modules of a program that ends with a return instruction. ROP gadgets are useful for attackers because they allow them to execute arbitrary code in the context of a program by chaining together multiple gadgets that together perform a desired action.</p><p>An example of a ROP gadget for the Windows platform might be the following assembly code, which is found in the <code>kernel32.dll</code> library:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-perl"><span class="hljs-keyword">pop</span> eax
<span class="hljs-keyword">pop</span> ebx
<span class="hljs-keyword">pop</span> ecx
ret
</code></div></div></pre><p>This gadget performs the following actions:</p><ul><li>The instruction <code>pop eax</code> pops the value at the top of the stack into the EAX register</li><li>The instruction <code>pop ebx</code> pops the value at the top of the stack into the EBX register</li><li>The instruction <code>pop ecx</code> pops the value at the top of the stack into the ECX register</li><li>The instruction <code>ret</code> returns control to the caller</li></ul><p>An attacker could use this gadget, along with others, to execute a payload such as injecting and running malicious code.</p><p>Note that ROP is usually used to exploit a vulnerability called buffer overflow, it is not a vulnerability in itself. Also many modern mitigation techniques like DEP and ASLR making it harder to use ROP in the wild.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'give examples of ASLR bypass on 32-bit system linux'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>ASLR (Address Space Layout Randomization) is a security feature that randomizes the memory locations of various components of a program, such as the stack, heap, and libraries, in order to make it more difficult for an attacker to predict memory addresses and execute successful attacks. On a 32-bit system, the virtual address space is limited to 4GB, which can make ASLR less effective.</p><p>There are several techniques that can be used to bypass ASLR on a 32-bit Linux system:</p><ol><li><p>Brute-forcing: An attacker can try to guess the randomized memory addresses by repeatedly executing the same attack and adjusting their payload accordingly. This technique is effective only if the attacker has access to the targeted system, and ASLR randomization is not that strong.</p></li><li><p>Information leaks: Some programs may contain bugs or other vulnerabilities that can be exploited to leak information about memory addresses, such as stack or heap addresses. With this information, an attacker can use ROP(Return-Oriented Programming) to chain together useful gadgets with known addresses.</p></li><li><p>Timing side-channel attacks: An attacker can use timing differences in the execution of different parts of a program to infer information about memory addresses. For example, if an attacker can observe how long it takes for a program to read from different memory locations, they can infer which memory locations are used by the program.</p></li><li><p>Using known DLL or library addresses: Some system-wide libraries are usually loaded at a fixed address across different systems. In a 32-bit system, with a limited address space, an attacker can use those known addresses to ROP chain.</p></li></ol><p>It is important to note that these methods are not foolproof, and their effectiveness depends on the specific implementation and configuration of the targeted system's ASLR implementation. Also, ASLR can be made more robust by using other mitigation techniques such as PIE(Position Independent Executables) which makes harder to use a fixed address.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'write educational CTF challenge in which allows ASLR bypass using Timing side-channel attacks for which code is written in C program where entire challenge is accessible over port 1337 for player to send input given for simple calculator program '},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Here's an example of a CTF challenge that involves using a timing side-channel attack to bypass ASLR and exploit a simple calculator program that is accessible over port 1337:</p><p>The challenge:</p><p>You have been given a simple calculator program that is accessible over a network connection on port 1337. The program allows the user to enter a mathematical expression and returns the result of the calculation. However, the program contains a vulnerability that allows an attacker to gain control of the program's execution flow. Your task is to exploit this vulnerability to gain control of the program's execution flow and execute arbitrary code.</p><p>The program:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-c"><span class="hljs-meta">#<span class="hljs-keyword">include</span> <span class="hljs-string">&lt;stdio.h&gt;</span></span>
