    <span class="hljs-type">Resource</span> <span class="hljs-variable">resource</span> <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> resourceLoader.getResource(<span class="hljs-string">"classpath:certificates/"</span> + fileName);
    <span class="hljs-type">InputStream</span> <span class="hljs-variable">inputStream</span> <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> resource.getInputStream();
    <span class="hljs-type">CertificateFactory</span> <span class="hljs-variable">certFactory</span> <span class="hljs-operator">=</span> CertificateFactory.getInstance(<span class="hljs-string">"X.509"</span>);
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> (X509Certificate) certFactory.generateCertificate(inputStream);
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>This code loads a certificate from the file system using the <code>ResourceLoader</code> and <code>CertificateFactory</code> classes. You can then use the returned <code>X509Certificate</code> object in your application.</p><ol><li>Keystore: You can store the certificates in a keystore file and load them using Spring's <code>KeyStoreFactoryBean</code>. For example, you can create a <code>keystore.p12</code> file and store your certificates in it. You can then load the certificates using the following code:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>java</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-java"><span class="hljs-meta">@Autowired</span>
