</code></div></div></pre><p>Paste the conversation text into the <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> element, save the file with an .html extension, and then share it. The recipient can open the file in Safari or any other web browser to view the conversation.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'navigator.clipboard.writeText not work in http domain'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="request-:R2d6:-0 markdown prose dark:prose-invert break-words light"><p>The <code>navigator.clipboard</code> API is only supported in secure contexts, such as HTTPS or localhost. This means that it cannot be used in regular HTTP domains. This is a security measure to prevent malicious websites from accessing sensitive data in the clipboard without the user's knowledge or permission.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'other alternative'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="request-:R2d6:-1 markdown prose dark:prose-invert break-words light"><p>If you need to copy text to the clipboard in an HTTP domain, you can use a different method. One approach is to use a hidden textarea element in your HTML. You can populate the textarea with the text you want to copy, and then use JavaScript to select and copy the text from the textarea. Here is an example of how you might do this:</p><pre><div class="bg-black"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="w-4 h-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><polyline points="20 6 9 17 4 12"></polyline></svg>Copied!</button></div><div class="p-4"><code class="!whitespace-pre-wrap hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-comment">// create a hidden textarea element</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> textarea = <span class="hljs-variable language_">document</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">createElement</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"textarea"</span>);
textarea.<span class="hljs-property">style</span>.<span class="hljs-property">position</span> = <span class="hljs-string">"absolute"</span>;
textarea.<span class="hljs-property">style</span>.<span class="hljs-property">left</span> = <span class="hljs-string">"-9999px"</span>;

<span class="hljs-comment">// add the textarea to the document</span>
<span class="hljs-variable language_">document</span>.<span class="hljs-property">body</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">appendChild</span>(textarea);

<span class="hljs-comment">// copy the text to the textarea</span>
textarea.<span class="hljs-property">value</span> = <span class="hljs-string">"This is the text to be copied"</span>;

<span class="hljs-comment">// select the text in the textarea</span>
textarea.<span class="hljs-title function_">select</span>();

<span class="hljs-comment">// copy the selected text to the clipboard</span>
<span class="hljs-variable language_">document</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">execCommand</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"copy"</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// remove the textarea from the document</span>
<span class="hljs-variable language_">document</span>.<span class="hljs-property">body</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">removeChild</span>(textarea);
</code></div></div></pre><p>This method should work in most modern browsers, although it is not supported by all browsers. You may need to check for compatibility and use a different method for older browsers.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Write some code to reverse a string.'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Sure, here's an example code snippet in Python to reverse a string:</p><pre><div><div><span>python</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewbox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-python"><span class="hljs-comment"># Take input string from user</span>
input_string = <span class="hljs-built_in">input</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"Enter a string: "</span>)

<span class="hljs-comment"># Reverse the string using slicing</span>
reversed_string = input_string[::-<span class="hljs-number">1</span>]

<span class="hljs-comment"># Print the reversed string</span>
<span class="hljs-built_in">print</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"Reversed string: "</span>, reversed_string)
</code></div></div></pre><p>This code takes a string input from the user, and then reverses it using string slicing. Finally, the reversed string is printed to the console.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "The 'sanitize-html' library seems to be removing classes from my html - why?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>The <code>sanitize-html</code> library is designed to remove potentially dangerous or malicious code from HTML strings to prevent XSS (Cross-site Scripting) attacks. One of the ways it does this is by removing classes from HTML elements, as classes can be used to execute scripts or perform other malicious actions.</p><p>By default, <code>sanitize-html</code> removes all attributes from HTML elements except for a small whitelist of allowed attributes. If you want to allow certain classes in your HTML, you can pass a <code>allowedClasses</code> option to the <code>sanitizeHtml</code> function:</p><pre><div><div><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewbox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript"><span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> sanitizeHtml = <span class="hljs-built_in">require</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'sanitize-html'</span>);

<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> dirtyHtml = <span class="hljs-string">'&lt;div class="foo bar"&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/div&gt;'</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> cleanHtml = <span class="hljs-title function_">sanitizeHtml</span>(dirtyHtml, {
  <span class="hljs-attr">allowedClasses</span>: {
    <span class="hljs-string">'div'</span>: [<span class="hljs-string">'foo'</span>, <span class="hljs-string">'bar'</span>]
  }
});

<span class="hljs-variable language_">console</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">log</span>(cleanHtml);
<span class="hljs-comment">// Output: '&lt;div class="foo bar"&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/div&gt;'</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we're allowing the classes "foo" and "bar" on <code>div</code> elements. Any other classes will be removed by <code>sanitize-html</code>. Note that you can also allow classes on other HTML elements by adding them to the <code>allowedClasses</code> object.</p><p>If you're still experiencing issues with classes being removed, you may want to double-check your options and make sure that you're using the latest version of the <code>sanitize-html</code> library.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Implement the oauth client with Identity Server'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To implement an OAuth client with Identity Server, you will need to follow these steps:</p><ol><li><p>Register your application with Identity Server: You will need to create an application registration in Identity Server to obtain a client ID and client secret. These values will be used to identify your application and authenticate requests made to Identity Server.</p></li><li><p>Configure your application: You will need to configure your application to use the OAuth flow provided by Identity Server. This will typically involve setting up a redirect URI that Identity Server will use to send the user back to your application after they have authenticated.</p></li><li><p>Authenticate the user: Once you have configured your application, you can direct the user to the Identity Server login page, where they will be prompted to enter their credentials. Once they have successfully authenticated, Identity Server will redirect the user back to your application.</p></li><li><p>Retrieve an access token: After the user has been redirected back to your application, you can exchange the authorization code provided by Identity Server for an access token. This access token can then be used to make requests to protected resources on behalf of the user.</p></li></ol><p>Here is an example implementation of an OAuth client with Identity Server in C#:</p><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>csharp</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-csharp"><span class="hljs-comment">// Create a new Identity Server client using the client ID and client secret obtained during registration</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> client = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> HttpClient();
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> tokenEndpoint = <span class="hljs-string">"https://identityserver.example.com/connect/token"</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> clientId = <span class="hljs-string">"your_client_id"</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> clientSecret = <span class="hljs-string">"your_client_secret"</span>;
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> tokenRequest = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> Dictionary&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>, <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>&gt;
{
    { <span class="hljs-string">"grant_type"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"authorization_code"</span> },
    { <span class="hljs-string">"code"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"authorization_code_from_identity_server"</span> },
    { <span class="hljs-string">"redirect_uri"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"https://your_application.com/redirect_uri"</span> },
    { <span class="hljs-string">"client_id"</span>, clientId },
    { <span class="hljs-string">"client_secret"</span>, clientSecret }
};

<span class="hljs-comment">// Exchange the authorization code for an access token</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> response = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> client.PostAsync(tokenEndpoint, <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> FormUrlEncodedContent(tokenRequest));
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> tokenResponse = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> response.Content.ReadAsAsync&lt;Dictionary&lt;<span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>, <span class="hljs-built_in">string</span>&gt;&gt;();
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> accessToken = tokenResponse[<span class="hljs-string">"access_token"</span>];

<span class="hljs-comment">// Use the access token to make requests to protected resources on behalf of the user</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> apiEndpoint = <span class="hljs-string">"https://api.example.com/protected_resource"</span>;
client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = <span class="hljs-keyword">new</span> AuthenticationHeaderValue(<span class="hljs-string">"Bearer"</span>, accessToken);
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> apiResponse = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> client.GetAsync(apiEndpoint);
<span class="hljs-keyword">var</span> content = <span class="hljs-keyword">await</span> apiResponse.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
</code></div></div></pre><p>This example demonstrates how to obtain an access token from Identity Server using an authorization code and use it to make requests to a protected resource. However, there are many variations of the OAuth flow and the exact implementation will depend on the specifics of your use case.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'How can I send a notification message to Slack from Go lang app that is running in GCP?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>You can send a notification message to Slack from a Go lang app running in GCP by using the Slack API. Here are the steps:</p><ol><li>Create a new Slack app and get the API token for your Slack workspace.</li><li>Write a Go program that sends an HTTP request to the Slack API. The request should contain a JSON payload with the message you want to send.</li><li>Use the "net/http" package to send the HTTP request to the Slack API endpoint.</li><li>To authenticate the request, you'll need to pass the API token in the request header.</li></ol><p>Here is an example of how the code might look like:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">go</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-go"><span class="hljs-keyword">package</span> main

<span class="hljs-keyword">import</span> (
	<span class="hljs-string">"bytes"</span>
	<span class="hljs-string">"encoding/json"</span>
	<span class="hljs-string">"net/http"</span>
)

<span class="hljs-keyword">type</span> SlackMessage <span class="hljs-keyword">struct</span> {
	Text <span class="hljs-type">string</span> <span class="hljs-string">`json:"text"`</span>
}

<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">func</span> <span class="hljs-title">main</span><span class="hljs-params">()</span></span> {
	url := <span class="hljs-string">"https://slack.com/api/chat.postMessage"</span>
	token := <span class="hljs-string">"your-api-token"</span>
	message := SlackMessage{Text: <span class="hljs-string">"This is a message from Go running in GCP."</span>}

	jsonValue, _ := json.Marshal(message)

	req, _ := http.NewRequest(<span class="hljs-string">"POST"</span>, url, bytes.NewBuffer(jsonValue))
	req.Header.Add(<span class="hljs-string">"Authorization"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"Bearer "</span>+token)
	req.Header.Add(<span class="hljs-string">"Content-Type"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"application/json; charset=utf-8"</span>)

	client := &amp;http.Client{}
	resp, _ := client.Do(req)

	<span class="hljs-keyword">defer</span> resp.Body.Close()
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>This is a basic example and you can customize it as per your requirements.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Can I store the token in a secure way?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Yes, storing the Slack API token in a secure way is important to prevent unauthorized access to your Slack workspace. Here are a few methods to securely store the token:</p><ol><li><p>Environment Variables: You can store the token as an environment variable in the server where your Go app is running. This way, the token is not stored in the source code and is easily configurable.</p></li><li><p>Secrets Manager: If you are running your Go app on a cloud platform like Google Cloud Platform (GCP), you can use a service like GCP's Secrets Manager to securely store the token. This service allows you to store sensitive information, such as API keys and passwords, and securely access them in your applications.</p></li><li><p>Configuration File: You can also store the token in a configuration file that is not checked into version control. This way, the token is not included in the source code and can be easily changed without modifying the code.</p></li></ol><p>No matter which method you choose, make sure to follow security best practices, such as using secure encryption, setting appropriate access controls, and regularly rotating the token.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': "If I'd have the Configuration File, what is the best way to share it within a development team in a secure way?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Sharing a configuration file that contains sensitive information such as an API token within a development team can be challenging, especially if you want to ensure that the information remains secure. Here are a few ways to securely share the configuration file within your team:</p><ol><li><p>Encrypted Configuration File: You can encrypt the configuration file using a tool like GPG or PGP and share the encrypted file with your team. The file can be decrypted using a private key that is only known to the members of the team.</p></li><li><p>Secrets Management Tool: You can use a secrets management tool such as HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or Google Cloud Key Management Service to securely store the configuration file and share it within your team. These tools provide secure storage, encryption, and access controls for secrets and configuration data.</p></li><li><p>Network File Shares: You can store the configuration file on a network file share that is only accessible to the members of your team. You can also set permissions to ensure that only the necessary people have access to the file.</p></li></ol><p>Regardless of the method you choose, it's important to regularly review and update the access controls to ensure that only the right people have access to the configuration file. Additionally, make sure to regularly rotate the API token to minimize the risk of unauthorized access.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'what are vulnerabilities in the following code? from django.db.utils import IntegrityError
from rest_framework import permissions, viewsets, generics, status
from rest_framework.response import Response
from rest_framework.exceptions import ValidationError
from .models import CertificationRequest, Status, Competence
from .serializers import CertificationRequestSerializer
from .permissions import IsVolunteer
# Create your views here.


class CertificationRequestViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet):
    """Viewset for certification requests"""
    serializer_class = CertificationRequestSerializer  # use the serializer class defined in serializers.py
    permission_classes = [permissions.IsAdminUser | IsVolunteer]

    http_method_names = ['get', 'head', 'delete', 'post']

    def get_queryset(self):
        """Only show certification requests of the current user if not admin"""
        if self.request.user.is_staff:
            return CertificationRequest.objects.all()
        return CertificationRequest.objects.filter(user=self.request.user)

    def perform_create(self, serializer):
        """Create a certification request for the current user if not admin"""
        try:
            if self.request.user.is_staff:
                raise ValidationError(
                    "Admins can't create certification requests")
            # save the certification request with the current user
            serializer.save(user=self.request.user)
        except IntegrityError as e:
            raise ValidationError(
                'An identical Certification Request already exists')


class AnswerCertificationRequest(generics.GenericAPIView):
    """View to answer certification requests"""
    permission_classes = [permissions.IsAuthenticated]

    def post(self, request):

        # check if id and status is provided
        if not(request.data.get('id') and request.data.get('status')):
            return Response({'error': 'id and status is required'}, status=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST)

        certification_request = CertificationRequest.objects.get(
            id=request.data['id'])

        if certification_request is None:  # check if certification request exists
            return Response({'error': 'Certification Request does not exist'}, status=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST)

        if certification_request.status != 'P':  # check if certification request is pending
            return Response({'error': 'Certification Request is not pending'}, status=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST)

        state = request.data['status']

        if state == 'A':  # if accepted
            certification_request.status = Status.ACCEPTED
        elif state == 'D':  # if declined
            certification_request.status = Status.DECLINED
        else:
            return Response({'error': 'Status must be A or D'}, status=status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST)
        certification_request.save()
