headers = {<span class="hljs-string">"Authorization"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"Bearer your-access-token"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"Content-Type"</span>: <span class="hljs-string">"application/json"</span>}
data = {<span class="hljs-string">"request_id"</span>: encoded_malicious_object}

response = requests.post(url, headers=headers, json=data)
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>Analyze the response: If the application is vulnerable to insecure deserialization, it might execute the malicious code and return an unexpected response. In this case, you might see the output "Malicious code executed" in the server logs or application logs.</li></ol><p>Remember that attempting to exploit a vulnerability on a production system without permission is illegal and unethical. Always perform security tests on your own applications or on applications where you have explicit permission to do so.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'what is the exact OWASP top 10 id of the vulnerability Remote code execution - Insecure deserialization?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>The OWASP Top 10 2021 list does not have a specific category for "Remote Code Execution - Insecure Deserialization." However, it can be classified under the category "A10:2021 - Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)." This category covers various types of vulnerabilities that allow attackers to induce the server-side application to perform unintended actions, which could include remote code execution due to insecure deserialization.</p><p>In previous versions of the OWASP Top 10, such as the 2017 edition, insecure deserialization was listed as a separate category called "A8:2017 - Insecure Deserialization." It is important to note that the OWASP Top 10 list evolves over time, and the categorization may change in future iterations.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'what is the content of WSTG-INPV-11 in the OWASP top 10?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>The OWASP Web Security Testing Guide (WSTG) is not part of the OWASP Top 10. They are two separate projects under the OWASP umbrella. The OWASP Top 10 is a list of the most critical web application security risks, while the WSTG provides a detailed guide for web application security testing.</p><p>WSTG-INPV-11 refers to the "Testing for Local File Inclusion" test scenario in the OWASP Web Security Testing Guide. Local File Inclusion (LFI) vulnerabilities occur when an application uses untrusted user input to construct a file path for local resources. An attacker can exploit LFI vulnerabilities to read sensitive files on the server, execute arbitrary code, or even gain control over the system.</p><p>To test for LFI vulnerabilities, security testers typically:</p><ol><li>Identify points in the application where user input is used to reference local files.</li><li>Attempt to manipulate the input to read unintended local files or directory listings.</li><li>Try various encoding and path traversal techniques to bypass input validation and access sensitive files.</li></ol><p>It is essential to address LFI vulnerabilities to protect the confidentiality, integrity, and availability of the web application and its underlying systems.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Do you have examples of CVE related to Remote code execution - Insecure deserialization?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Yes, here are a few examples of CVEs related to Remote Code Execution (RCE) due to Insecure Deserialization:</p><ol><li><p>CVE-2015-4852: This vulnerability affected the Oracle WebLogic Server, where the Apache Commons Collections library was susceptible to unsafe deserialization of Java objects. Attackers could exploit this vulnerability to execute arbitrary Java code remotely.</p></li><li><p>CVE-2017-9805: Apache Struts, a popular Java web application framework, had a deserialization vulnerability in its REST plugin. By sending a specially crafted XML payload to an endpoint using the REST plugin, an attacker could execute arbitrary code on the affected server.</p></li><li><p>CVE-2019-2725: Another vulnerability in Oracle WebLogic Server allowed attackers to execute arbitrary code remotely due to unsafe deserialization. This was possible because the server did not restrict the classes available for deserialization, enabling attackers to send a malicious payload to trigger the RCE.</p></li><li><p>CVE-2018-7600: Also known as Drupalgeddon2, this vulnerability affected the popular Drupal content management system. The vulnerability was caused by insufficient input validation and insecure deserialization, allowing attackers to exploit it for remote code execution.</p></li></ol><p>These examples show that insecure deserialization vulnerabilities can impact various types of software, including web servers, web application frameworks, and content management systems. It is essential to be aware of these risks and apply security best practices, such as input validation, secure coding, and keeping software up to date with the latest security patches.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'esp8266 http cors'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert dark"><p>The ESP8266 is a popular Wi-Fi module that can be used to add wireless connectivity to a wide range of projects. When building web applications that run on the ESP8266, it's important to understand how to handle Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) requests.</p><p>CORS is a security feature implemented by web browsers to prevent web pages from making requests to resources hosted on a different domain than the one serving the web page. This is a security feature to prevent malicious scripts from accessing sensitive data.</p><p>To enable CORS on the ESP8266, you can add the following code to the beginning of your sketch:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">csharp</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-csharp"><span class="hljs-meta">#include &lt;ESP8266WebServer.h&gt;</span>

<span class="hljs-function">ESP8266WebServer <span class="hljs-title">server</span>(<span class="hljs-params"><span class="hljs-number">80</span></span>)</span>;

<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">void</span> <span class="hljs-title">handleCORS</span>()</span> {
  server.sendHeader(<span class="hljs-string">"Access-Control-Allow-Origin"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"*"</span>);
  server.sendHeader(<span class="hljs-string">"Access-Control-Max-Age"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"10000"</span>);
  server.sendHeader(<span class="hljs-string">"Access-Control-Allow-Methods"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"POST, GET, OPTIONS"</span>);
  server.sendHeader(<span class="hljs-string">"Access-Control-Allow-Headers"</span>, <span class="hljs-string">"*"</span>);
  server.send(<span class="hljs-number">204</span>);
}

<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">void</span> <span class="hljs-title">setup</span>()</span> {
  server.<span class="hljs-keyword">on</span>(<span class="hljs-string">"/"</span>, handleCORS);
  server.begin();
}

<span class="hljs-function"><span class="hljs-keyword">void</span> <span class="hljs-title">loop</span>()</span> {
  server.handleClient();
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this code, the <code>handleCORS</code> function is called when a request is made to the root endpoint ("/"). The <code>handleCORS</code> function sends the necessary headers to enable CORS. Specifically, it sets the "Access-Control-Allow-Origin" header to "*", which allows requests from any origin. It also sets the "Access-Control-Allow-Methods" header to "POST, GET, OPTIONS" to allow those HTTP methods.</p><p>Note that handling CORS is not specific to the ESP8266, but is a general concept for web development. It's important to understand and properly implement CORS to ensure secure and reliable web applications.</p></div>'}]

