</code></div></div></pre><p>This updated code sets the <code>_MRT</code> cookie header for the refresh token and the <code>Authorization</code> header for the access token. It uses the <code>flutter_secure_storage</code> package to securely store and retrieve the tokens, and sets up an HTTP interceptor to add the headers to outgoing requests. Note that this assumes that the server returns the refresh token as a cookie in the <code>Set-Cookie</code> header, and that the access token is returned in the <code>Authorization</code> header in the server response.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': "I'd like to build an API that interfaces with a piece of third party software so that I can build a new interface using that API rather than rely on the provided API of the third party software. Is that something you could help me build?"},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>As an AI language model, I can offer guidance and suggestions on how to build an API that interfaces with third-party software. However, I cannot directly help you build the API or write the code for you.</p><p>To get started, you will need to understand the documentation of the third-party software, identify the data or functionality that you want to access through the API, and plan out the architecture and design of your API.</p><p>Here are some general steps to consider when building an API:</p><ol><li><p>Define the purpose and scope of your API: Identify the data or functionality you want to expose to your new interface, and decide on the resources and endpoints you will need.</p></li><li><p>Choose a programming language and framework: Select a programming language and framework that are suitable for your needs and compatible with the third-party software.</p></li><li><p>Define the API endpoints: Define the endpoints for your API, including the HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.) and the URLs that will be used to access them.</p></li><li><p>Define the request and response format: Define the format for the requests and responses to be used in your API. This may involve choosing a data format, such as JSON or XML, and defining the structure of the data.</p></li><li><p>Implement the API: Implement the API using your chosen programming language and framework. This may involve making API calls to the third-party software and transforming the data into the format you defined.</p></li><li><p>Test the API: Test the API to ensure that it is working correctly, and make any necessary adjustments to the implementation.</p></li><li><p>Document the API: Document the API, including the endpoints, request and response format, and any additional information that may be useful for developers who will be using your API.</p></li></ol><p>I hope this information helps you get started on building your API. Good luck!</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Can you give me an example of what it would look like to build an API request in PHP that creates a new user in a database?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Certainly! Here is an example of how you can create a new user in a database using PHP and an API request:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">php</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-php"><span class="hljs-meta">&lt;?php</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Define the endpoint for the API request</span>
<span class="hljs-variable">$apiEndpoint</span> = <span class="hljs-string">'https://api.example.com/users'</span>;

<span class="hljs-comment">// Define the request headers</span>
<span class="hljs-variable">$requestHeaders</span> = <span class="hljs-keyword">array</span>(
    <span class="hljs-string">'Content-Type: application/json'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'Authorization: Bearer YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN'</span>,
);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Define the data to be sent in the API request</span>
<span class="hljs-variable">$requestData</span> = <span class="hljs-keyword">array</span>(
    <span class="hljs-string">'name'</span> =&gt; <span class="hljs-string">'John Doe'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'email'</span> =&gt; <span class="hljs-string">'johndoe@example.com'</span>,
    <span class="hljs-string">'password'</span> =&gt; <span class="hljs-string">'password123'</span>,
);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Convert the data to JSON format</span>
<span class="hljs-variable">$jsonData</span> = <span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">json_encode</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$requestData</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Create a new cURL session to send the API request</span>
<span class="hljs-variable">$ch</span> = <span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">curl_init</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$apiEndpoint</span>);
<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">curl_setopt</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$ch</span>, CURLOPT_POST, <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>);
<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">curl_setopt</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$ch</span>, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, <span class="hljs-variable">$jsonData</span>);
<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">curl_setopt</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$ch</span>, CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER, <span class="hljs-variable">$requestHeaders</span>);
<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">curl_setopt</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$ch</span>, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, <span class="hljs-literal">true</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Send the API request and store the response</span>
<span class="hljs-variable">$response</span> = <span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">curl_exec</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$ch</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Close the cURL session</span>
<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">curl_close</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$ch</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Check the response for errors</span>
<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (!<span class="hljs-variable">$response</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">echo</span> <span class="hljs-string">'Error: '</span> . <span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">curl_error</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$ch</span>);
} <span class="hljs-keyword">else</span> {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">echo</span> <span class="hljs-string">'User created successfully!'</span>;
}

<span class="hljs-meta">?&gt;</span>
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we define the endpoint for the API request (<code>$apiEndpoint</code>), the request headers (<code>$requestHeaders</code>), and the data to be sent in the API request (<code>$requestData</code>). We then convert the data to JSON format using the <code>json_encode()</code> function.</p><p>Next, we create a new cURL session using <code>curl_init()</code>, set the options for the session using <code>curl_setopt()</code>, and send the API request using <code>curl_exec()</code>. We then store the response in the <code>$response</code> variable and close the cURL session using <code>curl_close()</code>.</p><p>Finally, we check the response for errors and print a success message if the user was created successfully.</p><p>Note that this is just an example and you will need to modify it to suit your specific needs and the requirements of the API you are working with.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'So if I were trying to write code that was receiving this API call via the $apiEndpoint URL what would some example code be to receive the request and write it to a database?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div class="markdown prose w-full break-words dark:prose-invert light"><p>Sure, I can provide an example of how to receive the API request sent to <code>$apiEndpoint</code> and write the received data to a database.</p><p>Here's an example of what the code could look like in PHP:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span class="">php</span><button class="flex ml-auto gap-2"><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><path d="M16 4h2a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v14a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H6a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V6a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h2"></path><rect x="8" y="2" width="8" height="4" rx="1" ry="1"></rect></svg>Copy code</button></div><div class="p-4 overflow-y-auto"><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-php"><span class="hljs-meta">&lt;?php</span>

<span class="hljs-comment">// Read the raw POST data from the API request</span>
<span class="hljs-variable">$rawData</span> = <span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">file_get_contents</span>(<span class="hljs-string">'php://input'</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Decode the raw data from JSON format into a PHP object</span>
<span class="hljs-variable">$data</span> = <span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">json_decode</span>(<span class="hljs-variable">$rawData</span>);

<span class="hljs-comment">// Connect to the database (replace with your own database credentials)</span>
<span class="hljs-variable">$host</span> = <span class="hljs-string">'localhost'</span>;
<span class="hljs-variable">$username</span> = <span class="hljs-string">'your-username'</span>;
