</code></div></div></pre><p>In addition to the two patterns that detect fileless malware execution traces, this rule now includes a third pattern (<code>$pattern3</code>) that detects the use of PowerShell functions that are commonly used to execute code in memory:</p><ul><li><code>$pattern3</code>: This is a regular expression that matches the following PowerShell functions:<ul><li><code>Invoke-Expression</code>: This function is used to execute arbitrary PowerShell code that is passed as a string argument.</li><li><code>$ExecutionContext</code>: This variable contains information about the current PowerShell execution environment, which can be used to access and execute in-memory code.</li><li><code>Add-Type</code>: This function is used to add a .NET Framework type definition to the current PowerShell session, which can be used to execute in-memory code.</li><li><code>$MyInvocation</code>: This variable contains information about the current PowerShell script or command, which can be used to access and execute in-memory code.</li><li><code>Register-TypeNameForDeserialization</code>: This function is used to register a .NET Framework type for deserialization, which can be used to execute in-memory code.</li></ul></li></ul><p>The <code>condition</code> section of the rule uses the <code>any of</code> operator to indicate that the rule should match if any of the three patterns are found in the scanned memory region.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'What if the attacker uses IEX shortcut?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Good point! To detect the use of the <code>IEX</code> PowerShell shortcut, which is commonly used by attackers to execute in-memory code, you can modify the third pattern in the YARA rule to include the string "IEX" in addition to the other PowerShell functions:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>ruby</span><svg stroke="current