    FModuleManager::<span class="hljs-built_in">Get</span>().<span class="hljs-built_in">LoadModule</span>(*ModuleName);
}
</code></div></div></pre><p>In this example, we first generate the C++ code using the <code>GenerateCode</code> function, and then save it to disk using the <code>FFileHelper::SaveStringToFile</code> function. We then use the UnrealBuildTool to compile the code into a dynamic library file, and finally load the library as a module using the <code>FModuleManager::Get().LoadModule</code> function.</p><p>Note that this is just an example and may require modification to fit your specific needs. Additionally, generating and compiling code at runtime can be complex and error-prone, so it's important to test and validate your approach thoroughly.</p></div>'},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'and if we wanted to regerate it'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>If you want to regenerate the C++ code and update the compiled library at runtime, you'll need to unload the old module before reloading the new one. Here's an example of how you could modify the code from my previous response to achieve this:</p><pre><div class="bg-black mb-4 rounded-md"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>cpp</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-cpp"><span class="hljs-meta">#<span class="hljs-keyword">include</span> <span class="hljs-string">"CoreMinimal.h"</span></span>
