    csrfToken: {
      name: "your-custom-csrf-token-name",
      options: {
        httpOnly: false,
        sameSite: "none",
        path: "/",
        secure: process.env.NODE_ENV === "production",
      },
    },
  },'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>Changing the name of your cookies can certainly break your login functionality. Here are a few things you can try:</p><ol><li><p>Check your server-side code to ensure that it's also using the new cookie names. For example, if you're using a session store like MongoDB or Redis, you'll need to update the cookie names in your server-side code as well.</p></li><li><p>Check your client-side code to ensure that it's also using the new cookie names. Make sure that any requests you make to the server include the new cookie names, and that any cookies you set on the client-side use the new names.</p></li><li><p>Check your browser's cookie storage to ensure that the old cookies have been removed and the new cookies have been set. You can do this by inspecting the cookies in your browser's developer console.</p></li><li><p>Make sure that your server is returning the new cookies with the correct name, domain, path, and other attributes. You can use a tool like Postman or cURL to inspect the cookies returned by your server.</p></li></ol><p>If none of these steps help, you may want to revert the changes you made and start over with a fresh configuration. When changing cookie names, it's important to make sure that all components of your application are updated to use the new names.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'My session and CSRF token data are saved in a script referenced by my popup.html file. But my request is being sent from a content script. How can my content script get data from popup.js, referenced in popup.html'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>To share data between a content script and a popup script, you can use the <code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage</code> method. Here's an example of how you can use this method:</p><ol><li>In your <code>popup.js</code> script, you can use the <code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage</code> method to send a message to the background script with the session and CSRF token data:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>php</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-php">chrome.runtime.<span class="hljs-title function_ invoke__">sendMessage</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">sessionToken</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'your-session-token'</span>, <span class="hljs-attr">csrfToken</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'your-csrf-token'</span> });
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>In your <code>background.js</code> script, you can listen for this message using the <code>chrome.runtime.onMessage</code> method:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript">chrome.<span class="hljs-property">runtime</span>.<span class="hljs-property">onMessage</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">addListener</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">request, sender, sendResponse</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (request.<span class="hljs-property">sessionToken</span> &amp;&amp; request.<span class="hljs-property">csrfToken</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-comment">// Save the session and CSRF token data in a variable or storage.</span>
  }
});
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>In your content script, you can use the <code>chrome.runtime.sendMessage</code> method to request the session and CSRF token data from the background script:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript">chrome.<span class="hljs-property">runtime</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">sendMessage</span>({ <span class="hljs-attr">action</span>: <span class="hljs-string">'getSessionAndCSRFTokenData'</span> }, <span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">response</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-comment">// Use the session and CSRF token data returned by the background script.</span>
});
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>In your <code>background.js</code> script, you can handle this message and return the session and CSRF token data:</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>javascript</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-javascript">chrome.<span class="hljs-property">runtime</span>.<span class="hljs-property">onMessage</span>.<span class="hljs-title function_">addListener</span>(<span class="hljs-keyword">function</span>(<span class="hljs-params">request, sender, sendResponse</span>) {
  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (request.<span class="hljs-property">action</span> === <span class="hljs-string">'getSessionAndCSRFTokenData'</span>) {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> sessionToken = <span class="hljs-comment">// Get the session token data from storage or a variable.</span>
    <span class="hljs-keyword">const</span> csrfToken = <span class="hljs-comment">// Get the CSRF token data from storage or a variable.</span>
    <span class="hljs-title function_">sendResponse</span>({ sessionToken, csrfToken });
  }
});
</code></div></div></pre><p>This way, your content script can request the session and CSRF token data from the background script, which can get the data from the popup script and return it to the content script.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'I want you to identify security vulnerabilities. I will provide some code consisting of multiple files. Each file is separated by "//File: path/to/file". You will attempt to identify the vulnerabilities.

//File: services/token.js

const getLocalRefreshToken = () =&gt; {
  return localStorage.getItem("refresh_token");
};

const setLocalRefreshToken = (token) =&gt; {
  localStorage.setItem("refresh_token", token);
};

const getLocalAccessToken = () =&gt; {
  return localStorage.getItem("access_token");
};

const updateLocalAccessToken = (token) =&gt; {
  localStorage.setItem("access_token", token);
};

const getUser = () =&gt; {
  return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem("user"));
};

const setUser = (user) =&gt; {
  console.log(JSON.stringify(user));
  localStorage.setItem("user", JSON.stringify(user));
};

const removeUser = () =&gt; {
  localStorage.removeItem("user");
  localStorage.removeItem("access_token");
  localStorage.removeItem("refresh_token");
};

const TokenService = {
  getLocalRefreshToken,
  setLocalRefreshToken,
  getLocalAccessToken,
  updateLocalAccessToken,
  getUser,
  setUser,
  removeUser,
};

export default TokenService;'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': "<div><p>I've identified a few potential security vulnerabilities in the provided code:</p><ol><li><p><strong>Insecure Storage of Sensitive Data</strong>: The code stores sensitive data like access tokens and refresh tokens in the browser's <code>localStorage</code>. This is not recommended, as data in <code>localStorage</code> is accessible by any JavaScript code running on the same domain, which makes it vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. Instead, consider using secure HTTP-only cookies or other more secure storage methods.</p></li><li><p><strong>Lack of Input Validation</strong>: The <code>setUser</code> function stores a user object in <code>localStorage</code> without performing any input validation. If an attacker can manage to tamper with this data or inject malicious content, it could lead to security vulnerabilities or application malfunction. Consider adding input validation to ensure the integrity of the data.</p></li><li><p><strong>Information Leakage</strong>: The <code>setUser</code> function logs the user object to the console using <code>console.log(JSON.stringify(user))</code>. This could potentially leak sensitive user data in production environments. To avoid this, consider removing this line or using a logging mechanism that respects different environments and log levels.</p></li></ol><p>Note that the provided code snippet is only a part of the whole application, so other vulnerabilities might exist elsewhere in the code. Make sure to perform a thorough security assessment of your entire application.</p></div>"},  {'from': 'human', 'value': 'Can you provide sources for that information?'},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>Certainly! Here are some sources that support the information I provided:</p><ol><li><strong>Insecure Storage of Sensitive Data</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li>OWASP's guide on storing JWT securely: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/JSON_Web_Token_for_Java_Cheat_Sheet.html#token-storage-on-client-side" target="_new">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/JSON_Web_Token_for_Java_Cheat_Sheet.html#token-storage-on-client-side</a></li><li>Mozilla's guide on securing client-side storage: <a href="https://infosec.mozilla.org/guidelines/web_security#client-side-storage" target="_new">https://infosec.mozilla.org/guidelines/web_security#client-side-storage</a></li></ul><ol><li><strong>Lack of Input Validation</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li>OWASP's guide on Input Validation: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html" target="_new">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Input_Validation_Cheat_Sheet.html</a></li><li>Mozilla's guide on securing user data: <a href="https://infosec.mozilla.org/guidelines/web_security#securing-user-data" target="_new">https://infosec.mozilla.org/guidelines/web_security#securing-user-data</a></li></ul><ol><li><strong>Information Leakage</strong>:</li></ol><ul><li>OWASP's guide on information leakage: <a href="https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure.html" target="_new">https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/2017/A3_2017-Sensitive_Data_Exposure.html</a></li><li>OWASP's guide on logging: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html" target="_new">https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html</a></li></ul><p>These sources are from reputable organizations like OWASP (Open Web Application Security Project) and Mozilla. They provide comprehensive guides and best practices for securing web applications, including handling sensitive data, input validation, and logging.</p></div>'}]

[ {'from': 'human', 'value': 'CVE description: 
Tensorflow is an Open Source Machine Learning Framework. An attacker can craft a TFLite model that would cause an integer overflow in `TfLiteIntArrayCreate`. The `TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes` returns an `int` instead of a `size_t. An attacker can control model inputs such that `computed_size` overflows the size of `int` datatype. The fix will be included in TensorFlow 2.8.0. We will also cherrypick this commit on TensorFlow 2.7.1, TensorFlow 2.6.3, and TensorFlow 2.5.3, as these are also affected and still in supported range.

   :
TfLiteIntArray* TfLiteIntArrayCreate(int size) {
  int alloc_size = TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes(size);
  if (alloc_size &lt;= 0) return NULL;
  TfLiteIntArray* ret = (TfLiteIntArray*)malloc(alloc_size);
  if (!ret) return ret;
  ret-&gt;size = size;
  return ret;
}
int TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes(int size) {
  static TfLiteIntArray dummy;

  int computed_size = sizeof(dummy) + sizeof(dummy.data[0]) * size;
#if defined(_MSC_VER)
  // Context for why this is needed is in http://b/189926408#comment21
  computed_size -= sizeof(dummy.data[0]);
#endif
  return computed_size;
}

      '},  {'from': 'gpt', 'value': '<div><p>  :
  <code>TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes</code>  .  <code>int</code>  <code>size_t</code>  ,    <code>computed_size</code> <code>int</code>       .</p><p> :
  , <code>TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes</code>  <code>size_t</code>  ,       .</p><ol><li>   <code>int</code> <code>size_t</code> .</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>c</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-c"><span class="hljs-type">size_t</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes</span><span class="hljs-params">(<span class="hljs-type">int</span> size)</span>;
</code></div></div></pre><ol><li>    .   <code>computed_size</code>    size    .</li></ol><pre><div class="bg-black rounded-md mb-4"><div class="flex items-center relative text-gray-200 bg-gray-800 px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans"><span>c</span><svg stroke="currentColor" fill="none" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="h-4 w-4" height="1em" width="1em" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"></svg>Copy code</div><div><code class="!whitespace-pre hljs language-c"><span class="hljs-type">size_t</span> <span class="hljs-title function_">TfLiteIntArrayGetSizeInBytes</span><span class="hljs-params">(<span class="hljs-type">int</span> size)</span> {
  <span class="hljs-type">static</span> TfLiteIntArray dummy;

  <span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> (size &gt; (INT_MAX - <span class="hljs-keyword">sizeof</span>(dummy)) / <span class="hljs-keyword">sizeof</span>(dummy.data[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>])) {
    <span class="hljs-keyword">return</span> <span class="hljs-number">0</span>; <span class="hljs-comment">//  </span>
  }

  <span class="hljs-type">size_t</span> computed_size = <span class="hljs-keyword">sizeof</span>(dummy) + <span class="hljs-keyword">sizeof</span>(dummy.data[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>]) * size;
<span class="hljs-meta">#<span class="hljs-keyword">if</span> defined(_MSC_VER)</span>
  <span class="hljs-comment">// Context for why this is needed is in http://b/189926408#comment21</span>
  computed_size -= <span class="hljs-keyword">sizeof</span>(dummy.data[<span class="hljs-number">0</span>]);
<span class="hljs-meta">#<span class="hljs-keyword">endif</span></span>
